add(
  "computer",
  "software",
  "open_source_software",
  "Open-Source Software",
  `
  <h2>Meaning of open-source software</h2>
  <p>Open-source software is software distributed under a licence that makes its source code available and grants permissions to use, inspect, modify, and redistribute the software, subject to the licence's conditions.</p>

  <p>Examples include the Linux kernel, Mozilla Firefox, LibreOffice, and the GIMP image editor. Specific projects and components may use different licences, so the terms of the particular project should be checked.</p>

  <h2>What is source code?</h2>
  <p>Source code is the human-readable set of instructions written by programmers in a programming language. A computer normally executes software through machine code or an appropriate runtime environment.</p>

  <p>Access to source code allows permitted users to study how the program works, adapt it, and contribute changes. It does not automatically mean every modification can be distributed under any conditions.</p>

  <h2>Common characteristics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Source-code availability:</strong> the code is available under the project's licence.</li>
    <li><strong>Modification rights:</strong> users may change the code within the licence's conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Redistribution rights:</strong> users may redistribute the software as permitted by the licence.</li>
    <li><strong>Collaborative development:</strong> developers from different locations may review, report problems, and contribute improvements.</li>
    <li><strong>Licence conditions:</strong> some licences impose requirements concerning notices, source-code distribution, attribution, or licensing of derivative works.</li>
    <li><strong>Different cost models:</strong> the software may be free of charge or supplied with paid hosting, support, customization, or other services.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Permissive and copyleft licences</h2>
  <p>Open-source licences differ in their obligations.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Permissive licences:</strong> generally allow broad reuse, including in some proprietary products, provided their conditions are followed.</li>
    <li><strong>Copyleft licences:</strong> require certain distributed derivative works or combined works, as defined by the licence, to preserve specified freedoms or licensing conditions.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are broad categories. The exact obligations depend on the licence, the way components are used, and applicable law.</p>

  <h2>Advantages</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Users can inspect code and modify it where their skills and the licence permit.</li>
    <li>Organizations may reduce dependence on a single vendor.</li>
    <li>Communities can contribute fixes, improvements, translations, and documentation.</li>
    <li>Projects can be adapted to particular technical or educational needs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Possible limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Some projects may have limited official support or documentation.</li>
    <li>Installation and customization may require technical skills.</li>
    <li>Project quality and maintenance vary; an abandoned project may contain unresolved defects.</li>
    <li>Organizations must understand licence obligations and maintain secure update practices.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Worked examples</h2>
  <h3>Example 1: A school wants to customize a learning tool</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> A school wants to add a local-language interface to an open-source learning application.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The source code and licence may permit the school to make and distribute an adapted version.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Review the licence, modify the appropriate interface text, test the changes, preserve required notices, and distribute the adaptation in accordance with the licence.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: A business offers paid support</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> A business charges customers for installing and supporting an open-source application. A customer claims that open-source software must be provided without charge.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> Open-source licensing concerns source-code availability and permissions, not a universal requirement that every related service be free.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> The business may charge for permitted services or distribution, provided it complies with the software's licence and other applicable obligations.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: Choosing an abandoned project</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> A company finds an open-source application that meets its needs but has not received updates for several years.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> Source-code access does not guarantee active maintenance, security fixes, or compatibility with current systems.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Review the project's maintenance history, known vulnerabilities, dependencies, documentation, and available support. Choose a maintained alternative or plan and resource responsible maintenance.</p>

  <h2>Common misconception</h2>
  <p>Open-source does not simply mean “free to download.” It refers to permissions associated with the source code and the licence. Open-source software can be sold, and free-of-charge software can be proprietary.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "A school downloads an open-source application and wants to translate its interface into Kiswahili. What should the school check and do before distributing its modified version?",
      hint: "Access to code is important, but distribution must also follow the licence.",
      steps: [
        "Inspect the project's licence and confirm that modification is permitted.",
        "Identify any requirements for notices, attribution, or distribution.",
        "Translate the relevant interface strings and test the result.",
        "Distribute the modified version in compliance with the licence."
      ],
      ans: "The school should check the licence, make and test the translation, and comply with any applicable attribution, notice, source-code, or redistribution requirements.",
      why: "Open-source licences grant meaningful freedoms but may also impose conditions on modified versions and their distribution."
    },
    {
      q: "A company charges customers for installing an open-source application and providing training. A customer claims that this is automatically prohibited. Explain why the claim is not necessarily correct.",
      hint: "Separate the software's licensing freedoms from the price of a service.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the application as open-source.",
        "Recognize that open-source licensing does not universally require zero-price services.",
        "Check the licence terms governing distribution and related obligations.",
        "Distinguish the installation and training fees from the software's licence conditions."
      ],
      ans: "The company may charge for installation and training if the arrangement complies with the applicable licence and law.",
      why: "Open-source software can be part of a paid service or commercial offering. Open-source status does not require every related service to be free."
    },
    {
      q: "A project publishes its source code but its licence prohibits users from redistributing modified copies. Can it automatically be classified as open-source? Explain.",
      hint: "Code visibility alone is not enough; consider the freedoms that the licence grants.",
      steps: [
        "Recognize that source code is available.",
        "Check whether users are granted the required modification and redistribution freedoms.",
        "Compare the restrictions with the requirements of an open-source licence.",
        "Avoid classifying the project as open-source based only on code visibility."
      ],
      ans: "No. Making source code visible is not sufficient if the licence fails to grant the permissions required for open-source software.",
      why: "Open-source status depends on the licence's permissions and conditions, not merely on whether a person can read the code."
    },
    {
      q: "A business selects an open-source application because it assumes that the application will never have security vulnerabilities and will always receive updates. Diagnose both assumptions.",
      hint: "Consider the difference between legal permissions and the actual maintenance of a software project.",
      steps: [
        "Recognize that source-code availability allows inspection but does not prove that every defect is known.",
        "Check whether the project is actively maintained.",
        "Review updates, vulnerability reports, dependencies, and available support.",
        "Establish an update and security-monitoring process."
      ],
      ans: "Both assumptions are incorrect. Open-source software can have vulnerabilities, and updates depend on the project's maintenance and support.",
      why: "A licence gives permissions; it does not guarantee software quality, continuous maintenance, or security."
    },
    {
      q: "A developer combines an open-source library with a proprietary application and plans to distribute the result. What must the developer establish before release?",
      hint: "Different open-source licences impose different obligations when software is redistributed or combined.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the exact library and its licence.",
        "Read the licence's conditions for redistribution and combined or derivative works.",
        "Check whether the intended distribution method satisfies those conditions.",
        "Meet applicable notice, attribution, source-code, or other obligations before release."
      ],
      ans: "The developer must review the library's exact licence and determine whether the combined distribution complies with its conditions and any other applicable obligations.",
      why: "Not all open-source licences impose identical requirements. Compatibility and obligations must be assessed for the actual way the software is used and distributed."
    }
  ]
);

add(
  "computer",
  "software",
  "freeware_and_shareware",
  "Freeware and Shareware",
  `
  <h2>Meaning of freeware</h2>
  <p>Freeware is software made available for use without a purchase price under its distribution terms. Freeware is often proprietary, meaning users may still be restricted from modifying its source code or redistributing copies.</p>

  <h2>Meaning of shareware</h2>
  <p>Shareware is software distributed so that users can try it before paying, or use it under limited conditions. Restrictions may include a trial period, limited features, usage limits, or reminders to purchase a licence.</p>

  <p>Modern software may also use free and paid tiers, freemium plans, or subscription trials. These arrangements can resemble shareware in some respects, but the actual terms vary.</p>

  <h2>How the terms differ</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Freeware:</strong> the software can generally be used without paying a purchase price under its stated terms.</li>
    <li><strong>Shareware:</strong> the software is offered for evaluation or limited use, often with payment required for continued or full use.</li>
    <li><strong>Open-source software:</strong> the licence grants specified source-code and redistribution freedoms. It may or may not cost money.</li>
    <li><strong>Proprietary software:</strong> use and other rights are controlled by the owner or rights holder. It may be paid or free of charge.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>These terms describe different aspects of software. Freeware and shareware mainly describe distribution or pricing arrangements, while open-source and proprietary describe permissions and control. A particular product should be classified according to its actual terms rather than its label alone.</p>

  <h2>Important considerations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Check whether the software is free for personal use, educational use, or commercial use.</li>
    <li>Check whether a trial expires or disables features.</li>
    <li>Check whether payment is one-time or recurring.</li>
    <li>Check privacy terms, data collection, advertisements, and optional bundled applications.</li>
    <li>Download from an official or otherwise trusted source.</li>
    <li>Do not bypass trial restrictions or assume that a free download grants redistribution rights.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Worked examples</h2>
  <h3>Example 1: A free image viewer</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> A student installs an image viewer that costs nothing but does not provide its source code.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The lack of a purchase price does not establish that the source code and modification rights are available.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> If the application is supplied for use without payment under its terms, it may be freeware. Check its licence to determine whether copying, modification, or commercial use is permitted.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: A 14-day trial</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> A student installs a design application that works fully for 14 days and then requests payment.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The application is provided for evaluation before the user pays for continued or full access.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Treat it as a trial or shareware-style arrangement if its terms fit that model. The student should purchase an appropriate licence, stop using it, or choose a lawful alternative when the trial ends.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: A free application for business use</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> A business finds a free application that permits personal use but excludes commercial use.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The zero purchase price does not remove the stated use restriction.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Obtain a licence that permits commercial use or select another application whose terms meet the business's requirements.</p>

  <h2>Common misconception</h2>
  <p>Freeware does not automatically mean open-source, and shareware does not automatically mean unsafe or illegal. The actual licence, source, and installation practices determine what is permitted and what risks should be considered.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "An image editor costs nothing to download, but its licence prohibits users from modifying its code. A learner calls it open-source because it is free. Diagnose the classification error.",
      hint: "Ask what the price tells you and what the licence tells you.",
      steps: [
        "Identify that the application has no purchase price.",
        "Check whether users have the required source-code and modification permissions.",
        "Recognize that those permissions are restricted.",
        "Classify it as potentially freeware and proprietary, depending on its actual terms."
      ],
      ans: "The learner has confused price with source-code freedoms. The application may be freeware while remaining proprietary.",
      why: "Freeware describes a no-purchase-price distribution arrangement; open-source status depends on the licence's permissions."
    },
    {
      q: "A program works for 30 days and then requires payment to continue using its full features. What type of distribution arrangement does this resemble, and what should the user do when the trial ends?",
      hint: "Consider software offered for evaluation before purchase.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the time-limited evaluation period.",
        "Recognize that continued or full use requires payment.",
        "Check the terms governing trial expiry.",
        "Purchase an appropriate licence, stop using the program, or select a lawful alternative."
      ],
      ans: "It resembles a shareware or time-limited trial arrangement. When the trial ends, the user should follow the terms by purchasing, stopping use, or choosing an alternative.",
      why: "A trial does not grant indefinite access to features that require a paid licence."
    },
    {
      q: "A teacher finds freeware and wants to install it on every school computer. What must be checked before deployment, even though the software costs nothing?",
      hint: "A zero purchase price does not answer every licensing question.",
      steps: [
        "Read the licence or terms of use.",
        "Check whether educational and institutional use is permitted.",
        "Check installation or device limits.",
        "Review privacy, security, and system requirements before deploying it."
      ],
      ans: "The teacher should verify educational and institutional permissions, installation limits, privacy and security terms, and system requirements.",
      why: "Freeware may still impose restrictions on users, devices, or purposes. Suitability and safety must also be assessed."
    },
    {
      q: "A learner's trial application has expired. The learner changes the computer's clock to try to extend the trial without permission. Explain why this is not an appropriate solution and give two alternatives.",
      hint: "Focus on complying with the software terms rather than finding a technical workaround.",
      steps: [
        "Recognize that the trial has ended under its stated terms.",
        "Identify that bypassing the restriction may violate those terms.",
        "Consider obtaining the required licence.",
        "Alternatively, choose a suitable application that can lawfully be used without payment."
      ],
      ans: "The learner should not assume that bypassing the trial restriction is permitted. They can purchase a licence or use a suitable lawful alternative.",
      why: "Technical ability to bypass a restriction does not establish permission to do so."
    },
    {
      q: "Two applications are available at no charge. One is open-source and the other is proprietary freeware. State one important difference in the permissions a user may have, and explain why both can cost nothing.",
      hint: "Compare source-code rights and the purchase price separately.",
      steps: [
        "Identify that both applications have a zero purchase price.",
        "Check the open-source application's licence for source-code, modification, and redistribution rights.",
        "Check the freeware application's terms, which may restrict those rights.",
        "Separate the pricing model from the permissions granted by each licence."
      ],
      ans: "The open-source application grants specified source-code and modification freedoms, while the proprietary freeware may restrict them. Both can cost nothing because price does not determine licensing freedoms.",
      why: "Software pricing and software licensing are separate dimensions. A free-of-charge product can still have restrictive terms."
    }
  ]
);

add(
  "computer",
  "software",
  "software_installation_and_compatibility",
  "Software Installation and Compatibility",
  `
  <h2>Meaning of software installation</h2>
  <p>Software installation is the process of preparing a program for use on a computer or other device. Depending on the application, this may involve copying files, configuring components, registering libraries, setting permissions, and creating shortcuts or system entries.</p>

  <p>Some applications run without a conventional installation, while others require an installer, an online setup process, or an administrator's approval.</p>

  <h2>System requirements</h2>
  <p>Before installing software, check the manufacturer's or project's system requirements.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Operating system:</strong> the application must support the device's operating system and version.</li>
    <li><strong>Processor architecture:</strong> the software must support the device's processor architecture, such as x86-64 or ARM64, unless a compatible translation or emulation layer is available.</li>
    <li><strong>Memory:</strong> sufficient RAM is needed for the application and its workload.</li>
    <li><strong>Storage:</strong> enough free space is needed for installation, updates, and working files.</li>
    <li><strong>Dependencies:</strong> some applications require runtimes, libraries, drivers, or other supporting components.</li>
    <li><strong>Graphics and peripherals:</strong> specialized software may require a suitable graphics processor, display, printer, camera, or other device.</li>
    <li><strong>Permissions:</strong> installation may require administrator approval or an authorized account.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Meaning of software compatibility</h2>
  <p>Software compatibility is the ability of an application to operate correctly within a particular environment or work correctly with other software, file formats, or hardware.</p>

  <p>Compatibility can involve several dimensions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Operating-system compatibility:</strong> the program supports the operating system and version.</li>
    <li><strong>Hardware compatibility:</strong> the device meets the processor, memory, storage, and peripheral requirements.</li>
    <li><strong>File compatibility:</strong> one application can open or correctly interpret files created by another.</li>
    <li><strong>Version compatibility:</strong> different software versions can work together or exchange data as required.</li>
    <li><strong>Dependency compatibility:</strong> required libraries and components are available in suitable versions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A safe installation procedure</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Confirm that the software is suitable for the intended task.</li>
    <li>Check system requirements and licence terms.</li>
    <li>Obtain the installer from the official developer or another trusted, authorized source.</li>
    <li>Check that the downloaded file is authentic when verification information is provided.</li>
    <li>Back up important files when the installation could affect existing data or system settings.</li>
    <li>Close unnecessary programs and install using appropriate permissions.</li>
    <li>Read the installation options and avoid unwanted bundled software.</li>
    <li>Restart the device if required, then launch and test the application.</li>
    <li>Apply relevant security updates and keep the software maintained.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common installation problems</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Insufficient storage:</strong> free up appropriate space or choose a suitable storage location.</li>
    <li><strong>Unsupported operating system:</strong> obtain a supported version or use a compatible environment.</li>
    <li><strong>Missing dependency:</strong> install the required component from a trusted source or use an installer that supplies it appropriately.</li>
    <li><strong>Permission denied:</strong> use an authorized account or ask the device administrator.</li>
    <li><strong>Corrupted download:</strong> download the installer again from a trusted source and verify it when possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Conflicting software:</strong> investigate the documented conflict and apply a safe, supported resolution.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Worked examples</h2>
  <h3>Example 1: An application will not install</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> A laptop has sufficient storage, but the installer reports that the operating system is unsupported.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> Free storage does not compensate for an unsupported operating system. The application may depend on features or components that the installed system does not provide.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Check the supported operating systems and versions. Use a compatible application release or a supported environment if available. Do not assume that repeatedly running the same installer will solve an actual compatibility restriction.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: A download requires administrator permission</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> A student on a school computer cannot install an application because the account lacks administrator privileges.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The restriction may be part of the school's security policy rather than an installation fault.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Request installation from the authorized administrator or ask whether an approved portable or web-based alternative is available. Do not attempt to bypass the school's access controls.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: A file will not open in another application</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> A learner creates a document in one application and sends it to a classmate whose application cannot open the file correctly.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The applications may use different file formats or may not support the same features.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Check the supported formats and export the document to a suitable exchange format, such as PDF when the recipient mainly needs to read it. If continued editing is required, test a format supported by both applications and verify the result.</p>

  <h2>Updating and uninstalling software</h2>
  <p>Updates can fix defects, address security vulnerabilities, improve compatibility, and add features. Install updates from trusted sources and consider compatibility requirements for critical systems.</p>

  <p>Uninstallation removes an application and some of its associated files or settings. It may not remove every user document, configuration file, or shared component. Before uninstalling important software, back up necessary data and understand what the uninstall process will remove.</p>

  <h2>Common misconception</h2>
  <p>If a computer has enough storage, that does not prove that an application will run. Operating-system support, processor architecture, memory, dependencies, permissions, and other requirements can also determine compatibility.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "A computer has 80 GB of free storage, but a program's installer says the operating system is unsupported. The learner assumes that freeing another 20 GB will solve the problem. Diagnose the reasoning.",
      hint: "Compare the reported problem with the system requirement that is actually failing.",
      steps: [
        "Recognize that the installer reports an operating-system incompatibility.",
        "Separate storage capacity from operating-system support.",
        "Check which operating systems and versions the program supports.",
        "Choose a supported release or suitable alternative if available."
      ],
      ans: "Freeing more storage is unlikely to solve an unsupported-operating-system error. The learner should check the supported operating systems and use a compatible option.",
      why: "An application must satisfy its relevant compatibility requirements. Excess storage cannot replace an unsupported operating system."
    },
    {
      q: "A student downloads an installer from an unfamiliar website because the official download is slower. The application installs successfully. Explain why successful installation does not prove that the download was safe and recommend a better procedure.",
      hint: "A program can install and still contain unwanted or malicious components.",
      steps: [
        "Recognize that installation success only shows that the installer ran sufficiently to complete.",
        "Consider the possibility of tampering or unwanted bundled software.",
        "Obtain the installer from the official developer or a trusted authorized source.",
        "Verify authenticity or integrity when suitable checks are available."
      ],
      ans: "Successful installation does not prove that the installer was safe. The student should use an official or trusted authorized source and verify the file when possible.",
      why: "A malicious or modified installer can complete installation. The source and authenticity of the file matter as well as whether it runs."
    },
    {
      q: "A learner can open a document in their own word processor, but a classmate cannot edit it in a different application. What should they investigate, and how should they choose an exchange format?",
      hint: "Distinguish opening a file for reading from editing it while preserving its structure.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the source application's file format.",
        "Check the formats supported by the recipient's application.",
        "Determine whether the recipient needs to read the file or continue editing it.",
        "Export or save to a mutually supported format and test the result."
      ],
      ans: "They should check file-format compatibility and the recipient's editing requirements. PDF may suit read-only sharing, while a mutually supported editable format is better for continued editing.",
      why: "A format that preserves appearance may not preserve editability, and support for a file extension does not guarantee that every feature will transfer correctly."
    },
    {
      q: "A school computer blocks an installation because the student does not have administrator permission. The student asks how to bypass the restriction. Give an appropriate response and two practical alternatives.",
      hint: "Consider authorization and the purpose of device access controls.",
      steps: [
        "Recognize that the restriction may be imposed by the school administrator.",
        "Avoid bypassing access controls without authorization.",
        "Ask the administrator to install the approved application.",
        "Check whether an authorized web-based or portable alternative is available."
      ],
      ans: "The student should not bypass the school's controls. They can request an authorized installation or use an approved web-based or portable alternative if available.",
      why: "Administrator restrictions protect managed devices. A technical workaround does not automatically grant permission to install software."
    },
    {
      q: "A graphics application requires 8 GB of RAM and a supported 64-bit operating system. A computer has 16 GB of RAM but runs an unsupported operating-system version. Is the computer necessarily compatible? Explain what else must be checked.",
      hint: "Meeting one requirement does not prove that every requirement is satisfied.",
      steps: [
        "Confirm that the computer meets the memory requirement.",
        "Check the operating-system version and architecture against the supported list.",
        "Check the processor architecture and other hardware requirements.",
        "Check dependencies, graphics requirements, storage, and permissions where applicable."
      ],
      ans: "No. The computer meets the RAM requirement but may still be incompatible because its operating-system version is unsupported. Other hardware, dependency, and permission requirements should also be checked.",
      why: "Compatibility depends on the full set of requirements, not a single specification."
    }
  ]
);
