add(
  "computer",
  "operating_systems",
  "account_security_and_session_management",
  "Account Security and Session Management",
  `<h3>1. Meaning of a User Session</h3>
  <p>A <strong>user session</strong> is the period during which a user is signed in and interacts with an operating system or service. A session may retain the user's identity and access state while applications are being used.</p>

  <p>Signing in usually establishes a session. Locking the screen, signing out and shutting down are different actions with different effects.</p>

  <h3>2. Locking, Signing Out and Shutting Down</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lock:</strong> Restricts immediate access to the current session until an approved unlock method is used. Applications commonly remain open.</li>
    <li><strong>Sign out:</strong> Ends the user's interactive session. Applications may close, so unsaved work should be saved first.</li>
    <li><strong>Shut down:</strong> Turns off the computer through the operating system's shutdown process and ends active sessions.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Exact behaviour varies by operating system and application. Locking a session is not the same as signing out, and neither action guarantees that every remote service session has ended.</p>

  <h3>3. Secure Session Habits</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Lock a computer before leaving it unattended.</li>
    <li>Sign out of shared computers when finished.</li>
    <li>Save work before signing out or shutting down.</li>
    <li>Avoid saving credentials on shared or untrusted devices.</li>
    <li>Review and revoke unfamiliar active sessions where a service provides that option.</li>
    <li>Keep the operating system and security software updated.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Automatic Screen Lock</h3>
  <p>An automatic screen lock activates after a configured period of inactivity. It helps reduce the chance that another person can use an unattended computer. The user should still lock the screen manually when leaving, rather than relying only on a timer.</p>

  <h3>5. Lost or Compromised Accounts</h3>
  <p>If a device is lost or an account may have been compromised, use the organisation's incident-reporting process. Depending on the system, appropriate actions may include changing the password from a trusted device, revoking active sessions, disabling a lost device's access, and reviewing recent account activity.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Leaving a Laboratory Computer</h3>
  <p><strong>Situation:</strong> A learner must leave a computer briefly to speak to a teacher while a document is open.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Save the document to avoid losing recent work.</li>
    <li>Lock the screen before leaving the computer unattended.</li>
    <li>Unlock it securely on return.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Locking protects the unattended session while allowing work to remain open.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finishing Work on a Shared Computer</h3>
  <p><strong>Situation:</strong> A learner has completed work in a school computer laboratory and will not use the computer again that day.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Save the document and verify that the save succeeded.</li>
    <li>Sign out of the learner's account.</li>
    <li>Close or sign out of separate web services when required.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Signing out helps prevent the next user from inheriting the previous learner's interactive session.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A Suspicious Active Session</h3>
  <p><strong>Situation:</strong> A learner's online account shows an active session on an unfamiliar device.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Use the official account security page from a trusted device.</li>
    <li>Revoke the unfamiliar session if the service provides that control.</li>
    <li>Change the password if compromise is suspected.</li>
    <li>Review recovery details and multi-factor settings, and report the incident if it is a school account.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Account protection may require managing remote sessions as well as changing a password.</p>

  <h3>Check Your Understanding</h3>`,
  [
    {
      q: "A learner leaves a computer for two minutes while a document is open. Which action is most appropriate, and why?",
      hint: "The learner will return shortly and wants the document to remain open.",
      steps: [
        "Recognise that the computer will be unattended.",
        "Save recent work.",
        "Lock the screen before leaving."
      ],
      ans: "Save the work and lock the screen. The session can remain open while access is restricted until the user unlocks it.",
      why: "Locking reduces the risk of someone else using an unattended session without unnecessarily ending it."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why signing out of a shared computer is different from locking it.",
      hint: "Consider whether the interactive session remains active.",
      steps: [
        "Identify locking as restricting access to the existing session.",
        "Identify signing out as ending the interactive session.",
        "Explain why signing out is usually appropriate when finished on a shared computer."
      ],
      ans: "Locking normally leaves the session and its applications open, whereas signing out ends the interactive session and may close applications. Signing out is generally appropriate when finished using a shared computer.",
      why: "The two actions manage the user's session differently."
    },
    {
      q: "A learner discovers an unfamiliar active session in an online account. State a sensible response.",
      hint: "The activity may involve another device, not just the current computer.",
      steps: [
        "Access the official security settings from a trusted device.",
        "Revoke the unfamiliar session if possible.",
        "Change the password if compromise is suspected.",
        "Review security settings and report the incident where appropriate."
      ],
      ans: "Use the official account security controls to revoke the unfamiliar session, secure the credentials if necessary, and report suspected compromise.",
      why: "Changing a password alone may not terminate every existing session."
    },
    {
      q: "A learner closes a laptop lid and assumes this always signs out of every online account. Explain why the assumption is unsafe.",
      hint: "Think about sleep, locking and sessions maintained by websites.",
      steps: [
        "Recognise that closing the lid may put the computer to sleep or trigger a configured action.",
        "Explain that this does not necessarily end the operating-system session.",
        "Explain that websites may maintain their own sessions.",
        "Recommend locking or signing out as appropriate."
      ],
      ans: "Closing the lid may only suspend or lock the computer, depending on its settings. It does not necessarily sign out the operating system or end sessions maintained by online services.",
      why: "Device power behaviour and application session management are separate mechanisms."
    },
    {
      q: "A school computer takes 20 minutes to lock automatically. Give two reasons why a learner should still lock it manually before leaving.",
      hint: "Consider what could happen during the waiting period.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the time when the computer is left unattended.",
        "Recognise that someone could access it before the automatic lock activates.",
        "Explain that manual locking protects the session immediately."
      ],
      ans: "Someone could use the computer during the 20-minute delay, and manual locking protects the session immediately instead of leaving it exposed until the timer activates.",
      why: "An automatic timeout is a backup protection, not a reason to leave a session accessible."
    }
  ]
);
