add("computer", "networking", "computer_networking_meaning_of_an_ip_address", "Meaning of an IP address", `
<h2>Meaning of an IP address</h2>

<p>An <b>Internet Protocol (IP) address</b> is a logical numerical address assigned to a network interface for communication using IP. It helps identify the source and destination of packets as they travel between networks.</p>

<h3>1. Why IP addresses are needed</h3>
<ul>
<li>They identify network interfaces at the IP layer.</li>
<li>They help devices direct packets toward their destinations.</li>
<li>Routers use destination IP addresses and routing information to forward packets.</li>
<li>IP addresses help distinguish network destinations, but they do not by themselves prove a user's identity.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Two IP versions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>IPv4:</b> Uses 32-bit addresses, commonly written as four decimal numbers separated by dots.</li>
<li><b>IPv6:</b> Uses 128-bit addresses, commonly written as groups of hexadecimal digits separated by colons.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Logical addressing</h3>
<p>An IP address is a logical address, unlike a MAC address, which is associated with a network interface at the data-link layer. IP addresses can change when a device joins a different network or receives a new address assignment.</p>

<h3>4. Example</h3>
<p>Consider the IPv4 address <b>192.168.1.25</b>.</p>
<ol>
<li>It is written in dotted-decimal form.</li>
<li>It contains four decimal octets separated by dots.</li>
<li>Each octet represents eight bits.</li>
<li>The address is commonly used as a private IPv4 address in local networks.</li>
</ol>

<h3>5. Important distinction</h3>
<p>An IP address can be assigned manually or automatically, for example by DHCP. Whether a device can communicate with another device depends on more than the address alone: the subnet configuration, routing, network connectivity and relevant security rules also matter.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing an IP address with a MAC address.</li>
<li>Assuming every IP address is globally reachable from the internet.</li>
<li>Assuming an IP address permanently identifies a particular person or physical device.</li>
</ul>
`, [
  {
    q: "Define an IP address.",
    hint: "Think about logical addressing and how packets are directed across networks.",
    steps: "1. IP communication uses logical addresses. 2. These addresses identify network interfaces for IP communication. 3. Routers use destination IP addresses with routing information to forward packets.",
    ans: "An IP address is a logical numerical address assigned to a network interface for IP communication and used to identify packet sources and destinations.",
    why: "IP addressing allows packets to be directed toward the correct network destination."
  },
  {
    q: "State two reasons why IP addresses are important in networking.",
    hint: "Consider identifying destinations and forwarding packets.",
    steps: "1. IP addresses identify network interfaces for IP communication. 2. A packet contains source and destination IP addresses. 3. Routers use destination addresses to help forward packets.",
    ans: "They identify source and destination interfaces for IP communication and help routers forward packets toward the correct destination.",
    why: "Without IP addressing, IP packets could not identify their intended network destinations."
  },
  {
    q: "Distinguish between IPv4 and IPv6 by the size of their addresses.",
    hint: "Compare the number of bits used by each version.",
    steps: "1. IPv4 uses 32 bits. 2. IPv6 uses 128 bits. 3. IPv6 therefore provides a much larger address space.",
    ans: "IPv4 uses 32-bit addresses, while IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses.",
    why: "The larger IPv6 address space supports a much greater number of unique addresses."
  },
  {
    q: "An IPv4 address is written as 192.168.1.25. Explain what the four numbers represent.",
    hint: "Consider how an IPv4 address is divided into octets.",
    steps: "1. IPv4 contains 32 bits. 2. These bits are divided into four groups of eight bits. 3. Each group is represented as a decimal number from 0 to 255. 4. The four numbers are separated by dots.",
    ans: "The four numbers are decimal representations of four 8-bit octets in a 32-bit IPv4 address.",
    why: "Dotted-decimal notation makes the 32-bit address easier for people to read."
  },
  {
    q: "Explain why an IP address does not necessarily identify a person permanently.",
    hint: "Consider automatic address assignment and connecting to different networks.",
    steps: "1. An IP address may be assigned dynamically. 2. A device may receive a different address later. 3. It may also use different addresses on different networks. 4. Therefore, the address alone does not permanently identify a person.",
    ans: "IP addresses can change through dynamic assignment or when a device joins another network, so an address alone does not permanently identify a person.",
    why: "IP addressing identifies network interfaces for communication, not a person's permanent identity."
  }
]);

add("computer", "networking", "computer_networking_ipv4_address_basics", "IPv4 address basics", `
<h2>IPv4 address basics</h2>

<p><b>IPv4</b> is an Internet Protocol version that uses a 32-bit address. An IPv4 address is usually written in dotted-decimal notation as four numbers separated by dots.</p>

<h3>1. The four octets</h3>
<p>Each 32-bit IPv4 address is divided into four 8-bit groups called <b>octets</b>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Each octet contains eight binary bits.</li>
<li>Each octet is written as a decimal number from 0 to 255.</li>
<li>The four octets are separated by dots.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Worked example: identifying the octets</h3>
<p>Consider <b>192.168.10.25</b>.</p>
<ol>
<li>The first octet is 192.</li>
<li>The second octet is 168.</li>
<li>The third octet is 10.</li>
<li>The fourth octet is 25.</li>
<li>There are four octets, each representing eight bits, giving \(4 × 8 = 32\) bits.</li>
</ol>

<h3>3. Binary place values</h3>
<p>The eight bit positions in an octet have the following place values, from left to right:</p>
<p><b>128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1</b></p>

<h3>Worked example: converting an octet</h3>
<p>Convert the binary octet <b>00001010</b> to decimal.</p>
<ol>
<li>Write the place values: 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1.</li>
<li>The 1 bits occur in the 8 and 2 positions.</li>
<li>Add their values: 8 + 2 = 10.</li>
<li>Therefore, 00001010 in binary equals 10 in decimal.</li>
</ol>

<h3>4. Network and host portions</h3>
<p>An IPv4 address is divided into network and host portions according to its subnet mask or prefix length. For example, <b>192.168.1.25/24</b> uses a 24-bit network prefix. The first 24 bits identify the network prefix, and the remaining eight bits identify the host portion within that subnet.</p>

<h3>5. Validity checks</h3>
<ul>
<li>An IPv4 address must contain four decimal octets when written in dotted-decimal form.</li>
<li>Each octet must be between 0 and 255 inclusive.</li>
<li>Whether an address is suitable for a particular host depends on its subnet and other addressing rules.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Allowing an octet value greater than 255.</li>
<li>Confusing the number of octets with the number of bits.</li>
<li>Assuming the network portion is always the first three octets; it depends on the prefix length.</li>
</ul>
`, [
  {
    q: "How many bits are in an IPv4 address, and how many octets does it contain?",
    hint: "Each octet contains eight bits.",
    steps: "1. IPv4 uses 32 bits. 2. Divide 32 by 8 to find the number of octets. 3. 32 ÷ 8 = 4 octets.",
    ans: "An IPv4 address contains 32 bits arranged in four 8-bit octets.",
    why: "Dotted-decimal notation represents each of the four octets as a decimal number."
  },
  {
    q: "How many bits are represented by the address 10.20.30.40?",
    hint: "Count the octets and multiply by the bits in each octet.",
    steps: "1. The address has four octets. 2. Each octet represents eight bits. 3. Calculate 4 × 8 = 32 bits.",
    ans: "32 bits.",
    why: "All IPv4 addresses use 32 bits regardless of the decimal values in their octets."
  },
  {
    q: "Convert the binary octet 00001101 to decimal.",
    hint: "Use the place values 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1.",
    steps: "1. The 1 bits occur in the 8, 4 and 1 positions. 2. Add the values: 8 + 4 + 1 = 13. 3. The decimal value is 13.",
    ans: "13.",
    why: "Only place values with a binary digit of 1 contribute to the decimal total."
  },
  {
    q: "Explain why 192.168.1.300 is not a valid dotted-decimal IPv4 address.",
    hint: "Check the permitted range of an octet.",
    steps: "1. IPv4 has four decimal octets. 2. Each octet must be between 0 and 255 inclusive. 3. The final octet, 300, exceeds 255.",
    ans: "It is invalid because the last octet is 300, which exceeds the maximum permitted octet value of 255.",
    why: "Each IPv4 octet represents eight bits and therefore has a decimal range of 0 to 255."
  },
  {
    q: "For 192.168.1.25/24, how many bits are in the network prefix and how many remain in the host portion?",
    hint: "IPv4 has 32 bits in total; the prefix length specifies the number of network bits.",
    steps: "1. The prefix length is 24 bits. 2. The total IPv4 address length is 32 bits. 3. Calculate the remaining host bits: 32 − 24 = 8.",
    ans: "The network prefix contains 24 bits, and the host portion contains 8 bits.",
    why: "The prefix length determines the division between network and host portions; it is not always a three-octet division."
  }
]);

add("computer", "networking", "computer_networking_public_and_private_addresses", "Public and private addresses", `
<h2>Public and private addresses</h2>

<p>IPv4 addresses can be classified as <b>public</b> or <b>private</b> according to their use and allocation.</p>

<h3>1. Public IPv4 addresses</h3>
<p>A public IPv4 address is globally unique within the public internet addressing system and can be routed on the public internet when correctly assigned and when network policies permit it. A public address alone does not guarantee that a service is reachable; firewalls, routing and other settings also matter.</p>

<h3>2. Private IPv4 addresses</h3>
<p>Private IPv4 addresses are reserved for use within private networks. They are not normally routed across the public internet. A private address can be reused in separate private networks.</p>

<p>The three private IPv4 ranges are:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255</b></li>
<li><b>172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255</b></li>
<li><b>192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255</b></li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Why private addresses are useful</h3>
<ul>
<li>They allow devices to communicate within local and organisational networks.</li>
<li>The same private address ranges can be reused in different private networks.</li>
<li>They reduce the need to assign a unique public IPv4 address to every internal device.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Accessing the internet</h3>
<p>A network using private IPv4 addresses commonly uses Network Address Translation (NAT) on a gateway to enable internet communication. NAT translates between private and public addressing as configured. Private addressing alone does not provide internet access.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A school computer uses <b>192.168.1.20</b>.</p>
<ol>
<li>The address falls within 192.168.0.0–192.168.255.255.</li>
<li>That range is reserved for private IPv4 use.</li>
<li>The computer can communicate with suitable devices on its local network.</li>
<li>For internet access, the network may send traffic through a gateway that provides routing and, commonly, NAT.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming every address beginning with 172 is private; only 172.16.0.0 through 172.31.255.255 in that block is reserved for private use.</li>
<li>Assuming a private address can never communicate with the internet through a gateway.</li>
<li>Assuming every public address is automatically reachable from every other device.</li>
</ul>
`, [
  {
    q: "Distinguish between a public IPv4 address and a private IPv4 address.",
    hint: "Consider whether the address is intended for public internet routing or private network use.",
    steps: "1. Public IPv4 addresses are globally unique in the public addressing system and can be routed on the internet when properly configured. 2. Private IPv4 addresses are reserved for use within private networks and are not normally routed across the public internet.",
    ans: "A public IPv4 address is intended for public internet addressing, while a private IPv4 address is reserved for use within private networks.",
    why: "The two categories serve different addressing purposes."
  },
  {
    q: "A computer has the IPv4 address 192.168.1.20. Is it public or private? Explain.",
    hint: "Compare the address with the reserved private IPv4 ranges.",
    steps: "1. One private range is 192.168.0.0–192.168.255.255. 2. The address 192.168.1.20 falls within that range. 3. It is therefore private.",
    ans: "It is a private IPv4 address because it falls within 192.168.0.0–192.168.255.255.",
    why: "The entire 192.168.0.0/16 range is reserved for private IPv4 use."
  },
  {
    q: "Is 172.20.5.10 a private IPv4 address? Show how you determine the answer.",
    hint: "Check the private range beginning at 172.16.0.0 and ending at 172.31.255.255.",
    steps: "1. The private 172 range covers 172.16.0.0 through 172.31.255.255. 2. The second octet in 172.20.5.10 is 20. 3. Since 20 is between 16 and 31 inclusive, the address is within the private range.",
    ans: "Yes. 172.20.5.10 is private because it falls within 172.16.0.0–172.31.255.255.",
    why: "Only the 172.16.0.0/12 block of the 172 range is reserved for private IPv4 use."
  },
  {
    q: "Explain how a computer using a private IPv4 address can access a public website.",
    hint: "Consider the role of the gateway and Network Address Translation.",
    steps: "1. The computer sends traffic to its configured gateway. 2. The gateway routes the traffic toward the internet. 3. A gateway commonly uses NAT to translate the private source address to a public address for internet communication. 4. Return traffic is translated and forwarded to the appropriate internal device.",
    ans: "The computer sends traffic through a gateway that routes it to the internet and commonly uses NAT to translate between private and public addressing.",
    why: "Private addresses are not normally routed across the public internet, so a gateway commonly provides the required translation."
  },
  {
    q: "A learner says, 'Every address beginning with 172 is private.' Explain why this statement is incorrect.",
    hint: "The private range covers only part of the 172 address block.",
    steps: "1. The private 172 range begins at 172.16.0.0. 2. It ends at 172.31.255.255. 3. Addresses such as 172.15.0.1 and 172.32.0.1 are outside that private range. 4. Therefore, the first octet alone is insufficient to classify every 172 address as private.",
    ans: "The statement is incorrect because only 172.16.0.0 through 172.31.255.255 is reserved for private IPv4 use; other 172 addresses are outside that range.",
    why: "Correct classification requires checking the full reserved range rather than just the first octet."
  }
]);
add("computer", "networking", "computer_networking_subnet_mask", "Subnet Masks", `
<h2>Subnet Masks</h2>
A subnet mask separates the network portion of an IPv4 address from the host portion. It helps a computer determine whether another IP address belongs to the same local network or whether data should be sent through a router.

A common IPv4 subnet mask is 255.255.255.0, also written as /24. In a typical /24 network, the first three octets identify the network, while the last octet identifies a host within that network.

For example, computers with addresses 192.168.1.10 and 192.168.1.20 using the mask 255.255.255.0 belong to the same subnet. An address such as 192.168.2.10 belongs to a different subnet under that mask.

A subnet mask does not identify a computer by itself. It works together with the IP address to determine the network boundary.
`, [
  { q: "What is the purpose of a subnet mask?", hint: "Think about the network and host portions of an IP address.", steps: "1. Identify the IP address. 2. Apply the subnet mask to distinguish the network portion from the host portion.", ans: "It identifies which bits of an IP address represent the network and which represent the host.", why: "Devices need to determine whether a destination is local or must be reached through a router." },
  { q: "What is the dotted-decimal form of the /24 subnet mask?", hint: "A /24 mask contains 24 network bits.", steps: "1. Convert the first 24 bits to decimal. 2. The first three octets become 255, and the last becomes 0.", ans: "255.255.255.0", why: "Each octet containing eight network bits has a decimal value of 255." },
  { q: "Do 192.168.1.10 and 192.168.1.20 belong to the same subnet when the mask is 255.255.255.0?", hint: "Compare their first three octets.", steps: "1. Compare 192.168.1 in both addresses. 2. The network portions match.", ans: "Yes, they belong to the same subnet.", why: "The first three octets are identical under this subnet mask." },
  { q: "Using the mask 255.255.255.0, are 192.168.1.10 and 192.168.2.10 in the same subnet?", hint: "Look for the first difference in the network portion.", steps: "1. Compare the first three octets. 2. The third octet is 1 in one address and 2 in the other.", ans: "No, they belong to different subnets.", why: "Their network portions differ." },
  { q: "A student says a subnet mask uniquely identifies each computer. What is wrong with this statement?", hint: "Compare the purpose of an IP address with the purpose of a mask.", steps: "1. Recall that an IP address identifies a network interface logically. 2. Recall that the mask separates network and host portions.", ans: "A subnet mask does not uniquely identify a computer; it defines the network and host boundary of an IP address.", why: "Many devices on the same subnet can use the same subnet mask." }
]);

add("computer", "networking", "computer_networking_mac_address", "MAC Addresses", `
<h2>MAC Addresses</h2>
A Media Access Control (MAC) address is a link-layer address associated with a network interface. It is commonly used to deliver frames within a local network.

A MAC address is usually represented as six pairs of hexadecimal digits, such as 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E. Network interfaces normally have a factory-assigned address, although some devices and operating systems can use a locally assigned or randomized MAC address.

An IP address and a MAC address serve different purposes. An IP address supports logical addressing and routing between networks. A MAC address is used for delivery on a local network link. When sending traffic to another network, a computer normally sends the local frame to its default gateway's MAC address, while the packet retains its destination IP address.

A MAC address is not guaranteed to be permanent, globally unique in every real-world situation, or a reliable way to identify a person.
`, [
  { q: "What does MAC stand for in networking?", hint: "It names the link-layer addressing system.", steps: "Recall the full name of the abbreviation.", ans: "Media Access Control.", why: "MAC addresses are used at the link layer for local frame delivery." },
  { q: "How is a typical MAC address written?", hint: "Look for six pairs of hexadecimal digits.", steps: "1. Count the pairs. 2. Check that each pair uses hexadecimal digits.", ans: "As six pairs of hexadecimal digits, for example 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E.", why: "This is a common representation of a 48-bit MAC address." },
  { q: "State one difference between an IP address and a MAC address.", hint: "Think about logical routing versus local frame delivery.", steps: "1. Identify the role of an IP address. 2. Identify the role of a MAC address.", ans: "An IP address supports logical addressing and routing; a MAC address is used for delivery on a local network link.", why: "They operate at different layers and serve different networking purposes." },
  { q: "A computer sends data to a server on another network. Which device's MAC address does it normally use for the first local frame?", hint: "The computer must first reach the next hop.", steps: "1. Recognize that the destination is outside the local subnet. 2. Identify the next hop as the default gateway.", ans: "The MAC address of its default gateway interface on the local network.", why: "The router forwards the packet toward the remote network." },
  { q: "A learner claims that a MAC address can never change. Is this always true?", hint: "Consider software settings and randomized addresses.", steps: "1. Recall that interfaces commonly have assigned MAC addresses. 2. Consider locally assigned or randomized addresses.", ans: "No. MAC addresses can sometimes be changed or randomized by software.", why: "A factory-assigned address is common, but it is not an absolute guarantee of an unchanging address." }
]);

add("computer", "networking", "computer_networking_dns", "Domain Name System (DNS)", `
<h2>Domain Name System (DNS)</h2>
The Domain Name System (DNS) translates human-readable domain names, such as example.com, into IP addresses that computers use to communicate.

Without DNS, users would often need to remember numerical IP addresses instead of domain names. When a device needs an address for a domain, it can query a DNS resolver. The resolver may answer from its cache or contact other DNS servers to obtain the required record.

DNS records can contain different kinds of information. An A record maps a name to an IPv4 address, while an AAAA record maps a name to an IPv6 address. Other records support functions such as mail routing.

DNS is not the same as a web browser or a search engine. It helps locate network destinations; it does not itself provide the website's pages or search results.
`, [
  { q: "What is the main purpose of DNS?", hint: "Think about domain names and IP addresses.", steps: "1. Identify what users type. 2. Identify the address computers need to contact the destination.", ans: "DNS translates domain names into the relevant network records, commonly including IP addresses.", why: "It allows users to use memorable names rather than having to remember numerical addresses." },
  { q: "What type of address does a DNS A record provide?", hint: "A is associated with one version of IP.", steps: "Recall the difference between IPv4 and IPv6.", ans: "An IPv4 address.", why: "An A record maps a domain name or hostname to an IPv4 address." },
  { q: "What is the purpose of an AAAA record?", hint: "It is the corresponding address record for the newer IP version.", steps: "1. Recall that A records represent IPv4. 2. Identify the version associated with AAAA.", ans: "It maps a name to an IPv6 address.", why: "IPv6 addresses require a different DNS record type from IPv4 addresses." },
  { q: "Why might a DNS resolver use a cache?", hint: "Consider whether the same domain is requested repeatedly.", steps: "1. Recognize that a domain may be looked up more than once. 2. Recall that cached records can be reused while valid.", ans: "To answer repeated lookups faster and reduce the need for additional DNS queries.", why: "Caching can reduce lookup delay and network traffic." },
  { q: "A website's domain name resolves successfully, but the page does not load. Does that prove DNS is working incorrectly?", hint: "Separate name resolution from the delivery of website content.", steps: "1. Recognize that DNS resolution succeeded. 2. Consider other possible causes, such as server, connection, or browser problems.", ans: "No. The failure may be caused by the web server, network connection, browser, or another service.", why: "DNS helps locate the destination but does not guarantee that the website itself is available." }
]);

add("computer", "networking", "computer_networking_dhcp", "Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP)", `
<h2>Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP)</h2>
Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) automatically supplies network configuration to devices joining a network. It can provide an IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, and DNS server addresses.

A DHCP server manages a pool of addresses and leases an available address to a client for a specified period. A client may renew its lease to continue using the address. This reduces the need to configure each device manually.

A common DHCP exchange is remembered as DORA: Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge. The client discovers available DHCP servers, a server offers configuration, the client requests an offered configuration, and the server acknowledges the lease.

DHCP does not replace DNS. DHCP configures a device to use the network; DNS helps resolve domain names. A network may use DHCP for most devices while reserving or manually configuring addresses for particular equipment.
`, [
  { q: "What is the main purpose of DHCP?", hint: "Think about what a device needs when it joins a network.", steps: "1. List common network settings. 2. Identify the protocol that can supply them automatically.", ans: "To automatically provide network configuration, such as an IP address, subnet mask, gateway, and DNS server.", why: "Automatic configuration reduces manual setup and address-entry errors." },
  { q: "What does the DORA sequence stand for?", hint: "It describes the common four-step DHCP exchange.", steps: "Recall the order: Discover, Offer, Request, then Acknowledge.", ans: "Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge.", why: "These steps describe how a client obtains a DHCP lease in the common exchange." },
  { q: "What is a DHCP lease?", hint: "An assigned address is usually provided for a period of time.", steps: "1. Recall that DHCP assigns configuration. 2. Identify the period for which the client may use the assigned address.", ans: "A time-limited allocation of network configuration, commonly including an IP address, to a client.", why: "The lease can be renewed or eventually returned to the pool." },
  { q: "How does DHCP differ from DNS?", hint: "One configures devices; the other resolves names.", steps: "1. State what DHCP supplies. 2. State what DNS resolves.", ans: "DHCP supplies network configuration; DNS resolves domain names to network records such as IP addresses.", why: "They solve different problems and are often used together." },
  { q: "A laptop connects to Wi-Fi but receives no usable IP configuration from DHCP. Give one possible consequence.", hint: "Think about what happens when the device lacks a valid network address.", steps: "1. Identify the missing configuration. 2. Connect it to the ability to communicate on the network.", ans: "The laptop may be unable to communicate normally on the local network or reach other networks.", why: "A usable IP configuration is needed for ordinary IP communication." }
]);

add("computer", "networking", "computer_networking_osi_model", "OSI Reference Model", `
<h2>OSI Reference Model</h2>
The Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) reference model divides network communication into seven conceptual layers. Each layer describes a different set of responsibilities, helping learners understand how data moves between applications and physical networks.

From top to bottom, the layers are:
7. Application — network services used by applications.
6. Presentation — data representation, translation, compression, and encryption concepts.
5. Session — establishing, managing, and ending communication sessions.
4. Transport — end-to-end delivery, segmentation, and reliability or flow control where provided.
3. Network — logical addressing and routing between networks.
2. Data Link — frames and delivery across a local link.
1. Physical — transmission of bits through physical or radio signals.

When data is sent, it moves down the layers at the sender and up the layers at the receiver. The OSI model is a reference framework, not a requirement that every real network protocol maps perfectly to one layer.
`, [
  { q: "How many layers are in the OSI reference model?", hint: "Count from Physical at the bottom to Application at the top.", steps: "List the layers in order: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application.", ans: "Seven layers.", why: "The OSI model divides communication into seven conceptual layers." },
  { q: "Which OSI layer is primarily associated with routing between networks?", hint: "It is layer 3.", steps: "1. Recall the bottom-up layer numbers. 2. Identify the Network layer.", ans: "The Network layer (Layer 3).", why: "This layer handles logical addressing and routing between networks." },
  { q: "Which OSI layer is associated with frames and local-link delivery?", hint: "It sits directly above the Physical layer.", steps: "1. Identify Layer 1 as Physical. 2. Move one layer up.", ans: "The Data Link layer (Layer 2).", why: "The Data Link layer handles frames and delivery over a local link." },
  { q: "At which OSI layer are TCP and UDP commonly placed?", hint: "These protocols support communication between applications across hosts.", steps: "1. Recall that TCP and UDP provide transport services. 2. Match these services to the OSI layer.", ans: "The Transport layer (Layer 4).", why: "TCP and UDP are transport-layer protocols in the usual OSI mapping." },
  { q: "Why is the OSI model useful even though real protocols do not always fit its layers perfectly?", hint: "Think about learning, describing, and troubleshooting networks.", steps: "1. Recognize that networking involves many responsibilities. 2. Explain how dividing them into layers helps people reason about problems.", ans: "It provides a common framework for understanding, describing, and troubleshooting network functions.", why: "A layered model makes complex communication easier to discuss without requiring every protocol to match it exactly." }
]);
add("computer", "networking", "computer_networking_tcp_ip_model", "TCP/IP Model", `
<h2>TCP/IP Model</h2>
The TCP/IP model describes how devices communicate across interconnected networks, including the internet. It groups networking functions into four layers.

1. Application layer — provides network services to applications. Examples include HTTP, DNS, and SMTP.
2. Transport layer — supports communication between applications on different devices. TCP provides reliable, ordered delivery, while UDP provides datagram delivery without guaranteeing delivery.
3. Internet layer — handles logical IP addressing and routing packets between networks.
4. Network Access layer — handles communication over the local network technology, including framing and physical transmission.

When data is sent, each layer adds information needed for delivery. At the receiving device, the corresponding layers process that information.

The TCP/IP model is related to the seven-layer OSI model, but their layer boundaries differ. The TCP/IP model is commonly used to explain the protocols that operate on real networks.
`, [
  { q: "How many layers are in the commonly taught four-layer TCP/IP model?", hint: "Count Application, Transport, Internet, and Network Access.", steps: "List the four named layers and count them.", ans: "Four layers.", why: "The four-layer model groups networking functions into four broad areas." },
  { q: "Which TCP/IP layer handles IP addressing and routing between networks?", hint: "It is responsible for moving packets between networks.", steps: "Identify the layer responsible for IP packets and routing.", ans: "The Internet layer.", why: "This layer handles logical addressing and routing." },
  { q: "At which TCP/IP layer do TCP and UDP operate?", hint: "These protocols support communication between applications.", steps: "Recall that TCP and UDP provide transport services.", ans: "The Transport layer.", why: "TCP and UDP are transport-layer protocols." },
  { q: "Name one protocol associated with the TCP/IP Application layer.", hint: "Consider web browsing, name resolution, or email.", steps: "Recall a protocol such as HTTP, DNS, or SMTP.", ans: "HTTP, DNS, or SMTP.", why: "These protocols provide services used by network applications." },
  { q: "State one difference between the TCP/IP model and the OSI model.", hint: "Compare their commonly taught number of layers.", steps: "Recall the four-layer TCP/IP model and the seven-layer OSI model.", ans: "The TCP/IP model commonly has four layers, while the OSI model has seven.", why: "The models organize networking functions differently." }
]);

add("computer", "networking", "computer_networking_troubleshooting", "Network Troubleshooting", `
<h2>Network Troubleshooting</h2>
Network troubleshooting is the systematic process of identifying and correcting problems that prevent devices from communicating properly.

Start by describing the problem clearly. Check simple causes first, such as a disconnected cable, disabled Wi-Fi, incorrect settings, or a powered-off device. Then check whether the problem affects one device, several devices, or the whole network.

Useful checks include:
- Inspecting cables, power, and wireless connections.
- Checking the device's IP address and network configuration.
- Testing connectivity to the local gateway.
- Testing access to a known remote IP address.
- Testing DNS resolution when IP connectivity works but domain names fail.

Change one thing at a time and test again. Record the cause and solution so the same problem can be resolved more efficiently in the future.

A successful connection to one destination does not prove that every network service is working. Troubleshooting should use evidence to narrow down the fault.
`, [
  { q: "What is network troubleshooting?", hint: "Think about finding and correcting communication problems.", steps: "1. Identify the problem. 2. Find its cause. 3. Apply and test a solution.", ans: "The systematic process of identifying and correcting network problems.", why: "A structured process helps locate faults instead of relying on guesses." },
  { q: "A computer cannot connect to Wi-Fi. State one simple check to perform first.", hint: "Check the connection before investigating advanced settings.", steps: "Check whether Wi-Fi is enabled and whether the device is connected to the intended network.", ans: "Check that Wi-Fi is enabled and the device is connected to the correct network.", why: "A disabled wireless adapter or incorrect network selection can prevent connectivity." },
  { q: "Several computers lose internet access at the same time. What does this suggest?", hint: "Consider what the affected devices have in common.", steps: "1. Note that multiple devices are affected. 2. Check shared equipment or services, such as the router or internet connection.", ans: "The fault may involve shared network equipment, the gateway, or the internet service.", why: "A common failure can affect multiple devices simultaneously." },
  { q: "A computer can reach a website using its IP address but cannot open it using its domain name. Which service should you investigate?", hint: "Which service translates domain names into network addresses?", steps: "1. Recognize that IP connectivity works. 2. Investigate the process that resolves the domain name.", ans: "DNS.", why: "The symptoms suggest a name-resolution problem, although further testing is needed to confirm it." },
  { q: "Why should a technician change one setting at a time when troubleshooting?", hint: "Think about identifying the actual cause of improvement or failure.", steps: "1. Change one setting. 2. Test the connection. 3. Observe whether the result changes.", ans: "To identify which change caused the result and avoid introducing additional problems.", why: "Changing many settings at once makes it difficult to determine which change fixed or worsened the fault." }
]);

add("computer", "networking", "computer_networking_network_security_basics", "Basic Network Security", `
<h2>Basic Network Security</h2>
Network security protects networked devices, communications, and data from unauthorized access, misuse, damage, or disruption.

Common security measures include:
- Authentication: checking a user's identity before granting access.
- Access control: limiting what an authenticated user or device is allowed to do.
- Encryption: making information unreadable to parties without the required key.
- Firewalls: filtering network traffic according to configured rules.
- Software updates: correcting known vulnerabilities.
- Secure Wi-Fi: using modern encryption and a strong network password.

Users should avoid sharing passwords, opening suspicious attachments, and connecting to untrusted networks without appropriate protection. Organizations should apply suitable access permissions and maintain backups of important data.

No single measure guarantees complete security. Effective protection combines technical controls, responsible user behaviour, and regular maintenance.
`, [
  { q: "What is the main purpose of network security?", hint: "Think about protecting devices, communication, and information.", steps: "Identify the assets being protected and the threats being prevented.", ans: "To protect networked devices, communications, and data from unauthorized access, misuse, damage, or disruption.", why: "Networks can expose information and services to different threats." },
  { q: "How does authentication differ from access control?", hint: "One checks identity; the other determines permissions.", steps: "1. Define authentication. 2. Define access control. 3. Compare their roles.", ans: "Authentication checks who a user is; access control determines what that user may access or do.", why: "A verified identity does not automatically grant permission to every resource." },
  { q: "What is the purpose of a firewall?", hint: "Think about controlling incoming and outgoing traffic.", steps: "Recall that a firewall applies rules to network traffic.", ans: "To filter network traffic according to configured security rules.", why: "Traffic filtering can block unwanted connections while allowing permitted communication." },
  { q: "Why should network software and devices receive security updates?", hint: "Updates may address weaknesses discovered after release.", steps: "1. Recognize that software can contain vulnerabilities. 2. Explain how updates can correct known weaknesses.", ans: "Updates can fix known security vulnerabilities and improve protection.", why: "Unpatched vulnerabilities may be exploited by attackers." },
  { q: "A school uses a strong Wi-Fi password but never updates its router. Is its network fully secure?", hint: "Consider whether one security measure addresses every possible weakness.", steps: "1. Recognize the benefit of a strong password. 2. Consider vulnerabilities that updates may fix.", ans: "No. A strong password helps, but the router also needs updates and other appropriate security measures.", why: "Network security requires multiple controls; no single measure guarantees complete protection." }
]);
