add("computer", "programming", "computer_programming_processing_collection_values", "Processing a collection of values", `
<h2>Processing a collection of values</h2>
<p>A collection such as a list can store many related values. A loop allows a program to process each element without writing a separate instruction for every value.</p>
<pre><code>marks = [50, 60, 70]
total = 0

for mark in marks:
    total = total + mark

print(total)</code></pre>
<p>Output: <code>180</code></p>
<p>The loop visits each mark once and adds it to <code>total</code>. The variable <code>total</code> is an accumulator because it stores a running result.</p>
<p>To calculate an average, divide the total by the number of values. An empty collection must be handled before dividing by its length.</p>
<pre><code>if len(marks) > 0:
    average = total / len(marks)
    print(average)</code></pre>
`, [
{q:"Why are loops useful when processing a list?",hint:"Consider what happens when a list has many elements.",steps:"1. Identify the repeated operation. 2. Notice that it applies to each element. 3. Use a loop to repeat it.",ans:"A loop processes each element without requiring a separate instruction for every element.",why:"Loops reduce repetition and make collection processing easier to maintain."},
{q:"What is the output when marks = [10, 20, 30] and a loop adds every mark to total, initially 0?",hint:"Add all three values.",steps:"1. Start at 0. 2. Add 10. 3. Add 20. 4. Add 30.",ans:"60",why:"The accumulator stores the sum of all elements."},
{q:"What is an accumulator?",hint:"Think of a variable that keeps a running result.",steps:"1. Find the variable updated repeatedly. 2. Identify what it stores. 3. Explain its role.",ans:"A variable that collects a running result, such as a sum or count.",why:"Each loop iteration updates the accumulated value."},
{q:"Write a loop that prints every value in numbers = [4, 7, 9].",hint:"Use a for loop to visit each element.",steps:"1. Start a for loop over numbers. 2. Use a variable for each element. 3. Print that variable.",ans:"for number in numbers:\\n    print(number)",why:"The loop prints each list element in turn."},
{q:"Why should a program check that a list is not empty before calculating its average using total / len(marks)?",hint:"What is the length of an empty list?",steps:"1. An empty list has length 0. 2. Division by zero is invalid. 3. Check the length first.",ans:"To prevent division by zero when the list contains no values.",why:"The average is undefined when there are no values to average."}
]);

add("computer", "programming", "computer_programming_purpose_of_functions", "Purpose of functions", `
<h2>Purpose of functions</h2>
<p>A function is a named block of code that performs a particular task. A function can be called whenever its task is needed.</p>
<p>Functions help programmers reuse code, divide a large problem into smaller parts and make programs easier to test and maintain.</p>
<pre><code>def greet():
    print("Welcome to Tixar")

greet()
greet()</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>Welcome to Tixar
Welcome to Tixar</code></pre>
<p>The function is defined once but called twice. Defining a function does not normally execute its body; the function runs when it is called.</p>
<p>A function can accept inputs through parameters and can return a result to the code that called it.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a function in programming?",hint:"Think of a named block of instructions.",steps:"1. Identify the named code block. 2. Identify the task it performs. 3. State its purpose.",ans:"A named block of code designed to perform a particular task.",why:"A function groups instructions so they can be called when needed."},
{q:"Give two benefits of using functions.",hint:"Consider reuse and program organization.",steps:"1. Think about avoiding repeated code. 2. Think about dividing a large program into smaller parts.",ans:"Code reuse and improved program organization.",why:"Functions reduce duplication and make code easier to test and maintain."},
{q:"How many times does the body run when greet() is called twice?",hint:"Each function call runs the function body.",steps:"1. Count the calls. 2. Each call executes the body once.",ans:"Twice.",why:"A function definition does not run its body by itself, but each call does."},
{q:"What is the difference between defining and calling a function?",hint:"One creates the function; the other requests its execution.",steps:"1. Identify what def does. 2. Identify what the function name followed by parentheses does.",ans:"Defining specifies the function's code; calling executes that code.",why:"The program needs a call to run the function body."},
{q:"Why might a programmer create a function to calculate tax?",hint:"Consider where the same calculation may be needed.",steps:"1. Identify the repeated calculation. 2. Put it in one function. 3. Call it wherever required.",ans:"To reuse the tax calculation and keep the program organized.",why:"A single function can be reused and corrected in one place."}
]);

add("computer", "programming", "computer_programming_defining_a_function", "Defining a function", `
<h2>Defining a function</h2>
<p>In Python, the keyword <code>def</code> begins a function definition. It is followed by the function name, parentheses and a colon. The function body is indented.</p>
<pre><code>def calculate_square(number):
    result = number * number
    print(result)

calculate_square(4)</code></pre>
<p>Output: <code>16</code></p>
<p>The name is <code>calculate_square</code>. The parameter is <code>number</code>. The indented statements form the function body.</p>
<p><strong>Common error:</strong> forgetting the colon or indentation causes a syntax or indentation error.</p>
<pre><code>def say_hello()
    print("Hello")</code></pre>
<p>This code is incorrect because the function header is missing a colon.</p>
`, [
{q:"Which Python keyword begins a function definition?",hint:"It appears before the function name.",steps:"1. Look at a Python function header. 2. Identify the keyword before the name.",ans:"def",why:"Python uses def to define a function."},
{q:"What is wrong with def greet() followed by print('Hi') on the next line without indentation?",hint:"Check the structure of the function body.",steps:"1. Identify the function header. 2. Check the body indentation. 3. Indent the statement.",ans:"The function body is not indented.",why:"Python uses indentation to identify the statements inside a function."},
{q:"Write a function named show_message that prints 'Keep learning'.",hint:"Start with def and indent the print statement.",steps:"1. Write the function header. 2. Add parentheses and a colon. 3. Indent the print statement.",ans:"def show_message():\\n    print('Keep learning')",why:"The definition creates a function whose body prints the message when called."},
{q:"Does defining def double(n): return n * 2 automatically calculate a result?",hint:"Distinguish between creating and executing a function.",steps:"1. Identify the definition. 2. Check whether a call has been made.",ans:"No. The function must be called to execute its body.",why:"A function definition establishes the code but does not call it."},
{q:"What is the output of def square(n): print(n * n); square(3), assuming the statements are correctly placed on separate lines?",hint:"Substitute 3 for n.",steps:"1. The argument is 3. 2. Multiply 3 by itself. 3. Print the result.",ans:"9",why:"The function receives 3 and prints its square."}
]);

add("computer", "programming", "computer_programming_parameters_and_arguments", "Parameters and arguments", `
<h2>Parameters and arguments</h2>
<p>A <strong>parameter</strong> is a variable listed in a function definition. An <strong>argument</strong> is a value supplied when the function is called.</p>
<pre><code>def greet(name):
    print("Hello", name)

greet("Amina")</code></pre>
<p>Output: <code>Hello Amina</code></p>
<p>In this example, <code>name</code> is the parameter and <code>"Amina"</code> is the argument. The argument's value is made available through the parameter during the call.</p>
<p>A function can have multiple parameters. The arguments must be supplied in a way that matches the function's requirements.</p>
<pre><code>def add(a, b):
    print(a + b)

add(4, 6)</code></pre>
<p>Output: <code>10</code></p>
`, [
{q:"What is a parameter?",hint:"Look at the function definition.",steps:"1. Examine the parentheses after the function name. 2. Identify the variable listed there.",ans:"A variable listed in a function definition that receives a value when the function is called.",why:"Parameters allow functions to work with supplied values."},
{q:"In def greet(name): print(name), what is name?",hint:"It appears in the definition.",steps:"1. Find the function definition. 2. Inspect the name inside its parentheses.",ans:"A parameter.",why:"The variable name is declared in the function header."},
{q:"In greet('Amina'), what is 'Amina'?",hint:"It is supplied in the call.",steps:"1. Identify the function call. 2. Find the value inside its parentheses.",ans:"An argument.",why:"An argument is the value passed to a function when calling it."},
{q:"What does add(4, 6) print if add is defined as def add(a, b): print(a + b)?",hint:"Match 4 with a and 6 with b.",steps:"1. Set a to 4. 2. Set b to 6. 3. Add them.",ans:"10",why:"The function adds the two argument values."},
{q:"What problem occurs if a function requires two arguments but is called with only one?",hint:"Check whether every required parameter receives a value.",steps:"1. Count the required parameters. 2. Count the supplied arguments. 3. Compare the counts.",ans:"Python raises a TypeError because a required argument is missing.",why:"The function call does not supply all required inputs."}
]);

add("computer", "programming", "computer_programming_return_values", "Return values", `
<h2>Return values</h2>
<p>A return value is the result a function sends back to the code that called it. In Python, the <code>return</code> keyword sends the result back.</p>
<pre><code>def add(a, b):
    return a + b

answer = add(4, 5)
print(answer)</code></pre>
<p>Output: <code>9</code></p>
<p>The expression <code>a + b</code> is evaluated, and its result is returned. The calling code stores that result in <code>answer</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Printing is not the same as returning.</strong> <code>print()</code> displays information. <code>return</code> provides a result that other code can use.</p>
<pre><code>def add_and_print(a, b):
    print(a + b)

result = add_and_print(2, 3)
print(result)</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>5
None</code></pre>
`, [
{q:"What is a return value?",hint:"It is sent from a function back to its caller.",steps:"1. Identify what return does. 2. Explain how the calling code can use the result.",ans:"A result that a function sends back to the code that called it.",why:"Returning allows a result to be stored, compared or used in another calculation."},
{q:"What does def multiply(a, b): return a * b produce when called as multiply(3, 4)?",hint:"Substitute the arguments into the expression.",steps:"1. Set a to 3. 2. Set b to 4. 3. Multiply them.",ans:"12",why:"The function returns the product of its arguments."},
{q:"What is the difference between print(5) and return 5 inside a function?",hint:"One displays a value; the other sends a result back.",steps:"1. Identify the effect of print. 2. Identify the effect of return.",ans:"print(5) displays 5; return 5 sends the value 5 back to the caller.",why:"Displayed output and a function's returned result serve different purposes."},
{q:"What is printed by def get_value(): return 7; value = get_value(); print(value), with each statement correctly placed on separate lines?",hint:"The returned value is assigned to value.",steps:"1. Call get_value. 2. Receive 7. 3. Print the variable.",ans:"7",why:"The function returns 7 and the caller stores it."},
{q:"Why does a function that only prints a number normally return None when its result is assigned to a variable?",hint:"Check whether the function contains a return statement.",steps:"1. Find the print statement. 2. Look for return. 3. Recall Python's default return behavior.",ans:"Because printing does not return the printed number; without a return value, the function returns None.",why:"A function must return a value explicitly if the caller needs that value as a result."}
]);

add("computer", "programming", "computer_programming_reusing_code", "Reusing code", `
<h2>Reusing code</h2>
<p>Code reuse means using an existing function or code block again instead of rewriting the same instructions. Functions are one of the main ways to reuse code.</p>
<pre><code>def calculate_total(price, quantity):
    return price * quantity

first_total = calculate_total(100, 2)
second_total = calculate_total(50, 3)

print(first_total)
print(second_total)</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>200
150</code></pre>
<p>The same function handles different inputs. If the calculation needs correction, the programmer can update the function once rather than changing multiple copies of the calculation.</p>
<p>Reusable code should have a clear purpose, suitable inputs and predictable results. Reuse does not mean copying code without checking whether it fits the new task.</p>
`, [
{q:"What does code reuse mean?",hint:"Think about using an existing solution again.",steps:"1. Identify code that already performs a task. 2. Use it again rather than rewriting it.",ans:"Using existing code again for the same task or a suitable related task.",why:"Reuse reduces duplication and makes maintenance easier."},
{q:"Why is a function useful when the same calculation is needed in ten places?",hint:"Consider what happens if the calculation changes.",steps:"1. Put the calculation in one function. 2. Call it in all ten places. 3. Correct the function once if necessary.",ans:"The calculation can be maintained in one place and called wherever needed.",why:"A single shared implementation reduces duplicated logic and inconsistent fixes."},
{q:"What is printed when calculate_total(100, 2) returns price * quantity?",hint:"Multiply the two arguments.",steps:"1. Set price to 100. 2. Set quantity to 2. 3. Multiply.",ans:"200",why:"The same reusable function can calculate totals for different inputs."},
{q:"What is a risk of copying the same calculation into many parts of a program?",hint:"Think about correcting an error consistently.",steps:"1. Imagine the formula changes. 2. Find every copied version. 3. Consider missing one during an update.",ans:"Some copies may remain incorrect or become inconsistent when the calculation changes.",why:"Centralizing repeated logic in a function makes changes easier to apply consistently."},
{q:"What makes a function suitable for reuse?",hint:"Think about its purpose and inputs.",steps:"1. Check that it performs a clear task. 2. Check that inputs are appropriate. 3. Check that its result or behavior is predictable.",ans:"It has a clear purpose, appropriate inputs and predictable behavior.",why:"Clear, focused functions are easier to call correctly and maintain."}
]);

add("computer", "programming", "computer_programming_syntax_errors", "Syntax errors", `
<h2>Syntax errors</h2>
<p>A syntax error occurs when code breaks the rules of the programming language. Python may be unable to parse the code and will report an error before the affected code can run normally.</p>
<p><strong>Incorrect code:</strong></p>
<pre><code>age = 16
if age >= 18
    print("Adult")</code></pre>
<p>The colon is missing after the condition.</p>
<p><strong>Corrected code:</strong></p>
<pre><code>age = 16
if age >= 18:
    print("Adult")</code></pre>
<p>Other common causes include unmatched parentheses, missing quotation marks and incorrect indentation. Read the error message and inspect the reported line and nearby lines.</p>
<p>Fixing a syntax error does not guarantee the program's logic is correct; the program must still be tested.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a syntax error?",hint:"It involves the rules used to write valid code.",steps:"1. Consider the programming language's grammar. 2. Identify code that breaks a rule.",ans:"An error caused by code that does not follow the programming language's syntax rules.",why:"The interpreter must parse valid syntax before it can execute the affected code."},
{q:"Find the syntax error in if score > 50 print('Pass').",hint:"Check the structure of an if statement in Python.",steps:"1. Identify the condition. 2. Check what must follow it. 3. Separate the body onto an indented line.",ans:"The colon is missing after the condition, and the body should be on an indented line.",why:"Python requires a colon after an if condition and an indented body."},
{q:"Correct this line: print('Hello).",hint:"Check whether the quotation marks match.",steps:"1. Find the opening quote. 2. Add the matching closing quote. 3. Keep the closing parenthesis.",ans:"print('Hello')",why:"The string must have matching quotation marks."},
{q:"Why can missing indentation cause a syntax-related error in Python?",hint:"Indentation defines code blocks.",steps:"1. Identify a statement that belongs inside a function or condition. 2. Check its indentation. 3. Indent it consistently.",ans:"Python uses indentation to mark blocks, so incorrect indentation can make the program invalid.",why:"Indentation is part of Python's syntax, not merely visual formatting."},
{q:"After fixing a syntax error, why should a program still be tested?",hint:"Valid syntax does not prove that the solution is correct.",steps:"1. Confirm that the code can run. 2. Check the result against expected behavior.",ans:"The program may still contain runtime errors or logic errors.",why:"Syntax checking verifies language structure, not whether the program solves the intended problem."}
]);

add("computer", "programming", "computer_programming_runtime_errors", "Runtime errors", `
<h2>Runtime errors</h2>
<p>A runtime error occurs while a program is executing. The code may have valid syntax but encounter a situation that prevents an operation from completing.</p>
<p><strong>Example: division by zero</strong></p>
<pre><code>number = 10
divisor = 0
result = number / divisor
print(result)</code></pre>
<p>This raises a <code>ZeroDivisionError</code> when the division is attempted.</p>
<p><strong>Another example: invalid list index</strong></p>
<pre><code>items = [10, 20]
print(items[5])</code></pre>
<p>This raises an <code>IndexError</code> because index 5 does not exist.</p>
<p>Prevent runtime errors by checking input and conditions before performing risky operations. Exception handling can be used when a program should respond safely to an operation that fails.</p>
<pre><code>divisor = 0

if divisor != 0:
    print(10 / divisor)
else:
    print("Cannot divide by zero")</code></pre>
`, [
{q:"What is a runtime error?",hint:"It occurs during execution rather than because the code cannot be parsed.",steps:"1. Confirm that the program starts running. 2. Identify an operation that fails during execution.",ans:"An error that occurs while a program is running.",why:"Runtime errors arise when an executed operation cannot be completed."},
{q:"What error occurs when Python evaluates 10 / 0?",hint:"Consider whether division by zero is allowed.",steps:"1. Identify the operation. 2. Check the divisor. 3. Name the Python exception.",ans:"ZeroDivisionError",why:"Python does not allow division by zero."},
{q:"Why does print(items[5]) fail when items = [10, 20]?",hint:"List indexes start at zero.",steps:"1. List the valid indexes. 2. Compare them with 5.",ans:"It raises an IndexError because only indexes 0 and 1 exist.",why:"The program attempts to access a position outside the list."},
{q:"Give one way to prevent division by zero when using a variable divisor.",hint:"Check the divisor before dividing.",steps:"1. Test whether divisor equals zero. 2. Divide only if it is not zero.",ans:"Use a condition such as if divisor != 0 before performing the division.",why:"Checking the condition prevents the invalid operation."},
{q:"How is a runtime error different from a syntax error?",hint:"Compare when the failure occurs.",steps:"1. Syntax errors violate language rules. 2. Runtime errors occur during execution.",ans:"A syntax error prevents code from being parsed correctly; a runtime error occurs while the program executes.",why:"The two error types happen at different stages and need different debugging approaches."}
]);

add("computer", "programming", "computer_programming_logic_errors", "Logic errors", `
<h2>Logic errors</h2>
<p>A logic error occurs when a program runs but produces an incorrect result because its instructions do not correctly represent the intended solution.</p>
<p><strong>Incorrect code:</strong></p>
<pre><code>length = 5
width = 3
area = length + width
print(area)</code></pre>
<p>The program runs, but it adds the dimensions instead of multiplying them.</p>
<p><strong>Corrected code:</strong></p>
<pre><code>length = 5
width = 3
area = length * width
print(area)</code></pre>
<p>Output: <code>15</code></p>
<p>Logic errors can be found by testing known cases, tracing variable values and comparing actual output with expected output. A program can have no syntax errors and still contain logic errors.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a logic error?",hint:"The program runs but the result is wrong.",steps:"1. Check whether execution completes. 2. Compare the output with the expected result. 3. Identify incorrect reasoning or instructions.",ans:"An error in the program's reasoning or instructions that causes incorrect behavior or results.",why:"Logic errors are not necessarily detected by the interpreter."},
{q:"A program calculates rectangle area using length + width. What is wrong?",hint:"Recall the formula for the area of a rectangle.",steps:"1. Identify the intended quantity. 2. Recall area = length × width. 3. Replace addition with multiplication.",ans:"It uses addition instead of multiplication; area should be length * width.",why:"The formula in the code does not match the mathematical definition of area."},
{q:"A program runs without errors but gives 12 when the expected answer is 20. What type of error may be present?",hint:"The code executes but its result is incorrect.",steps:"1. Rule out a failure to execute. 2. Compare actual and expected outputs. 3. Inspect the calculation and conditions.",ans:"A logic error may be present.",why:"Incorrect output despite successful execution is a common sign of a logic error."},
{q:"How can a programmer investigate a logic error?",hint:"Compare actual behavior with expected behavior.",steps:"1. Choose a test input. 2. Predict the correct output. 3. Run the program. 4. Trace values and compare.",ans:"Use test cases, trace variable values and compare actual results with expected results.",why:"Tracing and testing help reveal where the program's reasoning goes wrong."},
{q:"What is printed by this code, and what is the likely logic error? x = 8; y = 2; result = x / y; print(result), when the intended operation was subtraction.",hint:"Work out the operator actually used.",steps:"1. Evaluate 8 divided by 2. 2. Compare division with the intended subtraction.",ans:"4. The code uses division instead of subtraction; the intended result is 6.",why:"The program executes valid code but uses the wrong operation for the task."}
]);

add("computer", "programming", "computer_programming_tracing_execution", "Tracing program execution", `
<h2>Tracing program execution</h2>
<p>Tracing means following a program statement by statement and recording how variables and output change. A trace table helps reveal errors in calculations, conditions and loops.</p>
<pre><code>x = 2
y = 3
x = x + y
y = x * 2
print(x, y)</code></pre>
<p>Trace the assignments in order:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Statement</th><th>x</th><th>y</th></tr>
<tr><td>Initial values</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>x = x + y</td><td>5</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>y = x * 2</td><td>5</td><td>10</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Output: <code>5 10</code></p>
<p>Always apply assignments in their execution order. When a variable is updated, later statements use its new value, not its previous value.</p>
`, [
{q:"What does tracing a program mean?",hint:"Follow statements in execution order.",steps:"1. Start with initial values. 2. Apply each statement. 3. Record changes and output.",ans:"Following a program statement by statement to determine variable changes and output.",why:"Tracing makes the program's execution visible and helps identify errors."},
{q:"Trace x = 4; x = x + 3; print(x). What is printed?",hint:"Update x before printing it.",steps:"1. Start with x = 4. 2. Add 3. 3. Print the updated value.",ans:"7",why:"The assignment changes x from 4 to 7."},
{q:"Given a = 2; b = 5; a = b; b = a; print(a, b), what is the output?",hint:"Each assignment uses the current value on its right-hand side.",steps:"1. Set a to 2 and b to 5. 2. Set a to the current b. 3. Set b to the current a.",ans:"5 5",why:"After a = b, both variables hold 5, so the next assignment leaves both at 5."},
{q:"What is the purpose of a trace table?",hint:"Think about recording values after statements.",steps:"1. Identify the variables. 2. Record their initial values. 3. Update the table after each relevant statement.",ans:"To record how variables change as a program executes.",why:"A trace table helps check expected output and find where an incorrect value appears."},
{q:"Trace total = 0; for n in [2, 4, 6]: total = total + n. What is the final total?",hint:"Add each list element to the running total.",steps:"1. Start at 0. 2. Add 2. 3. Add 4. 4. Add 6.",ans:"12",why:"The accumulator progresses through 2, 6 and 12."}
]);

add("computer", "programming", "computer_programming_correcting_program_errors", "Correcting program errors", `
<h2>Correcting program errors</h2>
<p>Debugging is the process of identifying, investigating and correcting errors in a program. First reproduce the problem, then gather evidence, locate the cause, make a targeted change and test again.</p>
<p><strong>Buggy code:</strong></p>
<pre><code>marks = [40, 60, 80]
total = 0

for mark in marks
    total = total + mark

average = total / 2
print(average)</code></pre>
<p>This code has two errors: the loop header is missing a colon, and the average is divided by 2 even though there are three marks.</p>
<p><strong>Corrected code:</strong></p>
<pre><code>marks = [40, 60, 80]
total = 0

for mark in marks:
    total = total + mark

average = total / len(marks)
print(average)</code></pre>
<p>Output: <code>60.0</code></p>
<p>After correcting an error, rerun the program using the original test case and other useful cases. A fix should solve the diagnosed problem without creating another one.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is debugging?",hint:"It involves finding and correcting problems in code.",steps:"1. Reproduce the problem. 2. Investigate the cause. 3. Correct it. 4. Test again.",ans:"The process of identifying, investigating and correcting program errors.",why:"Debugging improves a program by fixing the causes of incorrect behavior."},
{q:"Find the syntax error in for n in [1, 2, 3] followed by an indented print(n).",hint:"Check the loop header.",steps:"1. Identify the for statement. 2. Check its ending punctuation. 3. Add the required colon.",ans:"The loop header is missing a colon: for n in [1, 2, 3]:",why:"Python requires a colon before the indented loop body."},
{q:"A program sums three marks correctly but divides the total by 2 to find the average. What should change?",hint:"Average equals total divided by the number of values.",steps:"1. Count the marks. 2. Identify the incorrect divisor. 3. Divide by the number of marks.",ans:"Divide by 3, or use total / len(marks).",why:"The average must use the count of values being averaged."},
{q:"Why should a program be tested again after a bug is corrected?",hint:"A change can fix one problem but introduce another.",steps:"1. Run the original failing test. 2. Check the expected result. 3. Try other relevant inputs.",ans:"To verify that the fix works and has not introduced another error.",why:"Retesting provides evidence that the corrected program behaves as intended."},
{q:"What is a sensible first step when a program produces an unexpected result?",hint:"You need a repeatable example of the problem.",steps:"1. Record the input and actual output. 2. Determine the expected output. 3. Reproduce the issue before changing code.",ans:"Reproduce the problem with a known input and compare actual output with expected output.",why:"A repeatable failure gives evidence for diagnosing the actual cause instead of guessing."}
]);
add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_meaning_of_a_database", "Meaning of a database", `
<h2>Meaning of a database</h2>
<p>A database is an organised collection of related data stored so that it can be accessed, managed, updated and retrieved efficiently.</p>
<p>For example, a school may keep student names, admission numbers, classes and marks in a student database.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Admission No.</th><th>Name</th><th>Class</th></tr>
<tr><td>101</td><td>Amina</td><td>Form 2</td></tr>
<tr><td>102</td><td>Brian</td><td>Form 3</td></tr>
<tr><td>103</td><td>Chebet</td><td>Form 2</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The information is organised in a structured way, making it easier to locate a particular student or update a class record.</p>
<h3>Characteristics of a database</h3>
<ul>
<li>Stores related data.</li>
<li>Organises data for easier access.</li>
<li>Allows authorised users to retrieve and modify data.</li>
<li>Can support many records and different types of information.</li>
</ul>
<p>A database is not necessarily a single table or a particular software application. It is the organised collection of data itself.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a database?",hint:"Think about how related data is collected and organised.",steps:"1. Identify what is stored. 2. Explain how it is organised. 3. State why it is organised.",ans:"An organised collection of related data that can be accessed, managed and retrieved.",why:"Organisation makes stored data useful and easier to manage."},
{q:"Give one example of data that could be stored in a school database.",hint:"Think about information schools manage.",steps:"1. Identify a school activity. 2. Identify the information needed for it.",ans:"Student admission numbers, names, classes or examination marks.",why:"These are related records that a school may need to retrieve and update."},
{q:"Why is a list of student names with admission numbers more useful when properly organised?",hint:"Consider finding a particular student.",steps:"1. Identify the stored information. 2. Consider how organisation helps users locate records.",ans:"It makes student records easier to find, manage and update.",why:"Organised data supports efficient retrieval and maintenance."},
{q:"Is a database necessarily just one table?",hint:"Distinguish the collection of data from one way of organising it.",steps:"1. A table stores related records. 2. A database can contain several related tables.",ans:"No. A database can contain one or more tables and other organised data structures.",why:"A table is one possible component of a database."},
{q:"A school stores student names, admission numbers and classes in an organised collection. Why does this qualify as a database?",hint:"Relate the example to the definition.",steps:"1. Identify the related data. 2. Identify its organisation. 3. Explain how it can be used.",ans:"It stores related student data in an organised form for retrieval and management.",why:"The data is related and organised to support useful operations."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_meaning_of_a_database_management_system", "Meaning of a database management system", `
<h2>Meaning of a database management system</h2>
<p>A database management system (DBMS) is software used to create, organise, maintain and control access to databases.</p>
<p>The database contains the data; the DBMS is the software that helps users and applications work with that data.</p>
<h3>Examples of DBMS software</h3>
<ul>
<li>MySQL</li>
<li>Microsoft Access</li>
<li>PostgreSQL</li>
<li>SQLite</li>
<li>Oracle Database</li>
</ul>
<h3>Functions of a DBMS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Create and structure databases.</li>
<li>Insert, retrieve, update and delete records.</li>
<li>Control user access and permissions.</li>
<li>Help maintain data accuracy and consistency.</li>
<li>Support backup and recovery features.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> A school may use a DBMS to create a student database and retrieve the marks of a particular student.</p>
<p>A DBMS is different from a database: one is the software used to manage data, while the other is the organised data being managed.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a DBMS?",hint:"It is the software used to work with a database.",steps:"1. Identify the role of software. 2. Explain how it manages stored data.",ans:"Software used to create, manage, maintain and control access to databases.",why:"A DBMS provides tools for working with organised data."},
{q:"State two functions of a DBMS.",hint:"Think about data operations and protection.",steps:"1. Identify an operation performed on records. 2. Identify a management or security function.",ans:"Any two: creating tables, retrieving records, updating data, deleting records, controlling access, or supporting backup and recovery.",why:"A DBMS supports both data management and controlled use."},
{q:"What is the difference between a database and a DBMS?",hint:"One is the data; the other is the software.",steps:"1. Define the database. 2. Define the DBMS. 3. Compare their roles.",ans:"A database is an organised collection of data; a DBMS is software used to manage that data.",why:"The stored information and the software managing it are different things."},
{q:"Name one example of a DBMS.",hint:"Recall software used to create and manage databases.",steps:"1. Identify a database software product. 2. Give its name.",ans:"MySQL, Microsoft Access, PostgreSQL, SQLite or Oracle Database.",why:"These are examples of database management software."},
{q:"A school wants to restrict who can view student records. How can a DBMS help?",hint:"Consider permissions and authorised users.",steps:"1. Identify the need to restrict access. 2. Use database user accounts or permissions.",ans:"It can control access by assigning permissions to authorised users.",why:"Access controls help protect records from unauthorised viewing or changes."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_uses_of_databases", "Uses of databases", `
<h2>Uses of databases</h2>
<p>Databases are used wherever organisations need to store, organise, retrieve and update related information.</p>
<h3>Common uses</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Education:</strong> student details, attendance, marks and enrolment.</li>
<li><strong>Banking:</strong> customer accounts, transactions and balances.</li>
<li><strong>Healthcare:</strong> patient details, appointments and treatment records.</li>
<li><strong>Retail:</strong> products, stock levels, sales and customer orders.</li>
<li><strong>Transport:</strong> bookings, routes and passenger information.</li>
<li><strong>Government:</strong> registrations, licences and service records.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example: shop stock</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Code</th><th>Product</th><th>Quantity</th></tr>
<tr><td>P01</td><td>Exercise book</td><td>120</td></tr>
<tr><td>P02</td><td>Pen</td><td>85</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A shop can use this information to check stock, update quantities after sales and identify products that need restocking.</p>
<p>The type of data stored depends on the organisation's purpose. Access to sensitive records should be limited to authorised users.</p>
`, [
{q:"Give two uses of databases in education.",hint:"Think about information a school must maintain.",steps:"1. Identify student-related information. 2. Name two kinds of records.",ans:"Managing student details, attendance, examination marks, enrolment or class lists.",why:"Schools need to maintain and retrieve these records regularly."},
{q:"How can a retail shop use a database?",hint:"Consider products and sales.",steps:"1. Identify information about products. 2. Explain how it supports stock management.",ans:"To record products and quantities, update stock after sales and identify items to restock.",why:"The database helps the shop monitor and manage inventory."},
{q:"Why might a hospital use a database?",hint:"Think about patient information and appointments.",steps:"1. Identify patient-related records. 2. Explain how staff use them.",ans:"To organise patient details, appointments and treatment records for authorised staff.",why:"Organised records support patient administration and continuity of care."},
{q:"A bank needs to check a customer's account balance. How can a database help?",hint:"Consider retrieving and updating transaction records.",steps:"1. Locate the customer's account. 2. Retrieve the relevant records. 3. Use the recorded transactions to determine the balance.",ans:"It stores account and transaction records that can be retrieved to determine the balance.",why:"Databases support accurate access to large collections of financial records."},
{q:"Why should access to sensitive database records be restricted?",hint:"Consider privacy and unauthorised changes.",steps:"1. Identify the sensitive information. 2. Identify possible misuse. 3. Restrict access to authorised users.",ans:"To protect privacy and prevent unauthorised viewing, disclosure or modification.",why:"Access controls reduce the risk of misuse of sensitive information."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_advantages_of_databases", "Advantages of databases", `
<h2>Advantages of databases</h2>
<p>Databases provide an organised way to manage information. Compared with poorly organised or duplicated records, a well-designed database can improve access, consistency and control.</p>
<h3>Main advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fast retrieval:</strong> users can search for particular records.</li>
<li><strong>Reduced duplication:</strong> good design can reduce repeated storage of the same facts.</li>
<li><strong>Improved consistency:</strong> updates can be managed so related records remain accurate.</li>
<li><strong>Data sharing:</strong> authorised users can work with the same managed information.</li>
<li><strong>Security:</strong> permissions can restrict access.</li>
<li><strong>Backup and recovery:</strong> supported tools can help restore data after loss.</li>
<li><strong>Better reporting:</strong> stored records can be searched, sorted and summarised.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>If a student's contact details change, a well-designed school database can allow the authorised user to update the relevant record rather than correcting many separate lists.</p>
<p>These benefits depend on good design, correct data entry, appropriate permissions and regular maintenance. A database does not automatically guarantee accurate data.</p>
`, [
{q:"State three advantages of using a database.",hint:"Think about retrieval, accuracy and protection.",steps:"1. Identify how data is accessed. 2. Identify how duplication can be reduced. 3. Identify a security or recovery benefit.",ans:"Any three: faster retrieval, reduced duplication, improved consistency, controlled sharing, access security, backup and recovery, or easier reporting.",why:"Databases provide structured tools for managing and using information."},
{q:"How can a database reduce data duplication?",hint:"Think about storing a fact once and linking related records.",steps:"1. Identify a fact repeated in several lists. 2. Store it in a suitable place. 3. Link related records where appropriate.",ans:"A well-designed database can store a fact once and link other records to it instead of repeatedly copying it.",why:"Reducing unnecessary copies lowers the risk of inconsistent information."},
{q:"Why does controlled access count as an advantage?",hint:"Consider who should view or change records.",steps:"1. Identify sensitive information. 2. Apply user permissions. 3. Allow only authorised actions.",ans:"It helps prevent unauthorised viewing or modification of records.",why:"Permission controls support the confidentiality and integrity of stored data."},
{q:"Does using a database automatically make every stored record correct?",hint:"Consider errors made during data entry.",steps:"1. A database stores what is entered. 2. Incorrect input can still be stored. 3. Validation and checking are needed.",ans:"No. Incorrect data can still be entered; validation and proper maintenance are needed.",why:"A database can help manage data but cannot guarantee correctness without suitable controls."},
{q:"A school keeps several separate copies of the same student's phone number. What database advantage could help reduce conflicting copies?",hint:"Think about unnecessary repetition of data.",steps:"1. Identify the duplicated information. 2. Consider a central, well-designed record. 3. Explain how fewer copies improve consistency.",ans:"Reduced data duplication and improved consistency.",why:"Managing a fact in one suitable place reduces the chance of different copies disagreeing."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_file_based_systems_versus_databases", "File-based systems versus databases", `
<h2>File-based systems versus databases</h2>
<p>A file-based system stores information in separate files managed by individual applications or users. A database system uses a structured collection of data managed through a DBMS.</p>
<h3>File-based example</h3>
<p>A school keeps student names in one spreadsheet, marks in another file and fee records in a separate document. The same student's details may be repeated in several places.</p>
<h3>Database example</h3>
<p>A school uses a database with related tables for students, classes, marks and fee records. Records can be linked using suitable identifiers.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>File-based system</th><th>Database system</th></tr>
<tr><td>Data organisation</td><td>Often separated across files</td><td>Managed in a structured database</td></tr>
<tr><td>Duplication</td><td>May be high when data is repeated</td><td>Can be reduced through good design</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sharing</td><td>May require separate copies</td><td>Can support controlled shared access</td></tr>
<tr><td>Control</td><td>Depends on the files and applications</td><td>DBMS can provide central rules and permissions</td></tr>
</table>
<p>File-based systems can be suitable for simple tasks. Database systems become especially useful when data is large, related, shared or frequently updated.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a file-based system?",hint:"Think about information stored in separate files.",steps:"1. Identify where the information is stored. 2. Explain how the files are managed.",ans:"A system in which data is stored in separate files managed by applications or users.",why:"Each file may be managed separately rather than through one integrated database system."},
{q:"Give one difference between a file-based system and a database system.",hint:"Compare how related data is organised.",steps:"1. Describe separate files. 2. Describe data managed through a DBMS.",ans:"A file-based system often stores data in separate files, while a database system organises data in a database managed by a DBMS.",why:"The database system is designed to manage related data in a more integrated way."},
{q:"Why can separate files lead to inconsistent student details?",hint:"Consider updating repeated copies.",steps:"1. Find a student's details in multiple files. 2. Change only one copy. 3. Compare the copies.",ans:"Some copies may be updated while others remain unchanged.",why:"Repeated information can become inconsistent when updates are not applied everywhere."},
{q:"When might a file-based system be adequate?",hint:"Think about a small, simple task with little sharing.",steps:"1. Consider the amount of data. 2. Consider how many people use it. 3. Assess whether complex relationships are needed.",ans:"For a simple task with a small amount of data and limited sharing or relationships.",why:"A database system may not be necessary for every small task."},
{q:"A school needs several departments to share student records and update them consistently. Which system is generally more suitable, and why?",hint:"Think about shared access and related records.",steps:"1. Identify the need for shared data. 2. Identify the risk of separate copies. 3. Choose a system that manages related records centrally.",ans:"A database system, because a DBMS can support controlled shared access and more consistent management of related records.",why:"A well-designed shared database can reduce duplicated copies and simplify coordinated updates."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_tables", "Tables", `
<h2>Tables</h2>
<p>A table is a database structure that organises related data into rows and columns. In a relational database, each table usually represents one type of entity, such as students, books or products.</p>
<h3>Example: Students table</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>AdmissionNo</th><th>Name</th><th>Class</th></tr>
<tr><td>201</td><td>Wanjiku</td><td>Form 1</td></tr>
<tr><td>202</td><td>Otieno</td><td>Form 2</td></tr>
<tr><td>203</td><td>Njeri</td><td>Form 1</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The column headings describe the information stored. Each row contains the values for one student.</p>
<h3>Good table design</h3>
<ul>
<li>Give each table a clear purpose.</li>
<li>Use meaningful field names.</li>
<li>Store one kind of information in each field.</li>
<li>Use a suitable key to identify records uniquely.</li>
<li>Avoid unnecessary repetition of the same facts.</li>
</ul>
<p>A database can contain multiple tables connected through keys. For example, a Students table and a Marks table may be linked using a student identifier.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a table in a relational database?",hint:"It organises related data into rows and columns.",steps:"1. Identify the columns. 2. Identify the rows. 3. Explain how the data is organised.",ans:"A structure that stores related data in rows and columns.",why:"Tables provide a structured way to represent records and their fields."},
{q:"In a Students table, what does a row usually represent?",hint:"Look across one row of values.",steps:"1. Identify the values across the row. 2. Relate them to one student.",ans:"One student record.",why:"A row groups the values describing one record."},
{q:"What do the columns of a table represent?",hint:"Consider headings such as Name and Class.",steps:"1. Inspect the column headings. 2. Identify the kind of value stored under each heading.",ans:"Fields or attributes describing the records, such as Name or Class.",why:"Each column stores a particular type of information for the table's records."},
{q:"Why should a Students table have a clear purpose?",hint:"Think about organising and maintaining records.",steps:"1. Decide what the table represents. 2. Include fields relevant to that purpose.",ans:"A clear purpose helps keep the table organised and prevents unrelated information from being mixed together.",why:"Purposeful table design makes data easier to understand and maintain."},
{q:"A school wants to store student details and examination marks separately but link them. How can tables help?",hint:"Consider one table for each kind of record.",steps:"1. Create a Students table. 2. Create a Marks table. 3. Use a suitable student identifier to link them.",ans:"Use separate related tables, linked by a student identifier or key.",why:"Related tables organise different kinds of information while allowing them to be associated."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_records", "Records", `
<h2>Records</h2>
<p>A record is a collection of related field values describing one item, person, event or entity. In a relational database table, a record is represented by a row.</p>
<h3>Example: Students table</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>AdmissionNo</th><th>Name</th><th>Class</th></tr>
<tr><td>301</td><td>Kamau</td><td>Form 2</td></tr>
<tr><td>302</td><td>Achieng</td><td>Form 3</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The row containing <code>301, Kamau, Form 2</code> is one record. It groups the admission number, name and class belonging to the same student.</p>
<p>Records can be inserted, retrieved, updated or deleted as part of database operations.</p>
<h3>Important distinction</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Table:</strong> the structure containing related records.</li>
<li><strong>Record:</strong> one row describing an item.</li>
<li><strong>Field:</strong> one category of data within a record.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each record should be distinguishable from other records, often using a primary key.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a record in a database table?",hint:"Think of one complete row of related values.",steps:"1. Identify a row. 2. Identify the values belonging to one item. 3. Explain what they describe.",ans:"A collection of related field values describing one item, represented by a row in a relational table.",why:"The record groups information belonging to the same entity."},
{q:"In a Students table, what does one row containing admission number, name and class represent?",hint:"All the values belong to one person.",steps:"1. Read the values across the row. 2. Identify the entity they describe.",ans:"One student record.",why:"The row groups the details of a single student."},
{q:"What is the difference between a record and a table?",hint:"One is a row; the other contains related rows.",steps:"1. Define the table. 2. Define the record. 3. Compare their roles.",ans:"A table stores related records; a record is one row within that table.",why:"A table contains multiple records organised using common fields."},
{q:"What might happen when a record is updated?",hint:"Consider changing a field value.",steps:"1. Identify the record. 2. Select a field. 3. Replace its existing value with a new one.",ans:"One or more field values in that record are changed.",why:"Updating changes stored values without necessarily creating a new record."},
{q:"Why is a unique identifier useful for records?",hint:"Consider two students with the same name.",steps:"1. Imagine duplicate names. 2. Use a unique identifier. 3. Distinguish the records reliably.",ans:"It allows each record to be identified unambiguously, even when other values are similar.",why:"A suitable unique identifier prevents confusion between records."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_fields", "Fields", `
<h2>Fields</h2>
<p>A field is a named category of data in a database table. Each field is represented by a column in a relational table.</p>
<h3>Example: Students table</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>AdmissionNo</th><th>Name</th><th>DateOfBirth</th><th>Class</th></tr>
<tr><td>401</td><td>Nyambura</td><td>2010-04-12</td><td>Form 2</td></tr>
<tr><td>402</td><td>Barasa</td><td>2009-11-25</td><td>Form 3</td></tr>
</table>
<p><code>Name</code>, <code>DateOfBirth</code> and <code>Class</code> are examples of fields. Each field stores one kind of information for every record.</p>
<h3>Choosing fields</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use names that clearly describe the data.</li>
<li>Choose a suitable data type for each field.</li>
<li>Avoid combining unrelated values in one field.</li>
<li>Include only fields needed for the table's purpose.</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, storing a student's first name and date of birth in one field makes searching and updating each value more difficult than storing them in separate fields.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a field in a database?",hint:"Think of a named column in a table.",steps:"1. Identify a column heading. 2. Determine the kind of data it stores.",ans:"A named category of data in a table, represented by a column.",why:"A field defines one type of information recorded for each record."},
{q:"In a table with columns AdmissionNo, Name and Class, name two fields.",hint:"The column headings are the fields.",steps:"1. Read the headings. 2. Select any two.",ans:"Any two of AdmissionNo, Name and Class.",why:"Each heading identifies a field."},
{q:"Why should field names be meaningful?",hint:"Think about how users understand the stored data.",steps:"1. Identify the purpose of the field. 2. Choose a name that describes its contents.",ans:"Meaningful names make the table easier to understand, use and maintain.",why:"Clear names reduce confusion about what each field stores."},
{q:"Why is it usually better to store a student's name and date of birth in separate fields?",hint:"Consider searching and updating each value independently.",steps:"1. Identify the two kinds of information. 2. Consider separate searches and updates. 3. Store them in separate fields.",ans:"It allows each value to be searched, validated and updated independently.",why:"Separate fields organise different attributes and support more precise operations."},
{q:"Which is more suitable as a field name for a student's date of birth: DOB or X1? Explain.",hint:"Choose the name that communicates the field's meaning.",steps:"1. Compare the names. 2. Identify which one is understandable without extra explanation.",ans:"DOB, because it communicates the field's purpose more clearly than X1.",why:"Descriptive field names improve readability and reduce ambiguity."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_data_types", "Data types", `
<h2>Data types in databases</h2>
<p>A data type defines the kind of value a field can store and helps determine which values are valid for that field.</p>
<h3>Common data types</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Text:</strong> names, addresses and other character-based values.</li>
<li><strong>Integer:</strong> whole numbers, such as the number of books in stock.</li>
<li><strong>Decimal or real number:</strong> values with fractional parts, such as a measurement.</li>
<li><strong>Date/time:</strong> dates and times.</li>
<li><strong>Boolean:</strong> a true/false value, where supported.</li>
<li><strong>Currency:</strong> monetary values in systems that provide a dedicated currency type.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Choosing appropriate types</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Field</th><th>Suitable type</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
<tr><td>StudentName</td><td>Text</td><td>Stores letters and spaces</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quantity</td><td>Integer</td><td>Counts whole items</td></tr>
<tr><td>DateOfBirth</td><td>Date</td><td>Represents a calendar date</td></tr>
<tr><td>IsPaid</td><td>Boolean</td><td>Represents true or false</td></tr>
</table>
<p>An admission number may contain digits but still be stored as text if it is an identifier rather than a quantity used in arithmetic. For example, leading zeros may need to be preserved.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a data type in a database?",hint:"It describes what kind of value a field can store.",steps:"1. Identify a field. 2. Determine the permitted kind of value. 3. Explain the role of its type.",ans:"A classification that specifies the kind of value a field can store.",why:"Appropriate types support meaningful storage and validation."},
{q:"Which data type is suitable for a student's date of birth?",hint:"The value represents a calendar date.",steps:"1. Identify what the value represents. 2. Select the type designed for dates.",ans:"Date.",why:"A date type represents calendar dates and supports date-related operations."},
{q:"Why is an integer suitable for a stock quantity of whole books?",hint:"Books are counted in whole units.",steps:"1. Identify the quantity being stored. 2. Determine whether fractional books are appropriate. 3. Choose the type.",ans:"Because the quantity counts whole items and normally does not require a fractional value.",why:"An integer is suitable for whole-number counts."},
{q:"Why might an admission number such as 00125 be stored as text instead of an integer?",hint:"An identifier is not necessarily a quantity.",steps:"1. Notice the leading zeros. 2. Consider whether arithmetic is required. 3. Preserve the identifier exactly.",ans:"Text can preserve leading zeros and treats the admission number as an identifier rather than a quantity.",why:"Converting it to an integer could remove leading zeros."},
{q:"Which type is most appropriate for a field called IsPaid that records only whether a payment has been made?",hint:"The value has only two logical states.",steps:"1. Identify the possible values. 2. Match them to a suitable data type.",ans:"Boolean, representing true or false.",why:"A Boolean type is designed for two-state logical values."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_primary_keys", "Primary keys", `
<h2>Primary keys</h2>
<p>A primary key is a field, or combination of fields, that uniquely identifies each record in a table.</p>
<p>Primary key values must be unique and must not be missing (NULL). A primary key helps the database distinguish records even when other values are the same.</p>
<h3>Example: Students table</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>AdmissionNo (Primary Key)</th><th>Name</th><th>Class</th></tr>
<tr><td>501</td><td>Kamau</td><td>Form 1</td></tr>
<tr><td>502</td><td>Kamau</td><td>Form 2</td></tr>
<tr><td>503</td><td>Wambui</td><td>Form 1</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The two students named Kamau can be distinguished by their admission numbers.</p>
<h3>Choosing a primary key</h3>
<ul>
<li>It must uniquely identify each record.</li>
<li>It should always have a value.</li>
<li>It should be stable where possible.</li>
<li>It should not rely on a value that may commonly be shared, such as a person's name.</li>
</ul>
<p>A primary key can be a single field, such as AdmissionNo, or a combination of fields when appropriate. A foreign key in another table can refer to a primary key to connect related records.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a primary key?",hint:"It identifies records uniquely.",steps:"1. Identify its role in a table. 2. State the uniqueness requirement.",ans:"A field or combination of fields that uniquely identifies each record in a table.",why:"A primary key distinguishes one record from every other record in the table."},
{q:"State two requirements of a primary key.",hint:"Think about repeated values and missing values.",steps:"1. Consider whether two records can share the same key. 2. Consider whether the key can be missing.",ans:"Its values must be unique and must not be NULL.",why:"Unique, non-missing values allow each record to be identified reliably."},
{q:"Why is a student's name usually a poor primary key?",hint:"Different students can have the same name.",steps:"1. Imagine two students with identical names. 2. Check whether the name distinguishes them.",ans:"Names are not guaranteed to be unique, so two students may share the same name.",why:"A primary key must uniquely identify every record."},
{q:"In a Students table, why could AdmissionNo be a better primary key than Class?",hint:"Many students can belong to the same class.",steps:"1. Compare the values across students. 2. Check which field can distinguish every student.",ans:"AdmissionNo can be assigned uniquely to each student, while many students share the same class.",why:"A primary key must distinguish individual records, not just group them."},
{q:"Two student records have the same primary key value. What is wrong?",hint:"Recall the uniqueness rule.",steps:"1. Compare the key values. 2. Check whether they are repeated. 3. Apply the primary key requirement.",ans:"The primary key uniqueness rule has been violated.",why:"A primary key value cannot identify two different records in the same table."}
]);
add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_foreign_keys", "Foreign keys", `
<h2>Foreign keys</h2>
<p>A foreign key is a field, or combination of fields, in one table that refers to a candidate or primary key in another table. It helps connect related records.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p><strong>Students table</strong></p>
<table>
<tr><th>StudentID (PK)</th><th>Name</th></tr>
<tr><td>101</td><td>Amina</td></tr>
<tr><td>102</td><td>Brian</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Marks table</strong></p>
<table>
<tr><th>MarkID (PK)</th><th>StudentID (FK)</th><th>Score</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>101</td><td>78</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>102</td><td>65</td></tr>
</table>
<p><code>StudentID</code> in Students is the primary key. <code>StudentID</code> in Marks is a foreign key because it refers to a student record.</p>
<h3>Why foreign keys matter</h3>
<ul>
<li>Connect records stored in different tables.</li>
<li>Help maintain valid relationships between tables.</li>
<li>Reduce the need to repeat all student details in every marks record.</li>
</ul>
<p>A foreign key value generally needs to match an existing referenced key value, unless the database permits a NULL value and the relationship is optional.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a foreign key?",hint:"It refers to a key in another table.",steps:"1. Identify the field in the related table. 2. Find the key it references. 3. Explain its linking role.",ans:"A field or combination of fields that refers to a key in another table.",why:"A foreign key connects related records across tables."},
{q:"In a Marks table, StudentID refers to StudentID in Students. What role does StudentID play in Marks?",hint:"The field points to a student record.",steps:"1. Identify the referenced field. 2. Locate the field in Marks. 3. Name its role there.",ans:"It is a foreign key in the Marks table.",why:"It refers to the key identifying a student in the Students table."},
{q:"Why use a foreign key instead of entering the student's full name in every marks record?",hint:"Think about repetition and reliable links.",steps:"1. Identify repeated student details. 2. Use the student identifier. 3. Link each marks record to its student.",ans:"It links records reliably and reduces repeated student information.",why:"An identifier is more reliable than names, which may be repeated or changed."},
{q:"Students contains StudentID values 101 and 102. A Marks record uses StudentID 105. Why might the database reject this record?",hint:"Check whether the referenced student exists.",steps:"1. Look at the available student IDs. 2. Check for 105. 3. Apply the foreign-key rule.",ans:"Because StudentID 105 does not match an existing student record.",why:"Referential integrity normally prevents references to nonexistent records."},
{q:"Can a foreign key value appear more than once in a table?",hint:"Consider several marks records belonging to one student.",steps:"1. Imagine one student has several marks. 2. Each marks record refers to the same student. 3. Check whether repetition is allowed.",ans:"Yes. A foreign key can repeat when several records refer to the same parent record.",why:"Foreign keys are not automatically unique; repeated values commonly support one-to-many relationships."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_relationships_between_tables", "Relationships between tables", `
<h2>Relationships between tables</h2>
<p>A relationship describes how records in different tables are associated. Relationships are commonly established by connecting a primary key in one table to a foreign key in another.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A school database may contain Students and Classes tables.</p>
<ul>
<li>Students contains StudentID, Name and ClassID.</li>
<li>Classes contains ClassID and ClassName.</li>
<li>ClassID in Classes identifies each class.</li>
<li>ClassID in Students refers to the student's class.</li>
</ul>
<p>This design avoids repeating the full class details for every student. The shared identifier connects the records.</p>
<h3>Why relationships are useful</h3>
<ul>
<li>Allow information in separate tables to be combined.</li>
<li>Reduce unnecessary duplication.</li>
<li>Help keep related information consistent.</li>
<li>Represent real situations, such as students belonging to classes.</li>
</ul>
<p>The number of records that can be associated determines the relationship type: one-to-one, one-to-many or many-to-many.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a relationship between database tables?",hint:"It connects records in different tables.",steps:"1. Identify the tables. 2. Identify how their records are linked. 3. Explain what the link represents.",ans:"An association between records in different tables, commonly established using keys.",why:"Relationships allow a database to represent connections between related information."},
{q:"How can a Students table be linked to a Classes table?",hint:"Look for an identifier shared by the two tables.",steps:"1. Give each class a ClassID. 2. Store ClassID in Classes as a key. 3. Refer to it from Students.",ans:"Use ClassID as the key in Classes and as a foreign key in Students.",why:"The matching identifier connects each student to the appropriate class."},
{q:"Why is it useful to keep class details in a separate Classes table?",hint:"Consider what happens when many students belong to the same class.",steps:"1. Identify repeated class details. 2. Store them once. 3. Link students to the class record.",ans:"It reduces repetition and makes class information easier to maintain consistently.",why:"A separate table avoids unnecessary duplication of class details."},
{q:"Which field connects a child record to a parent record in a relational database?",hint:"One table's key is referred to by another table.",steps:"1. Identify the referenced key. 2. Identify the field that refers to it.",ans:"A foreign key in the child table refers to a key in the parent table.",why:"The foreign key establishes the connection between the records."},
{q:"A database has Students, Subjects and Teachers tables. Why might relationships be needed among them?",hint:"Think about which student studies which subject and who teaches it.",steps:"1. Identify the real-world connections. 2. Determine which records must be associated. 3. Use suitable keys and relationships.",ans:"To represent and retrieve connections such as student enrolment in subjects and teachers assigned to subjects.",why:"Relationships let a database represent connected real-world information without storing everything in one table."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_relational_data_model", "Relational data model", `
<h2>Relational data model</h2>
<p>The relational data model organises data into tables, also called relations. Each table has columns for attributes and rows for records.</p>
<p>Tables can be connected through keys. A primary key identifies records in its table, while foreign keys can refer to keys in related tables.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p><strong>Students</strong></p>
<table>
<tr><th>StudentID (PK)</th><th>Name</th></tr>
<tr><td>11</td><td>Wanjiku</td></tr>
<tr><td>12</td><td>Otieno</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Subjects</strong></p>
<table>
<tr><th>SubjectID (PK)</th><th>SubjectName</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Mathematics</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Computer Studies</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A third table, Enrolments, can connect students to subjects using StudentID and SubjectID.</p>
<h3>Main ideas</h3>
<ul>
<li>Data is organised into tables.</li>
<li>Columns describe attributes; rows represent records.</li>
<li>Keys identify records and connect tables.</li>
<li>Relationships allow related data to be queried together.</li>
</ul>
`, [
{q:"What is the relational data model?",hint:"It represents data using tables and relationships.",steps:"1. Identify the main structure. 2. Explain how records are stored. 3. Mention how tables can be connected.",ans:"A data model that organises data into tables and connects related tables through keys.",why:"The relational model represents information as structured tables with defined relationships."},
{q:"In the relational model, what does a row represent?",hint:"A row contains values describing one item.",steps:"1. Look across a row. 2. Identify the entity or item described.",ans:"A record or tuple.",why:"Each row groups the values describing one record."},
{q:"What is the role of a primary key in the relational model?",hint:"Think about identifying individual records.",steps:"1. Identify the record. 2. Select a field or combination that uniquely identifies it.",ans:"It uniquely identifies each record in a table.",why:"Unique identification supports reliable retrieval and relationships."},
{q:"Why might a school use an Enrolments table between Students and Subjects?",hint:"A student can study several subjects, and a subject can have several students.",steps:"1. Identify the two sets of records. 2. Identify the many-to-many connection. 3. Use a linking table.",ans:"To represent which students take which subjects using StudentID and SubjectID.",why:"A linking table represents many-to-many relationships in a relational database."},
{q:"What is one advantage of storing related information in separate linked tables?",hint:"Think about avoiding repeated details.",steps:"1. Identify repeated data. 2. Store each type of information in a suitable table. 3. Link the tables with keys.",ans:"It can reduce duplication and make updates more consistent.",why:"Separating related entities avoids unnecessarily repeating their details."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_one_to_one_relationships", "One-to-one relationships", `
<h2>One-to-one relationships</h2>
<p>A one-to-one relationship occurs when each record in one table is associated with at most one record in another table, and vice versa.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Imagine a database with Employees and StaffParkingPermits tables. If each employee can have at most one permit and each permit belongs to at most one employee, the relationship is one-to-one.</p>
<p><strong>Employees</strong></p>
<table>
<tr><th>EmployeeID (PK)</th><th>Name</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Grace</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>David</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>StaffParkingPermits</strong></p>
<table>
<tr><th>PermitID (PK)</th><th>EmployeeID (FK, unique)</th></tr>
<tr><td>501</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>502</td><td>2</td></tr>
</table>
<p>In this design, making the foreign key unique prevents multiple permits from being assigned to the same employee. The exact constraints depend on the intended rules of the system.</p>
<h3>Why separate the tables?</h3>
<p>Separate tables may be useful when the information is optional, sensitive, or managed differently from the main employee details.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a one-to-one relationship?",hint:"Each record is associated with at most one record on the other side.",steps:"1. Consider a record in the first table. 2. Count its possible matches in the second. 3. Reverse the direction.",ans:"A relationship where each record on either side is associated with at most one record on the other side.",why:"The defining feature is the one-record-to-one-record association."},
{q:"Give a possible example of a one-to-one relationship.",hint:"Think of an item assigned to one person with at most one assignment per person.",steps:"1. Choose two related record types. 2. Set a rule allowing at most one match in either direction.",ans:"An employee and an individually assigned parking permit, if each can be associated with at most one of the other.",why:"The example fits only when the system's rules enforce the one-to-one limit."},
{q:"Why might EmployeeID in StaffParkingPermits need a unique constraint?",hint:"Consider whether one employee could otherwise appear in multiple permit records.",steps:"1. Check the foreign key values. 2. Imagine the same EmployeeID repeated. 3. Apply the one-per-employee rule.",ans:"To prevent more than one permit record from being linked to the same employee.",why:"Uniqueness helps enforce the intended one-to-one relationship."},
{q:"If one employee can receive several parking permits, is the relationship still one-to-one?",hint:"Check how many permits one employee can have.",steps:"1. Identify the employee. 2. Count possible permits. 3. Compare with the one-to-one definition.",ans:"No. It becomes one-to-many from Employees to StaffParkingPermits.",why:"One employee linked to several permits breaks the one-to-one rule."},
{q:"Why might a database designer put optional permit details in a separate table rather than Employees?",hint:"Consider information that not every employee has.",steps:"1. Identify optional information. 2. Consider whether it is needed for every employee. 3. Separate it when that suits the design.",ans:"It keeps optional or separately managed information apart from the main employee details.",why:"Separate tables can make a design more organised when attributes have different purposes or availability."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_one_to_many_relationships", "One-to-many relationships", `
<h2>One-to-many relationships</h2>
<p>A one-to-many relationship occurs when one record in a table can be associated with many records in another table, while each of those records refers to one record on the first side.</p>
<h3>Example: Classes and Students</h3>
<p>One class can contain many students. Each student belongs to one class in this example.</p>
<p><strong>Classes</strong></p>
<table>
<tr><th>ClassID (PK)</th><th>ClassName</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Form 1 East</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Form 1 West</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Students</strong></p>
<table>
<tr><th>StudentID (PK)</th><th>Name</th><th>ClassID (FK)</th></tr>
<tr><td>101</td><td>Amina</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>102</td><td>Brian</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>103</td><td>Chebet</td><td>2</td></tr>
</table>
<p>ClassID appears as a foreign key in Students. Several students can have the same ClassID, so the foreign key does not need to be unique.</p>
<h3>Where it is used</h3>
<ul>
<li>One department has many employees.</li>
<li>One customer places many orders.</li>
<li>One class contains many students.</li>
</ul>
`, [
{q:"What is a one-to-many relationship?",hint:"One record on one side can connect to several records on the other.",steps:"1. Consider one record in the first table. 2. Count its related records in the second. 3. Check how each child record relates back.",ans:"A relationship in which one record can be linked to many records in another table.",why:"It models situations where one parent record has several related child records."},
{q:"Why can several students have the same ClassID in the Students table?",hint:"Many students can belong to one class.",steps:"1. Identify ClassID as the class identifier. 2. Consider several students in that class. 3. Check the relationship type.",ans:"Because many student records can refer to the same class through the foreign key.",why:"A one-to-many relationship allows repeated foreign key values on the many side."},
{q:"Where is the foreign key usually stored in a one-to-many relationship?",hint:"It belongs on the side that can have many records.",steps:"1. Identify the one side. 2. Identify the many side. 3. Place the reference in the many-side table.",ans:"In the table on the many side, referring to the key on the one side.",why:"Each child record stores the identifier of its associated parent record."},
{q:"One customer can place many orders. Which table should normally contain CustomerID as a foreign key: Customers or Orders?",hint:"Each order belongs to a customer.",steps:"1. Identify the one side: Customers. 2. Identify the many side: Orders. 3. Put the reference in Orders.",ans:"Orders.",why:"Each order record refers to the customer who placed it."},
{q:"If ClassID in Students were made unique, what problem could arise?",hint:"Consider whether two students can belong to the same class.",steps:"1. Consider two students in the same class. 2. Both need the same ClassID. 3. Check the uniqueness restriction.",ans:"The database could prevent two students from sharing the same class identifier, incorrectly blocking normal class membership.",why:"The foreign key on the many side must usually allow repeated values."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_many_to_many_relationships", "Many-to-many relationships", `
<h2>Many-to-many relationships</h2>
<p>A many-to-many relationship occurs when each record in one table can relate to many records in another table, and each record in the second table can also relate to many records in the first.</p>
<h3>Example: Students and Subjects</h3>
<p>A student can study several subjects, and each subject can be studied by many students.</p>
<p>In a relational database, this relationship is usually represented using a linking table.</p>
<p><strong>Students:</strong> StudentID, Name</p>
<p><strong>Subjects:</strong> SubjectID, SubjectName</p>
<p><strong>Enrolments:</strong> StudentID, SubjectID</p>
<table>
<tr><th>StudentID (FK)</th><th>SubjectID (FK)</th></tr>
<tr><td>101</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>101</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>102</td><td>1</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Student 101 takes two subjects. Subject 1 is taken by students 101 and 102.</p>
<p>The Enrolments table may use the combination of StudentID and SubjectID as a composite primary key, preventing the same student-subject pair from being entered twice.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a many-to-many relationship?",hint:"Both sides can have multiple related records.",steps:"1. Check how many records one student can link to. 2. Check how many students one subject can link to.",ans:"A relationship in which records on both sides can each be related to many records on the other side.",why:"It represents multiple connections in both directions."},
{q:"Why is a linking table useful for a Students-to-Subjects relationship?",hint:"A student can take many subjects, and each subject can have many students.",steps:"1. Identify the many-to-many relationship. 2. Create an Enrolments table. 3. Store the keys from both tables in it.",ans:"It represents each student-subject association using StudentID and SubjectID.",why:"A linking table converts the many-to-many relationship into two one-to-many relationships."},
{q:"In an Enrolments table, what does a row containing StudentID 101 and SubjectID 2 represent?",hint:"Each row records one association.",steps:"1. Identify the student. 2. Identify the subject. 3. Combine them into one enrolment.",ans:"Student 101 is enrolled in subject 2.",why:"The linking record associates one student with one subject."},
{q:"Why might StudentID and SubjectID together form a composite primary key in Enrolments?",hint:"The pair identifies one particular student-subject combination.",steps:"1. Identify the two fields. 2. Consider whether the pair identifies an enrolment. 3. Prevent duplicate pairs.",ans:"The combination can uniquely identify each enrolment and prevent duplicate student-subject pairs.",why:"A composite key uses multiple fields together to identify a record."},
{q:"What two relationships are created when a many-to-many relationship is resolved using a linking table?",hint:"Look at each main table connecting to Enrolments.",steps:"1. Connect Students to Enrolments. 2. Connect Subjects to Enrolments. 3. Identify the relationship type for each.",ans:"A one-to-many relationship from Students to Enrolments and another from Subjects to Enrolments.",why:"Each student or subject can appear in many linking-table records."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_referential_integrity", "Referential integrity", `
<h2>Referential integrity</h2>
<p>Referential integrity is a set of rules that keeps relationships between tables valid. In a relational database, a foreign key must normally match an existing referenced key, unless NULL is allowed for an optional relationship.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Suppose Students contains StudentID values 101 and 102. A Marks record that refers to StudentID 101 has a valid reference. A Marks record referring to StudentID 999 has no matching student and would normally be rejected.</p>
<h3>How it helps</h3>
<ul>
<li>Prevents records from referring to nonexistent parent records.</li>
<li>Helps keep relationships consistent.</li>
<li>Can control what happens when a referenced record is deleted or updated.</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, the database may restrict deletion of a student who has related marks, allow deletion only after the related records are handled, or use a configured cascading action.</p>
<p>Referential integrity is different from data validation. Referential integrity checks relationships between records, while validation rules can check properties such as a score being within an acceptable range.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is referential integrity?",hint:"It protects the validity of links between tables.",steps:"1. Identify a foreign key. 2. Check the referenced record. 3. Explain the rule that keeps the link valid.",ans:"Rules that ensure foreign-key references point to valid records, subject to the database's NULL and relationship rules.",why:"It prevents broken references between related tables."},
{q:"Why would a database reject a Marks record with StudentID 999 when no such student exists?",hint:"Check the foreign key against the Students table.",steps:"1. Find the referenced ID. 2. Search for it in Students. 3. Apply the referential integrity rule.",ans:"The foreign key has no matching student record.",why:"The database prevents the marks record from referencing a nonexistent student."},
{q:"What could happen when a parent record is deleted while child records still refer to it?",hint:"The database must decide how to handle the references.",steps:"1. Identify the parent record. 2. Identify its dependent records. 3. Apply the configured delete rule.",ans:"The deletion may be restricted, or related records may be handled using a configured action such as cascading deletion.",why:"The database must preserve valid relationships according to its rules."},
{q:"How is referential integrity different from checking that a mark is between 0 and 100?",hint:"One checks a relationship; the other checks a value.",steps:"1. Identify the foreign-key check. 2. Identify the numerical range check. 3. Compare their purposes.",ans:"Referential integrity checks links between records; the mark range is a data validation rule.",why:"The two checks protect different aspects of data quality."},
{q:"A Students table has StudentID 101. A Marks record refers to 101. Is the reference valid, assuming the key exists and the database rules are satisfied?",hint:"Compare the foreign key with the referenced key.",steps:"1. Read the ID in Marks. 2. Find the same ID in Students. 3. Confirm that the reference matches.",ans:"Yes. StudentID 101 refers to an existing student.",why:"A foreign-key value that matches a valid referenced key satisfies this reference check."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_entity_relationship_diagrams", "Entity relationship diagrams", `
<h2>Entity relationship diagrams</h2>
<p>An entity relationship diagram (ERD) is a diagram used to plan and show the entities in a database, their attributes and the relationships between them.</p>
<h3>Main components</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Entity:</strong> something the database stores information about, such as Student or Subject.</li>
<li><strong>Attribute:</strong> a property of an entity, such as StudentName or DateOfBirth.</li>
<li><strong>Relationship:</strong> an association between entities, such as a student enrolling in a subject.</li>
<li><strong>Cardinality:</strong> the number of records that may be associated, such as one-to-one, one-to-many or many-to-many.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Simple example</h3>
<pre><code>STUDENT
- StudentID (PK)
- Name

       1
STUDENT --------< ENROLMENT
                  - StudentID (FK)
                  - SubjectID (FK)
SUBJECT
- SubjectID (PK)
- SubjectName</code></pre>
<p>This simplified sketch indicates that a student can have many enrolment records and a subject can also have many enrolment records. In a formal ERD, relationship symbols or cardinality labels should make the connections clear.</p>
<p>ERDs help identify tables, keys and relationships before implementation begins.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is an entity relationship diagram?",hint:"It is a planning diagram for database structure.",steps:"1. Identify what the diagram represents. 2. Name the elements it shows.",ans:"A diagram showing database entities, their attributes and the relationships between them.",why:"An ERD helps designers plan the database before creating tables."},
{q:"What is an entity in an ERD?",hint:"It is something the database stores information about.",steps:"1. Identify a thing or concept. 2. Decide whether the database needs to keep records about it.",ans:"A person, place, object, event or concept about which data is stored.",why:"Entities are the main subjects represented in the database."},
{q:"What is an attribute of the Student entity?",hint:"Think about a property describing a student.",steps:"1. Identify the Student entity. 2. Name one item of information about a student.",ans:"Examples include StudentID, Name, DateOfBirth or ClassID.",why:"Attributes describe the properties stored for an entity."},
{q:"Why should cardinality be shown in an ERD?",hint:"It describes how many records can be associated.",steps:"1. Identify the related entities. 2. Determine whether each record can link to one or many records. 3. Show the relationship limit.",ans:"It communicates whether the relationship is one-to-one, one-to-many or many-to-many.",why:"Cardinality helps ensure the tables and keys are designed to represent the intended rules."},
{q:"How can an ERD help before a database is built?",hint:"Think about planning tables and keys early.",steps:"1. Identify entities. 2. List attributes. 3. Plan keys and relationships. 4. Review the design before implementation.",ans:"It helps plan entities, attributes, keys and relationships before creating the database.",why:"A clear plan can reveal missing entities or incorrect relationships before implementation."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_identifying_entities", "Identifying entities", `
<h2>Identifying entities</h2>
<p>An entity is a person, place, object, event or concept about which a database stores information. Identifying entities is an early step in database design.</p>
<h3>Example: School library</h3>
<p>A library system may need to store information about:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Book:</strong> books held by the library.</li>
<li><strong>Member:</strong> people who borrow books.</li>
<li><strong>Loan:</strong> a borrowing transaction connecting a member and a book.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each entity usually becomes a table in a relational database. A table may include an identifier, such as BookID or MemberID, to distinguish its records.</p>
<h3>How to identify entities</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the problem description carefully.</li>
<li>Identify the main things or events the system must keep records about.</li>
<li>Separate entities from properties that describe them.</li>
<li>Check whether each proposed entity needs its own records.</li>
</ol>
<p>For example, a book's title is normally an attribute of Book, not a separate entity. A Loan is an entity when the system must store details of each borrowing event.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is an entity in database design?",hint:"It is something the system needs to store records about.",steps:"1. Read the system description. 2. Identify a main person, object or event. 3. Decide whether records are needed for it.",ans:"A person, object, place, event or concept about which the database stores information.",why:"Entities identify the main subjects whose information must be stored."},
{q:"Name three possible entities in a school library database.",hint:"Think about what is borrowed, who borrows it and the borrowing event.",steps:"1. Identify the item. 2. Identify the person. 3. Identify the transaction.",ans:"Book, Member and Loan.",why:"These are distinct subjects about which a library system may need to store records."},
{q:"In a library database, is BookTitle usually an entity or an attribute?",hint:"It describes a book.",steps:"1. Identify what BookTitle describes. 2. Decide whether it is a main record type or a property.",ans:"An attribute of the Book entity.",why:"A title describes a book rather than representing a separate type of record in this design."},
{q:"Why might Loan be a separate entity instead of storing only a borrowed book in Member?",hint:"A member may borrow many books over time.",steps:"1. Consider repeated borrowing events. 2. Identify information needed for each event. 3. Store each loan as a record.",ans:"A separate Loan entity can store each borrowing event, including its dates and associated member and book.",why:"Transactions have their own details and connect multiple entities."},
{q:"A school system stores students, classes and examination results. Identify three likely entities.",hint:"Look for the main types of records the system must maintain.",steps:"1. Identify student records. 2. Identify class records. 3. Identify examination result records.",ans:"Student, Class and ExaminationResult (or Result).",why:"Each represents a distinct category of information the school system needs to manage."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_identifying_attributes", "Identifying attributes", `
<h2>Identifying attributes</h2>
<p>An attribute is a property or characteristic that describes an entity. After identifying entities, a database designer determines which attributes must be stored for each one.</p>
<h3>Example: Student entity</h3>
<p>Possible attributes include:</p>
<ul>
<li>StudentID</li>
<li>FirstName</li>
<li>LastName</li>
<li>DateOfBirth</li>
<li>ClassID</li>
</ul>
<p>StudentID may be selected as the primary key if it uniquely identifies every student. ClassID may be a foreign key if it refers to a record in a Classes table.</p>
<h3>Choosing appropriate attributes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Include information needed for the system's purpose.</li>
<li>Use clear names that describe the stored values.</li>
<li>Choose suitable data types.</li>
<li>Avoid combining unrelated values in one field.</li>
<li>Do not collect unnecessary personal information.</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, storing a complete address in one field may make it difficult to search by town or postal code. If those parts need to be searched independently, separate attributes may be more suitable.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is an attribute?",hint:"It describes a property of an entity.",steps:"1. Identify the entity. 2. Name a characteristic that needs to be stored about it.",ans:"A property or characteristic describing an entity.",why:"Attributes specify what information is recorded for each entity."},
{q:"Give three possible attributes of a Student entity.",hint:"Think about information needed to identify and describe a student.",steps:"1. Choose an identifier. 2. Choose descriptive details. 3. Give three field names.",ans:"Examples include StudentID, FirstName, LastName, DateOfBirth and ClassID.",why:"These attributes describe or identify a student record."},
{q:"Why might StudentID be a primary key while FirstName is not?",hint:"Consider whether two students can share a first name.",steps:"1. Check whether StudentID is unique. 2. Check whether FirstName can repeat. 3. Apply the primary-key rule.",ans:"StudentID can be assigned uniquely to each student, while first names may be repeated.",why:"A primary key must uniquely identify each record."},
{q:"A system needs to search students by town and postal code. Why might Address be divided into separate attributes?",hint:"Consider searching individual parts of a combined value.",steps:"1. Identify the parts needed for searches. 2. Consider whether one combined field makes searching difficult. 3. Store the parts separately when appropriate.",ans:"Separate attributes allow the system to search and validate town and postal code independently.",why:"Attributes should be structured to support the system's actual information needs."},
{q:"Why should a database designer avoid adding attributes that the system does not need?",hint:"Think about unnecessary data collection and maintenance.",steps:"1. Identify the system's purpose. 2. Check whether the attribute supports that purpose. 3. Leave out unnecessary information.",ans:"It reduces unnecessary data collection, complexity and maintenance, and can limit privacy risks.",why:"Good design stores relevant information rather than collecting data without a clear purpose."}
]);
add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_choosing_primary_keys", "Choosing primary keys", `<h2>Choosing primary keys</h2>
<p>A primary key is a field, or combination of fields, that uniquely identifies each record in a table. It must contain unique values and cannot be NULL.</p>
<h3>How to choose one</h3>
<ul>
<li>Choose a field whose value is different for every record.</li>
<li>Prefer values that do not change frequently.</li>
<li>Avoid names when different people can share the same name.</li>
<li>Use an assigned identifier such as StudentID when suitable.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example</h3>
<table><tr><th>StudentID</th><th>Name</th></tr><tr><td>101</td><td>Brian</td></tr><tr><td>102</td><td>Brian</td></tr></table>
<p><code>StudentID</code> is a suitable primary key because the two students can share a name but have different identifiers.</p>
<p><strong>Remember:</strong> a primary key identifies a record, not merely a type of information.</p>`, [
{q:"Two students have the same name. Why is Name a poor primary key?",hint:"Check whether every value is unique.",steps:"1. Compare the two names. 2. Notice they are identical. 3. A primary key cannot identify both records uniquely.",ans:"Name is unsuitable because duplicate names can occur.",why:"A primary key must distinguish every record from every other record."},
{q:"A table stores StudentID, Name and Age. Which field is the best primary key if every StudentID is unique?",hint:"Choose the field that uniquely identifies each student.",steps:"1. Check StudentID values. 2. Confirm they are unique. 3. Select StudentID.",ans:"StudentID.",why:"Its unique values identify individual student records."},
{q:"A table has StudentID values 101, 102 and 101. What is wrong?",hint:"Look for a repeated key.",steps:"1. Compare all StudentID values. 2. Find the repeated 101. 3. Identify the uniqueness violation.",ans:"The primary key value 101 is duplicated.",why:"Primary key values must be unique."},
{q:"Can a primary key be left blank for one record?",hint:"Would the record still have a unique identifier?",steps:"1. Recall that every record needs an identifier. 2. A blank value identifies no specific record. 3. Apply the primary-key rule.",ans:"No. A primary key cannot be NULL.",why:"Every record must have a valid unique identifier."},
{q:"A school database stores learners' names, birth dates and assigned admission numbers. Which is usually the best primary key?",hint:"Consider which value is assigned to distinguish each learner.",steps:"1. Names can be shared. 2. Birth dates can be shared. 3. Admission numbers are assigned to identify learners.",ans:"Admission number, provided each is unique.",why:"An assigned unique identifier is more reliable than personal details that may repeat."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_designing_table_structures", "Designing table structures", `<h2>Designing table structures</h2>
<p>Table design means deciding what information belongs in each table and defining the fields, data types and keys needed to store it correctly.</p>
<h3>Design steps</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the entity being described, such as Student or Book.</li>
<li>List the attributes needed for that entity.</li>
<li>Choose a suitable data type for each field.</li>
<li>Select a primary key.</li>
<li>Use foreign keys where tables must be linked.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Example: Students table</h3>
<table><tr><th>Field</th><th>Data type</th><th>Purpose</th></tr><tr><td>StudentID</td><td>Integer</td><td>Primary key</td></tr><tr><td>FullName</td><td>Text</td><td>Learner's name</td></tr><tr><td>DateOfBirth</td><td>Date</td><td>Birth date</td></tr></table>
<p>Store information about different entities in separate related tables where appropriate. This reduces unnecessary repetition and makes records easier to maintain.</p>`, [
{q:"You are designing a table to store books. Give three suitable fields.",hint:"Think about identifying and describing each book.",steps:"1. Include an identifier. 2. Include the title. 3. Include another useful book attribute.",ans:"For example: BookID, Title and Author.",why:"These fields identify and describe each book."},
{q:"Why should DateOfBirth use a date data type rather than an integer?",hint:"Consider the kind of value being stored.",steps:"1. Identify the value as a calendar date. 2. Compare it with a whole number. 3. Select the type designed for dates.",ans:"Because it stores a date and supports date-specific validation and operations.",why:"Suitable data types help preserve meaning and prevent invalid values."},
{q:"A Students table stores StudentID, StudentName and ClassName. If class details are maintained separately, how could the design be improved?",hint:"Avoid repeating the same class information for many students.",steps:"1. Create a Classes table. 2. Give each class a unique ClassID. 3. Store ClassID in Students as a foreign key.",ans:"Create a Classes table and link Students to it using ClassID.",why:"This reduces repeated class details and supports consistent updates."},
{q:"What is the purpose of defining field data types during table design?",hint:"Think about valid values and storage.",steps:"1. Identify the expected value for each field. 2. Choose a compatible type. 3. Explain how it restricts or interprets data.",ans:"To store values appropriately and help prevent invalid data.",why:"For example, a date field is designed for dates rather than arbitrary text."},
{q:"A Marks table stores StudentID, SubjectID and Mark. Why might StudentID and SubjectID both be needed?",hint:"One identifies the learner; the other identifies the subject.",steps:"1. Identify the student receiving the mark. 2. Identify the subject being assessed. 3. Use both references to connect the mark to the right entities.",ans:"They identify which student and which subject the mark belongs to.",why:"A record often describes a relationship between multiple entities."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_data_validation_rules", "Data validation rules", `<h2>Data validation rules</h2>
<p>Data validation checks whether entered data meets defined rules before it is accepted. Validation improves accuracy but does not guarantee that every accepted value is factually correct.</p>
<h3>Common validation rules</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Range check:</strong> a mark must be between 0 and 100.</li>
<li><strong>Type check:</strong> a quantity must be numeric.</li>
<li><strong>Presence check:</strong> a required field cannot be blank.</li>
<li><strong>Length check:</strong> a code must have the required number of characters.</li>
<li><strong>Format check:</strong> a value must follow a specified pattern.</li>
<li><strong>Lookup check:</strong> a value must match an allowed list.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>For a student mark, a rule could require a numeric value from 0 through 100. The value 150 would fail the range check.</p>
<p><strong>Remember:</strong> validation checks data against a rule; verification checks whether data matches its original source.</p>`, [
{q:"A marks field accepts values from 0 to 100. Why should 125 be rejected?",hint:"Identify the rule that limits the permitted values.",steps:"1. Read the permitted range. 2. Compare 125 with the maximum of 100. 3. Apply the range rule.",ans:"It fails the range check because 125 is above 100.",why:"A range check rejects values outside the allowed limits."},
{q:"A required StudentID field is left blank. Which validation rule should detect this?",hint:"The problem is missing data.",steps:"1. Identify the field as required. 2. Notice that no value was entered. 3. Apply the rule for required entries.",ans:"Presence check.",why:"A presence check requires a value in a field."},
{q:"A phone-number field requires exactly 10 characters. Which check is most directly relevant?",hint:"Focus on the number of characters.",steps:"1. Count the characters. 2. Compare the count with 10. 3. Apply the length rule.",ans:"Length check.",why:"A length check tests whether a value has the required number of characters."},
{q:"A class field must contain one of Form 1, Form 2, Form 3 or Form 4. Which validation method fits?",hint:"The allowed values come from a fixed list.",steps:"1. Identify the permitted options. 2. Compare the entered value against them. 3. Reject values outside the list.",ans:"Lookup check.",why:"A lookup check restricts entries to approved values."},
{q:"Explain the difference between validation and verification.",hint:"One checks rules; the other checks against the source.",steps:"1. Define validation as checking data against rules. 2. Define verification as checking correspondence with the original source. 3. Give a simple example.",ans:"Validation checks whether data meets rules; verification checks whether it matches the original source.",why:"A correctly formatted value can still be copied incorrectly from the source."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_database_normalisation", "Database normalisation", `<h2>Database normalisation</h2>
<p>Database normalisation is the process of organising relational tables to reduce unnecessary duplication and prevent problems when inserting, updating or deleting data.</p>
<h3>Why normalise?</h3>
<ul>
<li>Reduce repeated information.</li>
<li>Keep facts about one entity in an appropriate table.</li>
<li>Reduce update anomalies, insertion anomalies and deletion anomalies.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example of repeated data</h3>
<p>A table stores StudentID, StudentName, ClassID and ClassTeacher. If many students belong to the same class, the teacher's name is repeated for every student in that class. Changing the teacher may require several updates.</p>
<p>A better design stores class information in a Classes table and links each student to the relevant class using ClassID.</p>
<h3>First Normal Form (1NF)</h3>
<p>Each field should contain a single value for each record, with no repeating groups of values in a field. For example, storing three subjects as a comma-separated list in one field is a poor relational table design.</p>
<p><strong>Remember:</strong> normalisation organises data and relationships; it does not mean splitting every field into its own table.</p>`, [
{q:"A class teacher's name is repeated in 40 student records. What design problem can this cause?",hint:"Consider what happens when the teacher changes.",steps:"1. Notice the repeated teacher information. 2. Imagine changing the teacher. 3. Recognise that multiple records must be updated.",ans:"Data duplication and a possible update anomaly.",why:"Repeated facts can become inconsistent if some copies are changed and others are not."},
{q:"A field contains the text 'Mathematics, English, Biology' for a student's subjects. Why can this violate First Normal Form?",hint:"Check whether the field contains one value or several.",steps:"1. Identify the separate subjects. 2. Notice they are stored together in one field. 3. Compare with the single-value principle.",ans:"It stores multiple values in one field instead of a single value.",why:"First Normal Form requires each field to contain an individual value rather than a repeating group."},
{q:"How can a separate Classes table reduce duplication?",hint:"Think about where class details should be stored.",steps:"1. Store class facts once in Classes. 2. Assign each class a ClassID. 3. Refer to that ClassID from student records.",ans:"It stores each class's details once and links students using ClassID.",why:"One authoritative class record reduces repeated teacher and class details."},
{q:"What is an update anomaly?",hint:"Consider repeated information that must be changed in several places.",steps:"1. Find a fact stored more than once. 2. Change only some copies. 3. Observe that the database now contains conflicting values.",ans:"An inconsistency caused when repeated data is not updated everywhere it appears.",why:"Normalisation helps reduce the risk of inconsistent copies of the same fact."},
{q:"Why is normalisation useful when designing a database?",hint:"Consider duplication and data changes.",steps:"1. Identify unnecessary repeated data. 2. Consider insertion, update and deletion problems. 3. Explain how better table organisation reduces them.",ans:"It reduces unnecessary duplication and helps prevent insertion, update and deletion anomalies.",why:"Well-structured related tables make information easier to maintain consistently."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_creating_tables", "Creating tables", `<h2>Creating tables</h2>
<p>Creating a table means defining its name, fields, data types and constraints before storing records. Tables can be created through a database design interface or SQL.</p>
<h3>SQL example</h3>
<pre><code>CREATE TABLE Students (
  StudentID INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  FullName VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
  Age INTEGER
);</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><code>CREATE TABLE</code> creates a new table.</li>
<li><code>StudentID</code> is an integer primary key.</li>
<li><code>FullName</code> stores text and cannot be NULL.</li>
<li><code>Age</code> stores an integer.</li>
</ul>
<p>Field types and supported syntax can differ between database systems. Define keys and validation constraints to match the information the table must store.</p>`, [
{q:"Which SQL command creates a new table?",hint:"Look for the command that defines a table structure.",steps:"1. Distinguish creating a structure from inserting data. 2. Recall the table-definition command. 3. State its syntax.",ans:"CREATE TABLE.",why:"CREATE TABLE defines a new table and its fields."},
{q:"In the example, what does PRIMARY KEY after StudentID mean?",hint:"Think about record identification.",steps:"1. Identify StudentID as the key field. 2. Recall primary-key requirements. 3. Explain its role.",ans:"StudentID uniquely identifies each record and cannot be NULL.",why:"The primary key enforces unique identification of records."},
{q:"What does NOT NULL mean for FullName?",hint:"Consider whether an empty database value is permitted.",steps:"1. Locate the constraint. 2. Apply it to FullName. 3. State what happens if the value is omitted as NULL.",ans:"FullName must have a non-NULL value.",why:"NOT NULL enforces the presence of a value in that field."},
{q:"What is the difference between creating a table and inserting a record?",hint:"One defines the structure; the other adds data.",steps:"1. Identify the table's fields and rules. 2. Identify a row of actual values. 3. Match each operation to its purpose.",ans:"Creating a table defines its structure; inserting a record adds a row of data.",why:"A table can exist before it contains any records."},
{q:"Write an SQL statement to create a Books table with BookID as an integer primary key and Title as text up to 150 characters.",hint:"Use CREATE TABLE and define both fields.",steps:"1. Start with CREATE TABLE Books. 2. Define BookID as INTEGER PRIMARY KEY. 3. Define Title as VARCHAR(150).",ans:"CREATE TABLE Books (BookID INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, Title VARCHAR(150));",why:"The statement defines the table, its identifier and its title field."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_inserting_records", "Inserting records", `<h2>Inserting records</h2>
<p>Inserting records means adding new rows to a database table. The values must match the fields' data types and satisfy the table's constraints.</p>
<h3>SQL example</h3>
<pre><code>INSERT INTO Students (StudentID, FullName, Age)
VALUES (101, 'Amina Otieno', 16);</code></pre>
<p>The field list identifies the destination columns. The values must correspond to those columns in the same order.</p>
<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Providing values in the wrong order.</li>
<li>Using a duplicate primary key.</li>
<li>Entering text where a numeric value is required.</li>
<li>Omitting a required field with no default value.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Remember:</strong> INSERT adds a new record; UPDATE changes an existing record.</p>`, [
{q:"Which SQL command adds a new row to Students?",hint:"Choose the command for adding records.",steps:"1. Identify the operation as adding a row. 2. Recall the SQL insertion command. 3. Name it.",ans:"INSERT INTO.",why:"INSERT INTO adds new records to a table."},
{q:"What does the field list in an INSERT statement specify?",hint:"It appears after the table name.",steps:"1. Read the column names. 2. Match each name to a supplied value. 3. Explain the mapping.",ans:"It specifies the columns into which the supplied values will be inserted.",why:"The field list makes the value-to-column mapping explicit."},
{q:"A record uses StudentID 101, but a record with StudentID 101 already exists as the primary key. What is the problem?",hint:"Recall the uniqueness requirement.",steps:"1. Compare the new key with existing keys. 2. Find the duplicate. 3. Apply the primary-key constraint.",ans:"The insertion violates the primary key's uniqueness rule.",why:"Two records cannot share the same primary key."},
{q:"Correct this statement if StudentID is numeric and FullName is text: INSERT INTO Students (StudentID, FullName) VALUES ('Amina', 101);",hint:"Match each value to the field named in the same position.",steps:"1. StudentID comes first and needs a number. 2. FullName comes second and needs text. 3. Swap the values into the correct order.",ans:"INSERT INTO Students (StudentID, FullName) VALUES (101, 'Amina');",why:"Each value must correspond to the intended column and data type."},
{q:"What is the difference between INSERT and UPDATE?",hint:"One adds a row; the other changes data already stored.",steps:"1. Define INSERT. 2. Define UPDATE. 3. Contrast their effects on existing records.",ans:"INSERT adds a new record; UPDATE modifies existing record values.",why:"Using the correct operation prevents accidental duplication or unintended changes."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_retrieving_records", "Retrieving records", `<h2>Retrieving records</h2>
<p>Retrieving records means reading stored information from a database. SQL commonly uses SELECT to retrieve specified columns and rows.</p>
<h3>Retrieve every column</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT * FROM Students;</code></pre>
<h3>Retrieve selected columns</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT FullName, Age
FROM Students;</code></pre>
<p><code>SELECT</code> identifies the columns to return, while <code>FROM</code> identifies the table. A query retrieves information without necessarily changing the stored data.</p>
<h3>Limit the records returned</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT FullName
FROM Students
WHERE Age = 16;</code></pre>
<p>This query returns the names of students whose Age equals 16, assuming the table contains those fields.</p>`, [
{q:"What does SELECT * FROM Students; do?",hint:"Interpret the asterisk in a SELECT query.",steps:"1. Identify Students as the table. 2. Recognise * as all columns. 3. Explain what records are returned when no filter is given.",ans:"It retrieves all columns from all records in Students.",why:"The query has no WHERE condition, so it does not filter the rows."},
{q:"Write a query to retrieve only FullName from Students.",hint:"Use SELECT with one field and FROM with the table.",steps:"1. Name the desired field after SELECT. 2. Add FROM Students. 3. End the statement.",ans:"SELECT FullName FROM Students;",why:"The query returns the specified column from the table."},
{q:"What does WHERE Age = 16 do in a query?",hint:"It restricts which rows are returned.",steps:"1. Identify Age as the field being tested. 2. Read the equality condition. 3. State which rows qualify.",ans:"It returns only records whose Age value is 16.",why:"WHERE filters records according to a condition."},
{q:"A query returns names and ages even though only names are needed. What should be changed?",hint:"Inspect the column list after SELECT.",steps:"1. Identify the unnecessary Age column. 2. Remove it from the SELECT list. 3. Keep the table source unchanged.",ans:"Use SELECT FullName FROM Students;",why:"Selecting only required columns avoids retrieving unnecessary data."},
{q:"Does a SELECT query normally change the stored records?",hint:"Distinguish reading data from editing it.",steps:"1. Identify SELECT as a retrieval command. 2. Compare it with UPDATE and DELETE. 3. State whether it changes the stored rows.",ans:"No. SELECT retrieves data without modifying stored records.",why:"SELECT reads matching information; modification commands perform changes."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_updating_records", "Updating records", `<h2>Updating records</h2>
<p>Updating records means changing values in existing rows. SQL uses UPDATE with SET to assign new values. A WHERE condition normally identifies which records should change.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>UPDATE Students
SET Age = 17
WHERE StudentID = 101;</code></pre>
<p>This changes the Age value for the student whose StudentID is 101.</p>
<h3>Important precaution</h3>
<p>An UPDATE statement without a WHERE condition updates every row in the table. Check the condition before executing a change.</p>
<pre><code>-- Changes Age for every student:
UPDATE Students
SET Age = 17;</code></pre>
<p><strong>Remember:</strong> SET states the new value; WHERE identifies the target records.</p>`, [
{q:"Which SQL command changes existing values in a table?",hint:"It works with the SET keyword.",steps:"1. Identify the task as changing existing data. 2. Recall the command paired with SET. 3. Name it.",ans:"UPDATE.",why:"UPDATE modifies existing records."},
{q:"What does WHERE StudentID = 101 do in an UPDATE statement?",hint:"It identifies which rows should be changed.",steps:"1. Read the condition. 2. Compare StudentID values in the table. 3. Select the matching row or rows.",ans:"It limits the update to records whose StudentID is 101.",why:"WHERE specifies the target records for the operation."},
{q:"What happens if UPDATE Students SET Age = 17; is executed without WHERE?",hint:"Ask whether any row is excluded.",steps:"1. Identify the table being updated. 2. Notice there is no WHERE condition. 3. Determine the scope of SET.",ans:"The Age field is set to 17 for every record in Students.",why:"Without WHERE, the update applies to all rows."},
{q:"Correct this query so it changes the age to 18 only for StudentID 105: UPDATE Students SET Age = 18;",hint:"Add a condition identifying StudentID 105.",steps:"1. Keep UPDATE Students. 2. Keep SET Age = 18. 3. Add WHERE StudentID = 105.",ans:"UPDATE Students SET Age = 18 WHERE StudentID = 105;",why:"The WHERE condition restricts the update to the intended student."},
{q:"Explain the roles of SET and WHERE in UPDATE.",hint:"One specifies the change and one specifies the target.",steps:"1. Read the assignment after SET. 2. Read the condition after WHERE. 3. Describe each role.",ans:"SET specifies the new field value; WHERE identifies the records to update.",why:"Both parts help ensure that the intended data is changed."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_deleting_records", "Deleting records", `<h2>Deleting records</h2>
<p>Deleting records removes rows from a table. In SQL, DELETE FROM identifies the table and WHERE identifies the rows to remove.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>DELETE FROM Students
WHERE StudentID = 101;</code></pre>
<p>This removes the record for StudentID 101, assuming the database permits the deletion under its constraints.</p>
<h3>Danger of omitting WHERE</h3>
<pre><code>DELETE FROM Students;</code></pre>
<p>This deletes all rows in Students. The table structure remains, but its records are removed.</p>
<p>Before deleting, verify the condition and consider whether other tables refer to the record. Foreign-key rules may prevent deletion or apply configured actions.</p>`, [
{q:"Which SQL command removes records from a table?",hint:"It begins with DELETE FROM.",steps:"1. Identify the operation as removal. 2. Recall its SQL syntax. 3. Name the command.",ans:"DELETE FROM.",why:"DELETE FROM removes rows from a table."},
{q:"What does DELETE FROM Students WHERE StudentID = 101; remove?",hint:"Focus on the condition after WHERE.",steps:"1. Identify Students as the table. 2. Identify StudentID 101 as the condition. 3. State which rows match.",ans:"The row or rows matching StudentID 101; a primary key means at most one row.",why:"WHERE restricts deletion to matching records."},
{q:"What is the effect of DELETE FROM Students; without WHERE?",hint:"No row-selection condition is present.",steps:"1. Identify the target table. 2. Notice that WHERE is absent. 3. Determine which rows the command targets.",ans:"It deletes every record in Students.",why:"Without WHERE, the deletion applies to all rows."},
{q:"Why should you check a DELETE statement before running it?",hint:"Consider whether removed rows can be recovered easily.",steps:"1. Check the target table. 2. Check the WHERE condition. 3. Consider the impact on the selected records and related tables.",ans:"To avoid removing unintended records and to check effects on related data.",why:"Deletion can cause data loss or violate relationship constraints."},
{q:"How does DELETE FROM Students WHERE StudentID = 101; differ from dropping the Students table?",hint:"Compare removing rows with removing the table itself.",steps:"1. Identify DELETE as a row operation. 2. Identify dropping a table as a structural operation. 3. State what remains after each.",ans:"DELETE removes selected records but leaves the table structure; dropping the table removes the table itself.",why:"Data deletion and table removal are different database operations."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_sorting_records", "Sorting records", `<h2>Sorting records</h2>
<p>Sorting arranges query results in a chosen order, such as alphabetical order or lowest to highest. In SQL, ORDER BY specifies the field used to sort the results.</p>
<h3>Ascending order</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT FullName, Age
FROM Students
ORDER BY FullName ASC;</code></pre>
<p><code>ASC</code> means ascending order. It is the default sort direction in SQL when no direction is specified.</p>
<h3>Descending order</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT FullName, Age
FROM Students
ORDER BY Age DESC;</code></pre>
<p><code>DESC</code> means descending order. For numeric values, this generally puts larger values before smaller ones.</p>
<p>Sorting changes the order in which results are presented, not the stored values themselves.</p>`, [
{q:"Which SQL clause sorts query results?",hint:"It is followed by a field name.",steps:"1. Identify the need to order results. 2. Recall the SQL sorting clause. 3. State it.",ans:"ORDER BY.",why:"ORDER BY arranges the returned records according to specified fields."},
{q:"What does ORDER BY FullName ASC mean?",hint:"Interpret ASC as a direction.",steps:"1. Identify FullName as the sort field. 2. Interpret ASC as ascending. 3. Describe the resulting order.",ans:"The results are sorted by FullName in ascending order, generally alphabetical for text.",why:"ASC requests ascending sorting."},
{q:"How would you sort Students by Age from highest to lowest?",hint:"Use ORDER BY and descending order.",steps:"1. Select the table. 2. Add ORDER BY Age. 3. Use DESC for descending order.",ans:"SELECT * FROM Students ORDER BY Age DESC;",why:"DESC sorts numeric ages from larger to smaller."},
{q:"Does sorting a SELECT result change the stored ages of students?",hint:"Sorting changes order, not the values.",steps:"1. Identify ORDER BY as a presentation-order clause. 2. Check whether any assignment is made. 3. State the effect on stored data.",ans:"No. It changes the order of the returned results, not the stored ages.",why:"ORDER BY does not update records."},
{q:"Write a query to display StudentID and FullName sorted by StudentID from lowest to highest.",hint:"Use SELECT, FROM and ORDER BY with ASC.",steps:"1. Select StudentID and FullName. 2. Read from Students. 3. Sort by StudentID ascending.",ans:"SELECT StudentID, FullName FROM Students ORDER BY StudentID ASC;",why:"The query returns the requested columns ordered by increasing StudentID."}
]);
add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_filtering_records", "Filtering records", `<h2>Filtering records</h2>
<p>Filtering records means selecting only the records that satisfy a specified condition. In SQL, the WHERE clause is commonly used to filter query results.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT FullName, Age
FROM Students
WHERE Age > 16;</code></pre>
<p>This retrieves the names and ages of students whose ages are greater than 16.</p>
<h3>Common comparison operators</h3>
<ul>
<li><code>=</code> Equal to</li>
<li><code>&lt;&gt;</code> Not equal to</li>
<li><code>&gt;</code> Greater than</li>
<li><code>&lt;</code> Less than</li>
<li><code>&gt;=</code> Greater than or equal to</li>
<li><code>&lt;=</code> Less than or equal to</li>
</ul>
<h3>Combining conditions</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE Age >= 16 AND ClassID = 2;</code></pre>
<p>AND requires both conditions to be true. OR requires at least one condition to be true.</p>
<p><strong>Remember:</strong> filtering determines which records appear in the result; it does not delete the other records.</p>`, [
{q:"Write a query to retrieve all students whose Age is less than 18.",hint:"Use WHERE with the less-than operator.",steps:"1. Select the records from Students. 2. Add a WHERE condition on Age. 3. Use the less-than operator with 18.",ans:"SELECT * FROM Students WHERE Age < 18;",why:"The condition selects only records whose Age is below 18."},
{q:"What is the difference between Age >= 16 and Age > 16?",hint:"Check whether a value of exactly 16 qualifies.",steps:"1. Test Age = 16 against the first condition. 2. Test it against the second. 3. Compare the results.",ans:"Age >= 16 includes 16; Age > 16 excludes 16.",why:"The >= operator includes equality, while > requires a strictly greater value."},
{q:"A query uses WHERE Age >= 16 AND ClassID = 2. A student is 17 but belongs to ClassID 3. Is the record returned?",hint:"AND requires both conditions to be true.",steps:"1. Check whether Age >= 16 is true. 2. Check whether ClassID = 2 is true. 3. Apply the AND rule.",ans:"No. The class condition is false.",why:"Both conditions must be true for an AND expression to match."},
{q:"What is the purpose of WHERE in a SELECT query?",hint:"Consider which rows qualify for the result.",steps:"1. Identify the condition following WHERE. 2. Compare each record against it. 3. Explain which records are returned.",ans:"It filters the results to records satisfying the specified condition.",why:"WHERE restricts the rows returned by a query."},
{q:"A teacher wants students who are in ClassID 1 or ClassID 3. Write the condition.",hint:"Use OR because either class is acceptable.",steps:"1. Check for ClassID = 1. 2. Check for ClassID = 3. 3. Join the conditions with OR.",ans:"WHERE ClassID = 1 OR ClassID = 3",why:"OR allows a record to qualify when either condition is true."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_purpose_of_sql", "Purpose of SQL", `<h2>Purpose of SQL</h2>
<p>SQL stands for Structured Query Language. It is used to communicate with relational database management systems.</p>
<h3>What SQL can do</h3>
<ul>
<li>Create database tables and define their structure.</li>
<li>Insert new records.</li>
<li>Retrieve information from tables.</li>
<li>Update existing records.</li>
<li>Delete records.</li>
<li>Control access and manage transactions in systems that support those features.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Examples of SQL commands</h3>
<table><tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr><tr><td>CREATE TABLE</td><td>Creates a table.</td></tr><tr><td>SELECT</td><td>Retrieves data.</td></tr><tr><td>INSERT</td><td>Adds records.</td></tr><tr><td>UPDATE</td><td>Changes existing records.</td></tr><tr><td>DELETE</td><td>Removes records.</td></tr></table>
<p>SQL is a language, not a database itself. A relational database system processes SQL statements to work with stored data.</p>`, [
{q:"What does SQL stand for?",hint:"Recall the full name of the database language.",steps:"1. Expand the letters S, Q and L. 2. State the complete phrase.",ans:"Structured Query Language.",why:"SQL is the standard language commonly used to work with relational databases."},
{q:"Which SQL command retrieves records?",hint:"It is used to request data from a table.",steps:"1. Identify the task as reading information. 2. Match it to the relevant SQL command.",ans:"SELECT.",why:"SELECT retrieves specified data from database tables."},
{q:"Is SQL itself a database management system?",hint:"Distinguish a language from the software that processes it.",steps:"1. Identify SQL as a language. 2. Identify an RDBMS as software for managing relational data. 3. Compare their roles.",ans:"No. SQL is a language used to communicate with database systems.",why:"A database system processes SQL statements; SQL is not itself the stored database."},
{q:"Which SQL command changes the age of an existing student?",hint:"Choose the command that modifies existing data.",steps:"1. Identify the task as changing a stored value. 2. Recall the command used with SET.",ans:"UPDATE.",why:"UPDATE changes values in existing records."},
{q:"A developer needs to create a table and then add a record. Which two SQL operations are needed?",hint:"One defines the structure; the other adds a row.",steps:"1. Choose a command to define the table. 2. Choose a command to add a record. 3. State both in order.",ans:"CREATE TABLE, followed by INSERT INTO.",why:"The table must be defined before records can be inserted into it."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_select_statements", "SELECT statements", `<h2>SELECT statements</h2>
<p>SELECT is an SQL statement used to retrieve specified columns from one or more tables. It can return all rows or work with conditions and sorting.</p>
<h3>Select all columns</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT *
FROM Students;</code></pre>
<p>The asterisk means all columns.</p>
<h3>Select specific columns</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT StudentID, FullName
FROM Students;</code></pre>
<p>This returns only StudentID and FullName.</p>
<h3>Select with a condition</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT FullName
FROM Students
WHERE Age = 17;</code></pre>
<p>This returns the names of students whose Age is 17.</p>
<h3>Select and sort</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT FullName, Age
FROM Students
ORDER BY Age DESC;</code></pre>
<p>ORDER BY sorts the returned rows. A SELECT query does not normally change the stored records.</p>`, [
{q:"What does the asterisk in SELECT * FROM Students; mean?",hint:"It is a shortcut for the fields in the table.",steps:"1. Locate the asterisk after SELECT. 2. Interpret it as all columns. 3. State what the query retrieves.",ans:"All columns from Students.",why:"The asterisk requests every column in the table."},
{q:"Write a query to retrieve StudentID and FullName from Students.",hint:"List both field names after SELECT.",steps:"1. Write SELECT. 2. List the required columns separated by a comma. 3. Specify the table using FROM.",ans:"SELECT StudentID, FullName FROM Students;",why:"The SELECT list determines which columns are returned."},
{q:"Write a query to retrieve the names of students whose Age is 17.",hint:"Combine SELECT, FROM and WHERE.",steps:"1. Select FullName. 2. Use Students as the table. 3. Filter with Age = 17.",ans:"SELECT FullName FROM Students WHERE Age = 17;",why:"The condition limits the returned names to students aged 17."},
{q:"What is the difference between SELECT * and SELECT FullName?",hint:"Compare the number of columns requested.",steps:"1. Identify what * represents. 2. Identify what FullName represents. 3. Compare the returned columns.",ans:"SELECT * returns all columns; SELECT FullName returns only the FullName column.",why:"Selecting specific columns avoids retrieving unnecessary fields."},
{q:"Correct this query so it retrieves names and sorts them alphabetically: SELECT FullName Students ORDER FullName;",hint:"Check the FROM clause and the sorting keywords.",steps:"1. Add FROM before Students. 2. Replace ORDER with ORDER BY. 3. Specify ascending order.",ans:"SELECT FullName FROM Students ORDER BY FullName ASC;",why:"The query needs a valid table clause and the complete ORDER BY clause."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_where_conditions", "WHERE conditions", `<h2>WHERE conditions</h2>
<p>WHERE specifies a condition that records must satisfy to appear in a query result or be affected by an UPDATE or DELETE statement.</p>
<h3>Equality condition</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE ClassID = 2;</code></pre>
<h3>Multiple conditions</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE Age >= 16 AND ClassID = 2;</code></pre>
<h3>Text values</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE FullName = 'Amina Otieno';</code></pre>
<p>Text values are commonly enclosed in single quotation marks. Numeric values are normally written without quotation marks when the field is numeric.</p>
<h3>Conditions and changes</h3>
<pre><code>UPDATE Students
SET Age = 18
WHERE StudentID = 101;</code></pre>
<p>Always check WHERE carefully in UPDATE and DELETE statements. If WHERE is omitted, the operation generally applies to every row in the table.</p>`, [
{q:"Write a condition to find records where ClassID equals 4.",hint:"Use equality with the ClassID field.",steps:"1. Name the field. 2. Use the equality operator. 3. Provide the required value.",ans:"WHERE ClassID = 4",why:"The condition matches records whose ClassID is 4."},
{q:"Why is 'Amina Otieno' enclosed in quotation marks in an SQL condition?",hint:"Identify the data type of the name.",steps:"1. Recognise the value as text. 2. Recall common SQL text-literal syntax. 3. Explain the role of quotation marks.",ans:"Because it is a text value.",why:"Quotation marks distinguish a text literal from a field name or other SQL token."},
{q:"A query uses Age > 15 AND Age < 18. Which integer ages satisfy both conditions?",hint:"The age must be greater than 15 and less than 18.",steps:"1. List integer values above 15. 2. Remove values that are 18 or greater. 3. State the remaining age.",ans:"Age 16 and age 17.",why:"Both comparisons must be true, so 16 and 17 qualify."},
{q:"What happens when a DELETE statement has no WHERE condition?",hint:"Consider how many rows match when there is no filter.",steps:"1. Identify the target table. 2. Notice the missing filter. 3. Determine the scope of the deletion.",ans:"All rows in the target table are deleted.",why:"Without WHERE, DELETE normally applies to every record in the table."},
{q:"A query should return students whose ClassID is 2 or whose Age is at least 18. Write the WHERE condition.",hint:"Either condition can qualify a record.",steps:"1. Write ClassID = 2. 2. Write Age >= 18. 3. Join them with OR.",ans:"WHERE ClassID = 2 OR Age >= 18",why:"OR returns records when either condition is true."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_order_by", "ORDER BY", `<h2>ORDER BY</h2>
<p>ORDER BY sorts query results using one or more columns. ASC sorts in ascending order and DESC sorts in descending order.</p>
<h3>Ascending order</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT FullName
FROM Students
ORDER BY FullName ASC;</code></pre>
<h3>Descending order</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT FullName, Age
FROM Students
ORDER BY Age DESC;</code></pre>
<h3>Sort using two fields</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT ClassID, FullName
FROM Students
ORDER BY ClassID ASC, FullName ASC;</code></pre>
<p>This sorts by ClassID first. Within records with the same ClassID, it sorts by FullName.</p>
<p>ORDER BY changes the order of the query results, not the stored field values. Without ORDER BY, do not assume the database will return records in a particular order.</p>`, [
{q:"What does DESC mean in ORDER BY Age DESC?",hint:"It is the reverse of ascending order.",steps:"1. Identify Age as the sort field. 2. Interpret DESC. 3. Describe the result order.",ans:"Descending order, generally from the highest age to the lowest.",why:"DESC sorts values in descending order."},
{q:"Write a query to list books by Title in ascending order.",hint:"Use ORDER BY Title ASC.",steps:"1. Select the required columns. 2. Specify Books after FROM. 3. Sort by Title ascending.",ans:"SELECT * FROM Books ORDER BY Title ASC;",why:"ORDER BY Title ASC requests ascending order by title."},
{q:"In ORDER BY ClassID ASC, FullName ASC, which field is sorted first?",hint:"The first sort field has priority.",steps:"1. Read the field list after ORDER BY. 2. Identify the first field. 3. Explain the secondary sort.",ans:"ClassID is sorted first; FullName sorts records within matching ClassID values.",why:"Multiple sort fields are applied in the order listed."},
{q:"Does ORDER BY Age DESC change a student's age in the database?",hint:"Distinguish arranging results from updating data.",steps:"1. Identify ORDER BY as sorting. 2. Check for any assignment to Age. 3. State whether the stored value changes.",ans:"No. It only changes the order in which results are returned.",why:"Sorting does not update the underlying records."},
{q:"A query must display the oldest students first. Which sorting direction should be used for Age?",hint:"The largest age should appear first.",steps:"1. Identify the desired first value as the largest. 2. Choose descending order. 3. Write the sorting clause.",ans:"ORDER BY Age DESC",why:"Descending order places larger numeric values before smaller values."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_insert_statements", "INSERT statements", `<h2>INSERT statements</h2>
<p>INSERT INTO adds new records to a table. A column list makes it clear which fields receive the supplied values.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>INSERT INTO Students (StudentID, FullName, Age)
VALUES (101, 'Amina Otieno', 16);</code></pre>
<p>The first value is assigned to StudentID, the second to FullName, and the third to Age.</p>
<h3>Insert into selected columns</h3>
<pre><code>INSERT INTO Students (StudentID, FullName)
VALUES (102, 'Brian Kamau');</code></pre>
<p>This is valid only if omitted fields can accept NULL or have suitable defaults, and the table's other constraints are satisfied.</p>
<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Providing a different number of values from the listed columns.</li>
<li>Putting values in the wrong order.</li>
<li>Using a duplicate primary key.</li>
<li>Violating a required field or data-type constraint.</li>
</ul>`, [
{q:"Write an INSERT statement to add StudentID 105, FullName 'Brian Kamau' and Age 17 to Students.",hint:"List the columns and then supply corresponding values.",steps:"1. Write INSERT INTO Students. 2. List StudentID, FullName and Age. 3. Supply 105, the text name and 17 in that order.",ans:"INSERT INTO Students (StudentID, FullName, Age) VALUES (105, 'Brian Kamau', 17);",why:"The values correspond to the listed fields in the same order."},
{q:"Why should an INSERT statement specify its column names?",hint:"Consider clarity and the order of values.",steps:"1. Identify the destination fields. 2. Match each value to its field. 3. Explain how a column list helps.",ans:"It clearly maps each supplied value to its intended field and reduces dependence on the table's column order.",why:"Explicit column names make the statement easier to understand and maintain."},
{q:"An INSERT statement lists three columns but supplies only two values. What is wrong?",hint:"Compare the number of columns and values.",steps:"1. Count the listed columns. 2. Count the supplied values. 3. Identify the mismatch.",ans:"The number of values does not match the number of listed columns.",why:"Each listed column needs a corresponding value unless the statement uses a supported alternative such as a default expression."},
{q:"A new record repeats an existing StudentID primary key. Why might insertion fail?",hint:"Recall the primary-key constraint.",steps:"1. Compare the new StudentID with stored identifiers. 2. Detect the duplicate. 3. Apply the uniqueness rule.",ans:"The insertion violates the primary key's uniqueness requirement.",why:"A primary key cannot be shared by two records."},
{q:"What is the difference between INSERT and UPDATE?",hint:"One adds a row, while the other changes existing data.",steps:"1. Define INSERT. 2. Define UPDATE. 3. Compare whether a new row is created.",ans:"INSERT adds a new record; UPDATE changes values in existing records.",why:"The commands perform different data-manipulation operations."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_update_statements", "UPDATE statements", `<h2>UPDATE statements</h2>
<p>UPDATE changes values in existing database records. SET specifies the fields and new values; WHERE identifies the records to change.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>UPDATE Students
SET Age = 17
WHERE StudentID = 105;</code></pre>
<p>This sets Age to 17 for the student with StudentID 105.</p>
<h3>Updating multiple fields</h3>
<pre><code>UPDATE Students
SET Age = 18, ClassID = 3
WHERE StudentID = 105;</code></pre>
<p>More than one field can be assigned in the same UPDATE statement.</p>
<h3>Important warning</h3>
<p>If WHERE is omitted, the update normally affects every row in the table. Confirm the condition before running the statement.</p>`, [
{q:"Write a query to change the Age of StudentID 110 to 19.",hint:"Use UPDATE, SET and WHERE.",steps:"1. Select Students as the table. 2. Set Age to 19. 3. Restrict the update to StudentID 110.",ans:"UPDATE Students SET Age = 19 WHERE StudentID = 110;",why:"The WHERE clause targets the intended student record."},
{q:"What does SET Age = 19 mean?",hint:"Identify the field and the value assigned to it.",steps:"1. Locate Age. 2. Identify 19 as the new value. 3. Describe the assignment.",ans:"It assigns the value 19 to the Age field of the targeted records.",why:"SET specifies the new value or values for the update."},
{q:"What is the effect of UPDATE Students SET Age = 18;?",hint:"Check whether the statement has a WHERE clause.",steps:"1. Identify the target table. 2. Notice WHERE is absent. 3. Determine which records are affected.",ans:"It sets Age to 18 for every record in Students.",why:"Without WHERE, all rows are targeted."},
{q:"Correct the statement to change both Age to 18 and ClassID to 3 for StudentID 105: UPDATE Students SET Age = 18 ClassID = 3 WHERE StudentID = 105;",hint:"Separate assignments in SET with a comma.",steps:"1. Keep both assignments. 2. Add a comma between them. 3. Retain the WHERE condition.",ans:"UPDATE Students SET Age = 18, ClassID = 3 WHERE StudentID = 105;",why:"SQL uses commas to separate multiple assignments in the SET clause."},
{q:"Why should a user inspect a WHERE condition before executing UPDATE?",hint:"Think about accidentally changing other records.",steps:"1. Identify which records the condition selects. 2. Consider the result if other records also match. 3. Explain the risk of omitting or miswriting the condition.",ans:"To ensure that only the intended records are changed.",why:"A missing or incorrect condition can modify many unintended records."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_delete_statements", "DELETE statements", `<h2>DELETE statements</h2>
<p>DELETE removes records from a table. WHERE is used to select which rows should be removed.</p>
<h3>Delete one identified record</h3>
<pre><code>DELETE FROM Students
WHERE StudentID = 105;</code></pre>
<p>This removes the record identified by StudentID 105 if the database allows the deletion.</p>
<h3>Delete records matching a condition</h3>
<pre><code>DELETE FROM Students
WHERE Age < 13;</code></pre>
<p>This removes every student record whose Age is less than 13.</p>
<h3>Delete all records</h3>
<pre><code>DELETE FROM Students;</code></pre>
<p>This removes all rows from Students but normally leaves the table structure in place.</p>
<p>Related tables and foreign-key constraints may affect whether a record can be deleted. Always confirm the table and condition before executing DELETE.</p>`, [
{q:"Write a statement to delete the record with StudentID 120.",hint:"Use DELETE FROM with a WHERE condition.",steps:"1. Name the table. 2. Add a condition for StudentID 120. 3. Complete the DELETE statement.",ans:"DELETE FROM Students WHERE StudentID = 120;",why:"The condition limits deletion to the identified record."},
{q:"What does DELETE FROM Students WHERE Age < 13; do?",hint:"Identify the rows satisfying the age condition.",steps:"1. Identify Students as the target table. 2. Evaluate Age < 13 for each record. 3. Remove records satisfying the condition.",ans:"It deletes all student records whose Age is less than 13.",why:"DELETE removes every row matching the WHERE condition."},
{q:"What is the risk of running DELETE FROM Students;?",hint:"There is no WHERE condition.",steps:"1. Check for WHERE. 2. Notice that it is absent. 3. Determine how many rows are targeted.",ans:"All records in Students are deleted.",why:"Without WHERE, the command targets every row in the table."},
{q:"Does DELETE FROM Students WHERE StudentID = 120; normally remove the table structure?",hint:"Distinguish deleting records from deleting a table.",steps:"1. Identify DELETE as a row-removal command. 2. Consider whether the table definition is targeted. 3. State what remains.",ans:"No. It removes matching rows but leaves the table structure.",why:"DELETE removes records, whereas dropping a table removes its definition and data."},
{q:"Why might deleting a student record fail when other tables refer to that student?",hint:"Think about foreign keys and referential integrity.",steps:"1. Identify the student record. 2. Identify records in other tables that reference it. 3. Consider whether the database permits an orphaned reference.",ans:"A foreign-key constraint may prevent deletion to preserve referential integrity.",why:"The database may require related records to be handled before the referenced record can be deleted."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_aggregate_functions", "Aggregate functions", `<h2>Aggregate functions</h2>
<p>Aggregate functions calculate a result from values in a set of records. They are commonly used with SELECT to summarise data.</p>
<h3>Common aggregate functions</h3>
<ul>
<li><code>COUNT()</code>: counts rows or non-NULL values, depending on the expression.</li>
<li><code>SUM()</code>: adds numeric values.</li>
<li><code>AVG()</code>: calculates the average of numeric values.</li>
<li><code>MIN()</code>: returns the minimum value.</li>
<li><code>MAX()</code>: returns the maximum value.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Examples</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Students;
SELECT AVG(Mark) FROM Results;
SELECT MAX(Mark) FROM Results;
SELECT MIN(Mark) FROM Results;
SELECT SUM(Mark) FROM Results;</code></pre>
<p>COUNT(*) counts rows. Most aggregate functions ignore NULL values in the expression they process. AVG and SUM are intended for numeric values.</p>
<h3>Filtering before aggregation</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT AVG(Mark)
FROM Results
WHERE SubjectID = 2;</code></pre>
<p>This calculates the average Mark for records matching SubjectID 2.</p>`, [
{q:"Which aggregate function counts the rows in a table?",hint:"It begins with COUNT.",steps:"1. Identify the task as counting records. 2. Recall the aggregate function for counting. 3. State its expression.",ans:"COUNT(*).",why:"COUNT(*) counts rows in the query result."},
{q:"Which function calculates the average of marks?",hint:"Choose the function for the arithmetic mean.",steps:"1. Identify marks as numeric values. 2. Recall the average function. 3. Apply it to the Mark field.",ans:"AVG(Mark).",why:"AVG calculates the average of numeric values in the selected set."},
{q:"A teacher wants the highest mark in Results. Write the query.",hint:"Use MAX on the Mark field.",steps:"1. Select MAX(Mark). 2. Read from Results. 3. Complete the statement.",ans:"SELECT MAX(Mark) FROM Results;",why:"MAX returns the largest non-NULL value of the specified expression."},
{q:"What is the difference between SUM(Mark) and AVG(Mark)?",hint:"One totals the values; the other calculates their mean.",steps:"1. Define SUM. 2. Define AVG. 3. Contrast the results.",ans:"SUM adds the marks; AVG calculates their average.",why:"The functions answer different questions about the same numeric data."},
{q:"Write a query to count the result records for SubjectID 3.",hint:"Filter with WHERE before counting.",steps:"1. Select COUNT(*). 2. Use Results as the table. 3. Add WHERE SubjectID = 3.",ans:"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Results WHERE SubjectID = 3;",why:"WHERE restricts the rows counted to those belonging to SubjectID 3."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_joining_tables", "Joining tables", `<h2>Joining tables</h2>
<p>A JOIN combines related rows from two or more tables using a matching condition. It allows a query to retrieve information stored across related tables.</p>
<h3>Example tables</h3>
<p>Students contains StudentID and FullName. Results contains StudentID, SubjectID and Mark. StudentID connects each result to a student.</p>
<h3>INNER JOIN</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT Students.FullName, Results.Mark
FROM Students
INNER JOIN Results
ON Students.StudentID = Results.StudentID;</code></pre>
<p>This returns matching student and result records. A student without a matching result is not included in this INNER JOIN result.</p>
<h3>Why use joins?</h3>
<ul>
<li>Retrieve related information stored in separate tables.</li>
<li>Avoid unnecessarily repeating the same details in many records.</li>
<li>Produce reports that combine fields from related entities.</li>
</ul>
<p>The ON condition specifies how the rows are related. A wrong join condition can produce incorrect or excessive combinations of rows.</p>`, [
{q:"What is the purpose of a JOIN in SQL?",hint:"Think about information stored across related tables.",steps:"1. Identify the tables involved. 2. Identify the related fields. 3. Explain what the query combines.",ans:"It combines related rows from multiple tables using a matching condition.",why:"JOIN retrieves connected information without requiring all details to be stored in one table."},
{q:"Students and Results both contain StudentID. How can the tables be joined?",hint:"Match the identifier in each table.",steps:"1. Identify Students.StudentID. 2. Identify Results.StudentID. 3. Use an ON condition to match them.",ans:"ON Students.StudentID = Results.StudentID",why:"The matching identifier connects result records to the corresponding students."},
{q:"What does an INNER JOIN return when a student has no matching result record?",hint:"An INNER JOIN keeps matching rows.",steps:"1. Check whether a matching Results row exists. 2. Apply the INNER JOIN rule. 3. Decide whether that student appears in the joined result.",ans:"That student is not included in the INNER JOIN result.",why:"INNER JOIN returns rows for which the join condition finds a match."},
{q:"Write a query to display student names and marks by joining Students and Results on StudentID.",hint:"Select the two fields and use INNER JOIN with ON.",steps:"1. Select Students.FullName and Results.Mark. 2. Use Students as the first table. 3. Join Results on matching StudentID values.",ans:"SELECT Students.FullName, Results.Mark FROM Students INNER JOIN Results ON Students.StudentID = Results.StudentID;",why:"The join matches each result to the student identified by the same StudentID."},
{q:"Why can an incorrect ON condition produce misleading query results?",hint:"The join depends on matching the correct related fields.",steps:"1. Identify the intended relationship. 2. Consider what happens if unrelated fields are matched. 3. Explain how the returned combinations can become incorrect.",ans:"It can match unrelated records and produce incorrect or excessive combinations of rows.",why:"The ON condition must reflect the actual relationship between the tables."}
]);
add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_purpose_of_database_forms", "Purpose of database forms", `<h2>Purpose of database forms</h2>
<p>A database form is a user interface used to enter, view or edit information stored in database tables. Forms make database interaction easier for users who do not need to work directly with tables or SQL.</p>
<h3>Main purposes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Provide an organised layout for entering data.</li>
<li>Display selected records in a readable format.</li>
<li>Allow authorised users to edit existing records.</li>
<li>Reduce data-entry errors through validation controls.</li>
<li>Make database operations easier for non-technical users.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A school admission form may contain fields for StudentID, FullName, DateOfBirth and ClassID. A user fills in the fields and submits the form to save the information.</p>
<h3>Forms and tables</h3>
<p>A table stores the actual records. A form provides an interface for interacting with those records. A form does not replace the underlying database table.</p>
<p><strong>Remember:</strong> forms support data entry and interaction; tables organise and store the data.</p>`, [
{q:"What is a database form?",hint:"Think about the interface a user interacts with.",steps:"1. Identify where the data is stored. 2. Identify the interface used to enter or view it. 3. Define the form.",ans:"A user interface for entering, viewing or editing database information.",why:"Forms make database interaction easier without requiring users to work directly with tables."},
{q:"Give two purposes of a database form.",hint:"Consider entering information and working with existing records.",steps:"1. Identify one task involving new data. 2. Identify another task involving stored data.",ans:"Entering new records and viewing or editing existing records.",why:"Forms provide a structured interface for working with database information."},
{q:"A school clerk enters new student details through a form. Where is the information normally stored?",hint:"Distinguish the interface from the data store.",steps:"1. Identify the form as the interface. 2. Identify the database table as the record store. 3. State where the record is saved.",ans:"In the relevant database table, when the form submission is successfully saved.",why:"The form provides access to the underlying data store."},
{q:"How can a form help reduce data-entry errors?",hint:"Think about required fields and permitted values.",steps:"1. Identify a possible input error. 2. Apply a form control or validation rule. 3. Explain how it prevents or detects the error.",ans:"It can use required fields, input controls and validation rules to detect or prevent invalid entries.",why:"Well-designed forms guide users and enforce applicable input rules."},
{q:"Why might a school use a form instead of allowing every clerk to edit database tables directly?",hint:"Consider usability and control.",steps:"1. Consider how a form guides the user. 2. Consider which fields the user can access. 3. Explain the benefit.",ans:"A form can provide a simpler, more controlled interface for entering and editing authorised information.",why:"Forms can make data entry easier and reduce accidental changes to unrelated fields."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_entering_records_through_forms", "Entering records through forms", `<h2>Entering records through forms</h2>
<p>Entering records through forms means supplying data into form fields and submitting it so that a database application can save a new record.</p>
<h3>Typical process</h3>
<ol>
<li>Open the appropriate form.</li>
<li>Enter the required values in the correct fields.</li>
<li>Check that the values are complete and correctly formatted.</li>
<li>Submit or save the form.</li>
<li>Confirm that the record was saved successfully.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A student registration form requests StudentID, FullName and Age. The clerk enters the details and saves the record.</p>
<h3>Common problems</h3>
<ul>
<li>Leaving a required field blank.</li>
<li>Entering a value in the wrong field.</li>
<li>Using an invalid data format.</li>
<li>Entering an identifier that already exists.</li>
<li>Closing the form without saving.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Remember:</strong> entering values into a form does not always mean the record has been saved. Confirm successful submission.</p>`, [
{q:"What should a user do after entering all required values in a database form?",hint:"The information must be committed to the database.",steps:"1. Check the entered values. 2. Submit or save the form. 3. Confirm that saving succeeded.",ans:"Submit or save the form and confirm that the record was saved successfully.",why:"Entered values may not be stored until the form is submitted or saved."},
{q:"A required StudentID field is blank and the form rejects submission. Why?",hint:"Consider the form's validation rules.",steps:"1. Identify StudentID as required. 2. Check whether a value was supplied. 3. Apply the required-field rule.",ans:"The required-field validation rule is not satisfied.",why:"Required fields must contain acceptable values before submission."},
{q:"A clerk enters a student's age in the FullName field. What type of problem has occurred?",hint:"The value is placed in the wrong field.",steps:"1. Identify the intended meaning of FullName. 2. Compare it with the entered age. 3. State the error.",ans:"The data has been entered into the wrong field.",why:"Each value must be entered into the field intended to store that information."},
{q:"Why should a clerk check for a duplicate StudentID before saving a record?",hint:"Recall the purpose of a primary key.",steps:"1. Identify StudentID as the record identifier. 2. Check whether the value already exists. 3. Explain the effect of duplication.",ans:"To avoid violating the primary key's uniqueness requirement.",why:"A primary key must uniquely identify each record."},
{q:"A clerk fills out a form but closes it without saving. What may happen?",hint:"Distinguish entering data from storing it.",steps:"1. Identify the values as entered but unsaved. 2. Consider what closing the form can do. 3. State the likely consequence.",ans:"The new record may not be stored, and the entered values may be lost.",why:"A record normally needs to be saved or submitted before it is persisted."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_designing_queries", "Designing queries", `<h2>Designing queries</h2>
<p>A database query is a request for specific information or an operation on database data. Designing a query involves deciding what information is needed and how to retrieve the correct records.</p>
<h3>Steps in query design</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the information required.</li>
<li>Identify the table or tables containing the information.</li>
<li>Select the fields to display.</li>
<li>Specify conditions for selecting records.</li>
<li>Choose a sorting order if necessary.</li>
<li>Run the query and check the results.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Task: display the names of students aged 16 or older, ordered alphabetically.</p>
<pre><code>SELECT FullName
FROM Students
WHERE Age >= 16
ORDER BY FullName ASC;</code></pre>
<p>The query selects the required field, filters the records and sorts the results.</p>
<h3>Query versus form</h3>
<p>A query requests or manipulates database information. A form provides an interface for users to enter, view or edit information.</p>`, [
{q:"What is a database query?",hint:"Think of a request made to a database.",steps:"1. Identify the information needed. 2. Identify the request sent to the database. 3. Define a query.",ans:"A request to retrieve or manipulate information in a database.",why:"Queries let users obtain specific information or perform database operations."},
{q:"A teacher needs names of students whose Age is at least 16. Which field and condition are required?",hint:"Identify the displayed information and the selection rule.",steps:"1. Select FullName for display. 2. Use Age as the condition field. 3. Set the condition to Age >= 16.",ans:"Select FullName with the condition Age >= 16.",why:"The field determines what is displayed, while the condition determines which records qualify."},
{q:"Why should a user identify the required fields before writing a query?",hint:"Not every column may be needed.",steps:"1. Identify the task's required information. 2. Match it to table fields. 3. Explain how this affects the SELECT list.",ans:"To retrieve the relevant information without unnecessarily displaying unrelated columns.",why:"Choosing appropriate fields makes the results focused and easier to use."},
{q:"A query returns too many records because it has no selection condition. Which SQL clause could restrict the results?",hint:"This clause filters rows.",steps:"1. Identify the unwanted extra rows. 2. Decide what distinguishes the required records. 3. Apply the filtering clause.",ans:"WHERE.",why:"WHERE restricts the result to records satisfying a specified condition."},
{q:"How can you check whether a query meets the user's requirements?",hint:"Compare the output with the original request.",steps:"1. Run the query. 2. Check the returned fields. 3. Check the matching records and their order if sorting was required.",ans:"Run it and verify that the returned fields, records and order match the requirements.",why:"Testing the result helps reveal incorrect fields, conditions or sorting rules."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_generating_reports", "Generating reports", `<h2>Generating reports</h2>
<p>A database report presents selected database information in an organised format suitable for reading, printing, sharing or decision-making.</p>
<h3>Uses of reports</h3>
<ul>
<li>Present student performance results.</li>
<li>Summarise school attendance.</li>
<li>List stock items and quantities.</li>
<li>Show sales totals over a specified period.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Typical report-generation process</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the purpose and audience.</li>
<li>Select the relevant data or query.</li>
<li>Choose the fields and sorting or grouping needed.</li>
<li>Arrange the information in a readable layout.</li>
<li>Preview and check the report.</li>
<li>Print, export or share it with authorised users.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Queries and reports</h3>
<p>A query can select and calculate the information needed for a report. The report arranges that information for presentation. A report should not include unrelated or unauthorised sensitive information.</p>`, [
{q:"What is a database report?",hint:"Focus on how database information is presented.",steps:"1. Identify the data source. 2. Identify the organised presentation. 3. Define the report.",ans:"An organised presentation of selected database information.",why:"Reports make information easier to read, review and communicate."},
{q:"A school wants a printed summary of students' examination marks. Which database output is suitable?",hint:"The school needs an organised presentation for review or printing.",steps:"1. Identify the information as examination marks. 2. Identify the need for a readable summary. 3. Select the appropriate output.",ans:"A database report.",why:"Reports present selected records in a structured format suitable for printing or review."},
{q:"Why might a query be used before generating a report?",hint:"The report should contain only relevant information.",steps:"1. Identify the information required. 2. Use the query to select or calculate it. 3. Use the result as the report's data source.",ans:"To select, filter or summarise the relevant information for the report.",why:"Queries help prepare the correct data for presentation."},
{q:"Name two things that should be checked before distributing a report.",hint:"Consider accuracy and who is allowed to see it.",steps:"1. Check the correctness of the results. 2. Check the intended recipients and access permissions.",ans:"The accuracy of the information and whether recipients are authorised to receive it.",why:"Reports should be correct and should protect confidential information."},
{q:"A report includes every field in a student table, including sensitive details unrelated to its purpose. What should be improved?",hint:"Apply relevance and privacy principles.",steps:"1. Identify fields unrelated to the report's purpose. 2. Remove unnecessary fields. 3. Check access permissions.",ans:"Include only relevant fields and restrict access to sensitive information.",why:"Purposeful reporting reduces clutter and unnecessary exposure of personal information."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_presenting_database_results", "Presenting database results", `<h2>Presenting database results</h2>
<p>Presenting database results means arranging retrieved information so that users can understand and use it effectively.</p>
<h3>Good presentation practices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use clear and meaningful column headings.</li>
<li>Arrange records in a logical order.</li>
<li>Group related records when this helps understanding.</li>
<li>Use suitable totals or summaries where needed.</li>
<li>Keep the layout readable and consistent.</li>
<li>Include only information relevant to the audience.</li>
<li>Protect confidential information from unauthorised access.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A school performance report may show StudentID, StudentName, Subject and Mark. Sorting by class or grouping by subject can make the results easier to review.</p>
<h3>Accuracy matters</h3>
<p>A visually clear report can still be misleading if the underlying query is wrong, the data is outdated or totals are calculated incorrectly. Check both the data and its presentation.</p>
<p><strong>Remember:</strong> effective presentation makes accurate, relevant information easier to interpret.</p>`, [
{q:"Why should a database report use clear column headings?",hint:"Readers need to understand what each value represents.",steps:"1. Consider a column containing numbers. 2. Explain how a heading identifies their meaning. 3. State the benefit.",ans:"Headings help readers understand the meaning of each column's values.",why:"Clear labels reduce ambiguity and improve interpretation."},
{q:"A report contains 500 student records in random order. Name one change that could make it easier to use.",hint:"Think about finding and comparing records.",steps:"1. Identify the difficulty in the current layout. 2. Choose a logical ordering or grouping. 3. Explain how it helps.",ans:"Sort by StudentID or name, or group records by class.",why:"Logical ordering and grouping make records easier to locate and compare."},
{q:"Why should a report include only information relevant to its audience?",hint:"Consider readability and privacy.",steps:"1. Identify unrelated fields. 2. Consider how they affect the report. 3. Consider whether unnecessary sensitive data is exposed.",ans:"To improve clarity and avoid exposing unnecessary information.",why:"Relevant reports are easier to understand and minimise unnecessary disclosure."},
{q:"A report looks professional but displays incorrect totals. Is it reliable? Explain.",hint:"Presentation and accuracy are different qualities.",steps:"1. Identify the incorrect totals. 2. Consider the effect on conclusions. 3. Decide whether appearance can correct inaccurate data.",ans:"No. Incorrect totals make the report unreliable despite its appearance.",why:"A report must present accurate information, not merely look attractive."},
{q:"A report is intended for teachers but contains private information that they are not authorised to access. What should be done?",hint:"Consider permissions and the report's purpose.",steps:"1. Identify the unauthorised information. 2. Remove unnecessary sensitive fields or restrict access. 3. Share only an appropriate report.",ans:"Remove or restrict the sensitive information and share only data the teachers are authorised to access.",why:"Database reporting must respect privacy and access controls."}
]);
add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_database_access_permissions", "Database access permissions", `
<h2>Database access permissions</h2>
<p>Database access permissions determine what a user is allowed to do in a database. They help prevent unauthorised viewing, changing or deleting of information.</p>

<h3>Common permissions</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Read:</strong> View stored information without changing it.</li>
<li><strong>Insert:</strong> Add new records.</li>
<li><strong>Update:</strong> Change existing records.</li>
<li><strong>Delete:</strong> Remove records.</li>
<li><strong>Administrator:</strong> Manage users, permissions and database settings.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example</h3>
<p>In a school database, a class teacher may be allowed to view and update marks for their class. A learner may only be allowed to view their own results. The database administrator manages accounts and permissions.</p>

<h3>Why permissions matter</h3>
<ul>
<li>Protect confidential information.</li>
<li>Reduce accidental changes and deletion.</li>
<li>Limit users to the tasks they need to perform.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Give each user only the permissions necessary for their role.</p>
`, [
{q:"A learner can view examination results but cannot change them. Which permission does the learner have?",hint:"Think about viewing without modifying.",steps:"1. The learner can see stored information. 2. The learner cannot edit it. 3. This is read access.",ans:"Read permission.",why:"Read permission allows information to be viewed without being changed."},
{q:"Which permission allows a clerk to add a new student record?",hint:"The record does not exist yet.",steps:"1. The clerk is creating a new record. 2. Creating a new record means adding data. 3. The required permission is insert.",ans:"Insert permission.",why:"Insert permission allows users to add new records."},
{q:"A teacher needs to correct a student's misspelled name. Which permission is required?",hint:"The record already exists.",steps:"1. The student's record already exists. 2. The teacher needs to change its contents. 3. This requires update access.",ans:"Update permission.",why:"Update permission allows existing information to be changed."},
{q:"Why should an ordinary learner not have administrator permissions?",hint:"Consider the damage they could cause.",steps:"1. Administrators can manage settings and permissions. 2. Unrestricted access could expose or damage records. 3. Learners should receive only the access needed for their role.",ans:"To prevent unauthorised changes, misuse of permissions and damage to the database.",why:"Limiting permissions protects information and reduces security risks."},
{q:"A school clerk can view and add records but cannot delete them. Name the two permissions the clerk has.",hint:"Viewing and adding are different actions.",steps:"1. Viewing information requires read permission. 2. Adding records requires insert permission. 3. The clerk does not have delete permission.",ans:"Read and insert permissions.",why:"Each permission authorises a different database operation."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_authentication", "Authentication", `
<h2>Authentication</h2>
<p>Authentication is the process of verifying the identity of a person or system attempting to access a database.</p>

<h3>Common authentication methods</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Password:</strong> A secret string used to sign in.</li>
<li><strong>One-time code:</strong> A temporary code sent to or generated for the user.</li>
<li><strong>Biometrics:</strong> Identification using features such as a fingerprint or face.</li>
<li><strong>Multi-factor authentication:</strong> Using two or more different types of verification.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A teacher enters a username and password before accessing the school database. The system checks the credentials before allowing access.</p>

<h3>Authentication versus authorisation</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Authentication:</strong> Are you who you claim to be?</li>
<li><strong>Authorisation:</strong> What are you allowed to do?</li>
</ul>

<h3>Safe practices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use strong, unique passwords.</li>
<li>Never share passwords.</li>
<li>Use multi-factor authentication when available.</li>
<li>Sign out when using a shared computer.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Authentication verifies identity; it does not automatically grant permission to perform every action.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is authentication in a database system?",hint:"Focus on verifying identity.",steps:"1. A person requests access. 2. The system checks evidence of identity. 3. This verification process is authentication.",ans:"The process of verifying the identity of a person or system requesting access.",why:"Authentication checks who is attempting to access the system."},
{q:"A teacher enters a username and password. What is the system checking?",hint:"It is checking the sign-in details.",steps:"1. The teacher supplies credentials. 2. The system checks whether they are valid. 3. It is verifying the teacher's identity.",ans:"The teacher's identity through the supplied credentials.",why:"Valid credentials help the system verify the person signing in."},
{q:"Explain the difference between authentication and authorisation.",hint:"One checks identity; the other checks allowed actions.",steps:"1. Authentication asks who the user is. 2. Authorisation checks what the user may do. 3. They serve different purposes.",ans:"Authentication verifies identity, while authorisation determines which resources or actions the user is permitted to access.",why:"A verified user may still be restricted from certain database operations."},
{q:"Why is sharing a database password unsafe?",hint:"Think about accountability and unauthorised access.",steps:"1. Another person could use the password. 2. Their actions could be attributed to the account owner. 3. They could access information without permission.",ans:"It can allow unauthorised access and make it difficult to identify who performed an action.",why:"Passwords should remain private to protect accounts and accountability."},
{q:"What is multi-factor authentication?",hint:"It requires more than one type of verification.",steps:"1. A password is one verification method. 2. A temporary code or biometric can provide another. 3. Using different verification types is multi-factor authentication.",ans:"A method that verifies identity using two or more different types of authentication factors.",why:"A stolen password alone may not be enough to access an account protected by another factor."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_database_backup", "Database backup", `
<h2>Database backup</h2>
<p>A database backup is a separate copy of database information kept so that the data can be restored if the original is lost, damaged or corrupted.</p>

<h3>Why backups are important</h3>
<ul>
<li>Recover information after accidental deletion.</li>
<li>Restore data after hardware failure.</li>
<li>Recover from certain forms of corruption or cyberattack.</li>
<li>Reduce the effects of disasters such as fire or flooding.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Common backup types</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Full backup:</strong> Copies all selected data.</li>
<li><strong>Incremental backup:</strong> Copies changes made since the most recent backup of any type in the backup chain.</li>
<li><strong>Differential backup:</strong> Copies changes made since the last full backup.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Good backup practices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Back up data regularly.</li>
<li>Keep backup copies separate from the original database.</li>
<li>Protect backups with suitable access controls.</li>
<li>Test backups to confirm that data can be restored.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A school backs up its student records every evening. If the computer fails the next morning, the school can restore data from a usable backup, although changes made after that backup may need to be recovered separately.</p>

<p><strong>Remember:</strong> A backup is useful only if it is available and can be restored successfully.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a database backup?",hint:"It is a separate copy kept for recovery.",steps:"1. The original database stores the working data. 2. A backup is another copy. 3. The copy can be used to restore information after data loss.",ans:"A separate copy of database information kept for restoration if the original is lost or damaged.",why:"Backups provide a way to recover information after certain failures."},
{q:"A school loses records after its storage device fails. How can a backup help?",hint:"Think about recovering the lost information.",steps:"1. The original storage device has failed. 2. The school locates a usable backup. 3. It restores the backed-up records to a working system.",ans:"The school can restore the records from a usable backup.",why:"A backup preserves a copy of data that may otherwise be lost."},
{q:"What is the difference between a full backup and an incremental backup?",hint:"Compare how much data each copies.",steps:"1. A full backup copies all selected data. 2. An incremental backup copies changes since the most recent backup in the chain. 3. The amount copied is different.",ans:"A full backup copies all selected data, while an incremental backup copies changes since the most recent backup in the backup chain.",why:"Incremental backups usually copy less data per backup, but restoration may require the full backup and subsequent incremental backups."},
{q:"Why should a school test its database backups?",hint:"A backup file may not always restore successfully.",steps:"1. A backup may be incomplete or damaged. 2. Testing checks whether it can be restored. 3. This helps reveal problems before an emergency.",ans:"To confirm that the backup is usable and the data can be restored.",why:"A backup that cannot be restored may not help during data loss."},
{q:"Why should a backup not be kept only on the same device as the original database?",hint:"Imagine that the device is destroyed.",steps:"1. Both copies would depend on the same device. 2. A device failure or physical disaster could destroy both. 3. A separate protected location reduces this risk.",ans:"Because a failure or disaster affecting that device could destroy both the original and its backup.",why:"Keeping backups separate helps protect them from the same incident affecting the original data."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_database_recovery", "Database recovery", `
<h2>Database recovery</h2>
<p>Database recovery is the process of bringing a database back to a usable and consistent state after a failure, accidental change or data loss.</p>

<h3>Causes of database failure</h3>
<ul>
<li>Power interruption.</li>
<li>Hardware or storage failure.</li>
<li>Software errors.</li>
<li>Accidental deletion or incorrect updates.</li>
<li>Malware or other security incidents.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ways to recover a database</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Restore a backup:</strong> Replace lost or damaged data with a usable saved copy.</li>
<li><strong>Use transaction logs:</strong> Where supported, use recorded database operations to help recover changes or return to an earlier state.</li>
<li><strong>Reapply valid changes:</strong> Where the recovery system supports it, replay recorded operations after restoring a backup.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A school accidentally deletes student records. The administrator identifies a suitable backup, restores it, and checks whether any records created after that backup need to be recovered.</p>

<h3>Backup versus recovery</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Backup:</strong> Creates and keeps a copy of data.</li>
<li><strong>Recovery:</strong> Uses available methods and saved data to restore the database after a problem.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Backup helps prepare for data loss; recovery is the process of restoring the database after a problem.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is database recovery?",hint:"Think about what happens after a failure.",steps:"1. A failure or data-loss event affects the database. 2. Recovery procedures are carried out. 3. The aim is to return the database to a usable state.",ans:"The process of restoring a database to a usable and consistent state after a failure or data loss.",why:"Recovery aims to restore access to reliable database information."},
{q:"State two events that may make database recovery necessary.",hint:"Consider hardware, software and user mistakes.",steps:"1. Hardware failure can make data unavailable. 2. Accidental deletion can remove needed records. 3. Either event may require recovery.",ans:"Any two: hardware failure, power interruption, software error, accidental deletion, incorrect updates or a security incident.",why:"Different failures can damage, remove or make database information unavailable."},
{q:"Explain the difference between database backup and database recovery.",hint:"One prepares a copy; the other restores data.",steps:"1. A backup creates and stores a copy. 2. Recovery uses backups or other supported methods after a problem. 3. The processes are related but different.",ans:"Backup creates and keeps a copy of data; recovery restores the database after a problem.",why:"Having a backup supports recovery, but creating the backup is not itself the recovery process."},
{q:"A database was restored from yesterday's backup, but today's new records are missing. Why might this happen?",hint:"Consider when the backup was created.",steps:"1. The backup contains data saved up to its backup point. 2. Today's records may have been entered later. 3. They may need another recovery method or re-entry.",ans:"The records were created after the backup was made, so they were not included in that backup.",why:"Restoring an older backup does not automatically restore changes made afterward."},
{q:"After restoring a school database, what should the administrator check before allowing normal use?",hint:"Do not assume restoration means everything is correct.",steps:"1. Confirm the database opens and records are available. 2. Check data accuracy and completeness. 3. Confirm that users can access the appropriate information.",ans:"Check that the database works, records are accurate and complete, and access permissions are correct.",why:"Verification helps identify missing data or problems before normal operations resume."}
]);

add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_protecting_sensitive_records", "Protecting sensitive records", `
<h2>Protecting sensitive records</h2>
<p>Sensitive records contain information that should not be exposed to unauthorised people. Examples include student medical details, examination results, personal contact information and financial records.</p>

<h3>Ways to protect sensitive records</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Access control:</strong> Restrict records to authorised users.</li>
<li><strong>Authentication:</strong> Verify the identity of users.</li>
<li><strong>Encryption:</strong> Convert data into a form that cannot be readily understood without the appropriate key.</li>
<li><strong>Backups:</strong> Keep protected copies for recovery.</li>
<li><strong>Audit logs:</strong> Record relevant actions so activity can be reviewed.</li>
<li><strong>Physical security:</strong> Prevent unauthorised people from accessing computers and storage devices.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A school stores students' medical details in a database. Only authorised staff should access these details. The school should also protect accounts, secure stored information and keep suitable backups.</p>

<h3>Good handling practices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Collect and store only information that is necessary.</li>
<li>Do not share records with unauthorised people.</li>
<li>Use strong passwords and sign out of shared computers.</li>
<li>Keep software updated and protect backup copies.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Protecting sensitive records involves preventing unauthorised access, inappropriate disclosure, alteration and loss.</p>
`, [
{q:"What is a sensitive record? Give one example.",hint:"Think of information that could harm or embarrass someone if exposed.",steps:"1. Identify information that needs special protection. 2. Give an example such as medical details or examination results.",ans:"A record containing private or confidential information that needs protection; for example, a student's medical details.",why:"Sensitive information should be accessible only to people with a legitimate reason and appropriate permission."},
{q:"How does access control help protect sensitive records?",hint:"Consider who is allowed to view or change data.",steps:"1. Access control defines permitted users and actions. 2. Unauthorised users are restricted. 3. This reduces inappropriate access to records.",ans:"It restricts access to authorised users and limits what they can do with the records.",why:"Limiting access reduces the risk of unauthorised viewing or changes."},
{q:"What is the purpose of encrypting sensitive database information?",hint:"Think about what happens if someone obtains the stored data.",steps:"1. Encryption transforms readable data into an encoded form. 2. The appropriate key is needed to recover readable information. 3. This helps protect confidentiality.",ans:"To make the data unreadable to people who do not have the appropriate decryption key.",why:"Encryption can help protect information if stored data is accessed without authorisation, provided the keys are also protected."},
{q:"Why should a school keep audit logs for its database?",hint:"Think about investigating changes or suspicious access.",steps:"1. Audit logs record relevant actions. 2. Administrators can review the recorded activity. 3. This can help investigate errors or suspicious behaviour.",ans:"To record database activity and help investigate unauthorised access, errors or changes.",why:"Logs provide evidence that can support monitoring and investigation."},
{q:"A teacher downloads a file containing students' medical details onto a shared computer. State two precautions the teacher should take.",hint:"Consider access by other people and what happens to the downloaded file.",steps:"1. The file contains sensitive personal information. 2. Prevent other users from opening it. 3. Avoid leaving the file on a shared device after use.",ans:"Any two: restrict access to the file, store it securely, encrypt it where appropriate, avoid unnecessary local copies, and securely remove the downloaded copy when no longer needed.",why:"Sensitive records must be protected from unauthorised access and unnecessary exposure."}
]);
add("computer", "databases", "computer_databases_meaning_of_a_nosql_database", "Meaning of a NoSQL database", `
<h2>Meaning of a NoSQL database</h2>

<p>A NoSQL database is a database that uses a data model other than the traditional relational table model, or uses a non-relational approach to organising and accessing data.</p>

<h3>How it differs from a relational database</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Relational database:</strong> Organises data mainly into tables containing rows and columns.</li>
<li><strong>NoSQL database:</strong> May organise data as documents, key-value pairs, graphs or other non-relational structures.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A school relational database might store students in a table with columns for admission number, name and class.</p>

<p>A document database could store each student's information as a document:</p>

<pre>{
  "admissionNumber": "S104",
  "name": "Amina",
  "class": "Form 2"
}</pre>

<p>The document groups related information into one record-like structure without requiring the data to be represented as a traditional relational table.</p>

<h3>Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li>Can support different data structures, depending on the database type.</li>
<li>Some NoSQL databases allow records to have different fields.</li>
<li>Different NoSQL systems provide different ways of searching and updating data.</li>
<li>They can be suitable for applications with large amounts of varied or rapidly changing data.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>
<p>NoSQL does not mean that the database cannot use query languages or that it has no structure. The exact features depend on the database system.</p>

<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Relational databases organise data primarily into related tables; NoSQL databases support other data models, such as documents, key-value pairs and graphs.</p>
`, [
{
q: "What is a NoSQL database?",
hint: "Think about how its data model differs from the traditional relational model.",
steps: "1. A relational database organises data primarily into related tables. 2. NoSQL databases use other data models or non-relational approaches. 3. Examples include document, key-value and graph databases.",
ans: "A database that uses a non-relational data model, such as documents, key-value pairs or graphs.",
why: "NoSQL databases provide ways of organising data beyond the traditional relational table model."
},
{
q: "State one difference between a relational database and a NoSQL database.",
hint: "Compare their data models.",
steps: "1. Relational databases organise data mainly into tables. 2. NoSQL databases may use documents, key-value pairs or graphs. 3. Their data organisation differs.",
ans: "A relational database organises data mainly into related tables, while a NoSQL database may use documents, key-value pairs or graphs.",
why: "The principal distinction is the data model used to organise and access information."
},
{
q: "A developer stores each customer's details as a separate JSON document. Which type of database could be suitable?",
hint: "Look for the database model that stores documents.",
steps: "1. JSON represents structured data. 2. Each customer's information is stored as a document. 3. A document database is designed for this model.",
ans: "A document database, which is a type of NoSQL database.",
why: "Document databases store information as documents, often using formats such as JSON."
},
{
q: "Does NoSQL mean that a database has no structure? Explain your answer.",
hint: "Consider whether documents and key-value pairs have organisation.",
steps: "1. NoSQL databases still organise information. 2. Their structures depend on the database model. 3. Therefore, NoSQL does not mean structureless data.",
ans: "No. NoSQL databases organise data using models such as documents, key-value pairs or graphs.",
why: "NoSQL refers to non-relational data models, not the complete absence of structure."
},
{
q: "Give two examples of NoSQL database models.",
hint: "Think of the models named in the notes.",
steps: "1. Identify the document model. 2. Identify another non-relational model, such as key-value or graph. 3. State both.",
ans: "Any two: document, key-value or graph databases.",
why: "These are examples of different models used by NoSQL databases."
}
]);

add("computer", "office_word_processing", "creating_and_saving_documents", "Creating and saving documents", `<h3>Why documents must be saved</h3>
<p>A word processor is used to create and edit text documents. Saving stores the document so it can be opened and used later. Unsaved changes may be lost when the application closes or the computer shuts down unexpectedly.</p>
<h3>Creating a document</h3>
<ol>
<li>Open a word-processing application.</li>
<li>Create a blank document or choose a suitable template.</li>
<li>Enter the required text.</li>
<li>Review the content before saving.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Saving a document</h3>
<p>Choose <b>Save</b> or <b>Save As</b>. Enter a meaningful file name and select the required folder. The application may add a file extension such as <code>.docx</code> or <code>.odt</code>.</p>
<p>Use <b>Save As</b> when creating a new named copy or saving in another location or format. Use <b>Save</b> to store subsequent changes to the current file.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Create a document containing class notes. Save it as <code>Biology_Notes.docx</code> in the schoolwork folder. Later changes should be saved again.</p>`, [
{q:"Why should you save a document?",hint:"Think about what happens when the application closes.",steps:"Saving writes the current document to storage.",ans:"To preserve the document and its changes for later use.",why:"Without saving, unsaved work may be lost."},
{q:"Give two steps involved in saving a new document.",hint:"Consider the name and location.",steps:"Choose Save or Save As, then provide a file name and location.",ans:"Enter a file name and choose a folder or location.",why:"The name identifies the file and the location determines where it is stored."},
{q:"A learner changes a saved document. What should the learner do to keep the changes?",hint:"Use the command that updates the current file.",steps:"Choose Save after making the changes.",ans:"Save the document again.",why:"The earlier saved version does not automatically contain every later change."},
{q:"What is one purpose of a file extension such as .docx?",hint:"Look at the end of a file name.",steps:"The extension helps identify the file format.",ans:"It indicates the file format.",why:"Applications use file formats to determine how document content is stored."},
{q:"You need to keep the original document and create a separately named version. Which command is suitable?",hint:"Choose the command that lets you specify a new name or location.",steps:"Use Save As and enter a different file name.",ans:"Save As.",why:"It allows a separate copy to be saved without replacing the original file."}
]);

add("computer", "office_word_processing", "editing_text", "Editing text", `<h3>What text editing means</h3>
<p>Editing changes the content of a document to correct mistakes, improve meaning or reorganise information. It is different from formatting, which changes appearance.</p>
<h3>Common editing actions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Insert:</b> add missing text.</li>
<li><b>Delete:</b> remove unwanted text.</li>
<li><b>Select:</b> mark text to act on it.</li>
<li><b>Cut:</b> remove selected content and place it on the clipboard.</li>
<li><b>Copy:</b> place a duplicate on the clipboard without removing the original.</li>
<li><b>Paste:</b> insert clipboard content at the cursor.</li>
<li><b>Undo:</b> reverse a recent action.</li>
<li><b>Find and Replace:</b> locate text and optionally substitute it.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Original: “The student submit the report.” Edited: “The student submits the report.” Only the incorrect word ending was changed; the sentence's meaning was preserved.</p>
<h3>Safe editing</h3>
<p>Select the correct text before deleting or replacing it. Use Undo if an unintended change occurs, then review the document.</p>`, [
{q:"What is the difference between editing and formatting?",hint:"One changes content; the other changes appearance.",steps:"Compare changing a word with changing its font.",ans:"Editing changes content; formatting changes how content looks.",why:"Correcting a word is editing, while making it bold is formatting."},
{q:"You want to duplicate a paragraph elsewhere without removing the original. Which actions should you use?",hint:"The original must remain.",steps:"Select the paragraph, copy it, then paste it at the destination.",ans:"Copy and Paste.",why:"Copy creates a duplicate on the clipboard while preserving the original."},
{q:"What does Undo do?",hint:"It reverses a recent action.",steps:"Use Undo to reverse the last supported change.",ans:"It reverses a recent editing action.",why:"Undo helps recover from unintended changes."},
{q:"A document uses the word “pupil” twenty times and must use “learner” instead. Which feature can help?",hint:"Look for a tool that locates and substitutes text.",steps:"Use Find and Replace to locate “pupil” and replace it with “learner”.",ans:"Find and Replace.",why:"It can make repeated text changes more efficiently, but replacements should be checked in context."},
{q:"You accidentally delete the wrong sentence. What should you try first?",hint:"Reverse the recent action.",steps:"Use Undo, then check that the sentence has returned.",ans:"Undo.",why:"Undo can reverse the accidental deletion if it remains in the action history."}
]);

add("computer", "office_word_processing", "character_formatting", "Character formatting", `<h3>What character formatting changes</h3>
<p>Character formatting changes the appearance of selected characters, words or phrases without changing their wording.</p>
<h3>Common character-formatting properties</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Font type:</b> the design of the letters.</li>
<li><b>Font size:</b> the size of the characters, commonly measured in points.</li>
<li><b>Bold:</b> makes text appear heavier.</li>
<li><b>Italic:</b> slants text.</li>
<li><b>Underline:</b> draws a line beneath text.</li>
<li><b>Font colour:</b> changes the colour of characters.</li>
<li><b>Highlight:</b> places a coloured background behind text.</li>
</ul>
<h3>How to format text</h3>
<ol>
<li>Select the required text.</li>
<li>Choose the formatting option from the toolbar or menu.</li>
<li>Check that the change affects only the intended text.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>In the heading “Computer Safety”, select the words and apply bold formatting. The words remain the same; only their appearance changes.</p>
<h3>Good practice</h3>
<p>Use a small, consistent set of fonts and styles. Excessive colours and typefaces can make a document harder to read.</p>`, [
{q:"What is character formatting?",hint:"It changes the appearance of letters and words.",steps:"Consider font, size, bold and colour.",ans:"Changing the appearance of selected characters or text.",why:"Character formatting affects presentation rather than the wording itself."},
{q:"Which formatting makes text slant?",hint:"It is commonly represented by an italic I.",steps:"Choose Italic after selecting the text.",ans:"Italic.",why:"Italic formatting slants the selected characters."},
{q:"How should you apply bold to one heading without affecting the whole document?",hint:"Selection controls the target.",steps:"Select only the heading, then choose Bold.",ans:"Select the heading before applying Bold.",why:"Formatting normally applies to the selected text."},
{q:"Does changing a word's font size change its spelling?",hint:"Appearance and content are different.",steps:"Compare the word's letters before and after resizing.",ans:"No.",why:"Font size changes appearance, not the letters in the word."},
{q:"Why should a formal report use consistent fonts and restrained colours?",hint:"Think about readability.",steps:"Consider how readers follow a document with a consistent style.",ans:"To improve readability and maintain a professional appearance.",why:"Too many competing styles distract readers from the content."}
]);

add("computer", "office_word_processing", "paragraph_formatting", "Paragraph formatting", `<h3>What paragraph formatting changes</h3>
<p>Paragraph formatting controls the arrangement and spacing of paragraphs. It usually applies to the paragraph containing the cursor or to selected paragraphs.</p>
<h3>Common paragraph settings</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Alignment:</b> left, centre, right or justified.</li>
<li><b>Line spacing:</b> the vertical space between lines.</li>
<li><b>Paragraph spacing:</b> space before or after paragraphs.</li>
<li><b>Indentation:</b> moves paragraph edges inward from the margins.</li>
<li><b>Bullets and numbering:</b> organise items into lists.</li>
</ul>
<h3>How to apply paragraph formatting</h3>
<ol>
<li>Place the cursor in the paragraph or select the required paragraphs.</li>
<li>Choose the alignment, spacing, indentation or list setting.</li>
<li>Review the layout for consistency.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A report title may be centred, while the body paragraphs are left-aligned. A procedure can use numbering to show the order of steps.</p>
<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Character formatting changes the appearance of text characters. Paragraph formatting changes how paragraphs are arranged.</p>`, [
{q:"Name two examples of paragraph formatting.",hint:"Think about alignment and spacing.",steps:"Identify settings that control a paragraph's arrangement.",ans:"Examples include alignment and line spacing.",why:"Both control paragraph layout rather than individual letter design."},
{q:"Which alignment places text evenly between the left and right margins?",hint:"Both edges of the paragraph line up.",steps:"Choose justified alignment.",ans:"Justified alignment.",why:"Justification adjusts spacing so text aligns with both margins, except where the final line is treated differently."},
{q:"You need to show the order of five instructions. Which list format is appropriate?",hint:"The steps have a sequence.",steps:"Use a numbered list.",ans:"A numbered list.",why:"Numbers make the sequence explicit."},
{q:"What does indentation do?",hint:"It changes a paragraph's position relative to the margins.",steps:"Consider moving the paragraph edge inward.",ans:"It moves a paragraph inward from the margin.",why:"Indentation changes paragraph placement."},
{q:"Explain the difference between line spacing and paragraph spacing.",hint:"One is between lines; the other separates paragraphs.",steps:"Identify the location of each space.",ans:"Line spacing is between lines within a paragraph; paragraph spacing is before or after paragraphs.",why:"They control different levels of vertical spacing."}
]);

add("computer", "office_word_processing", "page_layout", "Page layout", `<h3>Purpose of page layout</h3>
<p>Page layout controls how a document is arranged on printed pages or in page-based viewing. Good layout helps readers follow the content and supports printing.</p>
<h3>Common page-layout settings</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Margins:</b> blank space around the page content.</li>
<li><b>Orientation:</b> portrait (taller than it is wide) or landscape (wider than it is tall).</li>
<li><b>Paper size:</b> for example, A4.</li>
<li><b>Page breaks:</b> start content on a new page.</li>
<li><b>Columns:</b> arrange text in vertical sections.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Choosing settings</h3>
<p>Use portrait orientation for most letters and reports. Landscape can suit wide tables. Keep margins wide enough for comfortable reading and printing. Use a page break when a new section should begin on a new page instead of repeatedly pressing Enter.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A report with a wide results table may use landscape orientation on the relevant page if the word processor supports section-specific settings.</p>`, [
{q:"What are page margins?",hint:"They are the blank areas around the content.",steps:"Identify the space between content and page edges.",ans:"The blank spaces around the page content.",why:"Margins keep content away from page edges and provide room for printing and reading."},
{q:"Which orientation is wider than it is tall?",hint:"It is often useful for wide tables.",steps:"Compare portrait and landscape.",ans:"Landscape.",why:"Landscape pages have greater width than height."},
{q:"A table is too wide for a portrait page. What layout change might help?",hint:"The page needs more horizontal space.",steps:"Consider changing the page orientation to landscape.",ans:"Use landscape orientation.",why:"Landscape provides more horizontal room, although column widths may still need adjustment."},
{q:"Why use a page break rather than pressing Enter many times?",hint:"The next section should start on a new page reliably.",steps:"Insert a page break at the intended starting point.",ans:"A page break starts the next content on a new page more reliably.",why:"Repeated blank lines can shift when earlier content changes."},
{q:"What should you check before printing a document?",hint:"Check page arrangement and paper settings.",steps:"Review margins, orientation, paper size and page breaks.",ans:"Check the page layout and print preview.",why:"This helps prevent clipped content and unwanted blank pages."}
]);

add("computer", "office_word_processing", "tables", "Tables", `<h3>Why tables are used</h3>
<p>A table organises related information into rows and columns. It is useful when readers need to compare values or locate information quickly.</p>
<h3>Table structure</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Rows</b> run horizontally.</li>
<li><b>Columns</b> run vertically.</li>
<li><b>Cells</b> are the boxes formed where rows and columns meet.</li>
<li>A <b>header row</b> labels the information in the columns.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Creating and editing a table</h3>
<ol>
<li>Choose Insert Table.</li>
<li>Specify the number of rows and columns.</li>
<li>Enter information into the cells.</li>
<li>Add clear headings and adjust column widths as needed.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A class attendance table could have columns for Learner, Class and Present. Each learner's information occupies one row.</p>
<h3>Good practice</h3>
<p>Keep each column's information consistent. Avoid merging cells unnecessarily if it makes the table harder to read or sort.</p>`, [
{q:"What is a cell in a table?",hint:"It is formed where a row and a column meet.",steps:"Locate one box in the table grid.",ans:"The box at the intersection of a row and a column.",why:"Each cell holds a piece of table information."},
{q:"In a table, which direction do columns run?",hint:"Rows are horizontal.",steps:"Compare vertical columns with horizontal rows.",ans:"Vertically.",why:"Columns run from top to bottom."},
{q:"Why should a table have clear column headings?",hint:"Readers need to understand each column's values.",steps:"Consider how headings explain the data below them.",ans:"To identify the type of information stored in each column.",why:"Headings reduce ambiguity and make the table easier to interpret."},
{q:"A class list has 25 learners and one row per learner, plus a header row. How many rows are needed?",hint:"Include the heading row.",steps:"Add 25 learner rows and 1 header row.",ans:"26 rows.",why:"The header occupies an additional row."},
{q:"Why should dates not be mixed with telephone numbers in the same column?",hint:"Each column should hold a consistent kind of value.",steps:"Consider what the column heading promises and how readers interpret values.",ans:"Because the column would contain inconsistent types of information.",why:"Consistent columns are easier to understand, format and maintain."}
]);

add("computer", "office_word_processing", "images", "Images", `<h3>Using images in documents</h3>
<p>Images can explain ideas, illustrate a report or make a document easier to understand. Examples include photographs, diagrams, charts and screenshots.</p>
<h3>Inserting an image</h3>
<ol>
<li>Place the cursor where the image should appear.</li>
<li>Choose the picture or image insertion command.</li>
<li>Select the image file and insert it.</li>
<li>Resize and position it to suit the page.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Resizing and positioning</h3>
<p>Resize using the image's handles. When possible, preserve the aspect ratio so the image does not look stretched. Text-wrapping settings control whether text appears above, below or around an image.</p>
<h3>Useful practice</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use images that support the document's purpose.</li>
<li>Choose a size that keeps labels and details readable.</li>
<li>Add a caption when readers need context.</li>
<li>Use images you have permission to use and acknowledge sources when required.</li>
</ul>`, [
{q:"Give one reason to insert an image into a report.",hint:"Think about how a picture supports written information.",steps:"Consider illustration or explanation.",ans:"To illustrate or clarify information.",why:"A relevant image can help readers understand a topic."},
{q:"What can happen if an image is resized without preserving its proportions?",hint:"Its width and height may change independently.",steps:"Imagine stretching a circle horizontally.",ans:"The image may become stretched or distorted.",why:"Changing proportions alters the original shape."},
{q:"What is a caption used for?",hint:"It gives information about the image.",steps:"Describe how a short label helps a reader interpret a picture.",ans:"To identify or explain an image.",why:"A caption provides context for the image."},
{q:"Why should you check an image's position after inserting it?",hint:"It should not obscure important text.",steps:"Review the surrounding text and page layout.",ans:"To ensure the image fits the layout and does not obscure content.",why:"Positioning and text wrapping affect readability."},
{q:"You find a useful image online for a school report. What should you consider before using it?",hint:"Availability online does not automatically grant permission.",steps:"Check permission or licence requirements and whether attribution is needed.",ans:"Check whether you are allowed to use it and acknowledge the source when required.",why:"Online images can be protected by copyright or licence conditions."}
]);

add("computer", "office_word_processing", "headers_and_footers", "Headers and footers", `<h3>What headers and footers do</h3>
<p>A header appears in the top margin area of a page; a footer appears in the bottom margin area. They can repeat information across pages.</p>
<h3>Common contents</h3>
<ul>
<li>Document title or chapter name.</li>
<li>Author or organisation name.</li>
<li>Page numbers.</li>
<li>Date or other relevant document details.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Adding them</h3>
<ol>
<li>Open the Insert menu.</li>
<li>Choose Header or Footer.</li>
<li>Enter the required text or insert a page-number field.</li>
<li>Close header/footer editing and check several pages.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>A school report may display the report title in the header and page numbers in the footer.</p>
<h3>Important distinction</h3>
<p>A page number inserted as a field can update automatically when pages are added or removed. Typing the number manually may leave incorrect numbering after the document changes.</p>`, [
{q:"Where does a header normally appear?",hint:"It is at the top of the page.",steps:"Identify the page's top margin area.",ans:"At the top of the page.",why:"Headers occupy the upper margin area."},
{q:"Give two items that could appear in a footer.",hint:"Think of repeated page information.",steps:"Name common footer elements.",ans:"For example, a page number and the organisation name.",why:"Footers can contain repeated identifying information."},
{q:"Why is an automatic page-number field better than typing “1” on every page?",hint:"Page counts can change.",steps:"Consider what happens when pages are inserted.",ans:"The field can update numbering automatically.",why:"Manual numbers do not automatically change when page order or count changes."},
{q:"A report has ten pages. Where could its title be placed so it repeats at the top of pages?",hint:"Use the repeating area at the top.",steps:"Insert the title in the header.",ans:"In the header.",why:"A header can repeat across pages according to the document's settings."},
{q:"After adding a footer, what should you check?",hint:"Make sure it appears as intended throughout the document.",steps:"Review several pages and check that the footer does not overlap content.",ans:"Check its placement, repeated appearance and page numbering.",why:"The footer should be readable and consistent across the document."}
]);

add("computer", "office_word_processing", "spelling_and_grammar_tools", "Spelling and grammar tools", `<h3>Purpose of spelling and grammar tools</h3>
<p>Word processors can identify possible spelling errors and some grammatical or writing issues. They help writers review a document, but they do not guarantee that every sentence is correct.</p>
<h3>Using spell check</h3>
<ol>
<li>Run the spelling and grammar checker, or review the marked words.</li>
<li>Read the sentence containing each flagged item.</li>
<li>Choose a suitable correction, ignore a valid name or term, or add it to a personal dictionary if appropriate.</li>
<li>Review the document again.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Limitations</h3>
<p>A checker may miss a correctly spelled word used incorrectly. For example, “Their going to class” uses a correctly spelled word but the intended sentence requires “They're going to class.” The tool may also flag valid names, technical terms or local words.</p>
<h3>Best practice</h3>
<p>Use automated tools alongside careful reading. Check meaning, punctuation, names, figures and context before submitting or printing the document.</p>`, [
{q:"What is the purpose of a spelling checker?",hint:"It identifies possible spelling mistakes.",steps:"Run the checker and inspect flagged words.",ans:"To identify possible spelling errors and suggest corrections.",why:"It helps review writing but cannot guarantee correctness."},
{q:"Why should you not accept every suggested correction automatically?",hint:"Some flagged words may be correct in context.",steps:"Check the word's meaning and the sentence before accepting.",ans:"A suggestion may be wrong for the context, or a flagged word may be valid.",why:"Automated tools can misinterpret names, technical terms and intended meaning."},
{q:"In “Their going home,” which word is likely intended if the meaning is “they are going home”?",hint:"Use the contraction meaning “they are.”",steps:"Replace “Their” with the contraction for “they are.”",ans:"They're.",why:"“They're” means “they are”; “their” indicates possession."},
{q:"Can a grammar checker detect every error in a document?",hint:"Consider words that are spelled correctly but used wrongly.",steps:"Think of a sentence with a correctly spelled but inappropriate word.",ans:"No.",why:"Some contextual, factual or stylistic errors may not be detected."},
{q:"What should you do when the checker flags a person's name that is spelled correctly?",hint:"The checker may not recognise the name.",steps:"Verify the spelling, then keep it if correct.",ans:"Check the name and retain it if it is correct.",why:"Not every flagged item is an actual error."}
]);
